values
('arrays-hashing',
  'Hash Table',
  'Arrays & Hashing',
  array['The brute force is a nested loop that, for each element, searches the rest of the array for a partner, a copy or a match.', 'The question is about counts or membership: duplicates, anagrams, the most frequent items, whether a value exists.', 'Things that should be treated as the same need to be grouped: words with the same letters, values with the same remainder, cells in the same row.', 'Order doesn''t matter, or you only need an index back, not a sorted result.']::text[],
  'Walk the array once. For each element, ask the hash map a question about the elements before it, then add the current element to the map. The map is a memory of everything to the left of i, organised so that one lookup answers what a whole inner loop used to.

What you store depends on the question. "Is there a partner?" stores value → index. "How many?" stores value → count. "Which group?" stores key → list, where the key is something every member of the group shares, like a word''s sorted letters.

It''s fast because a hash lookup takes O(1) time on average, and each element is looked up and inserted once, so the pass is O(n). It''s correct because when you reach index i, the map holds exactly the elements at 0..i−1. If the answer pairs i with an earlier index, that earlier element is already in the map, so you can''t miss it.

The order matters. Look up first, insert second. If you insert first, an element can find itself: in Two Sum, 4 + 4 = 8 would match a single 4.

It breaks down when you need order: the smallest value above x, everything in a range, the k-th largest. A hash map keeps no order. Use sorting, binary search or a heap there. Hash lookups are O(1) on average, not guaranteed, but interviews treat them as constant.',
  'def two_sum(nums: list[int], target: int) -> list[int]:
    # value -> index, for everything left of i
    seen: dict[int, int] = {}
    for i, x in enumerate(nums):
        # 1. is the partner already stored?
        need = target - x
        if need in seen:
            return [seen[need], i]
        # 2. no: store this value for later numbers
        seen[x] = i
    return []
',
  'Time O(n) on average · Space O(n) for the map or set',
  array['Inserting before looking up, so an element pairs with itself (Two Sum with [3, 2, 4], target 6, must not return 3 + 3).', 'Storing value → index when values repeat, and overwriting the index you needed. Decide whether you want the first or the last index.', 'Reading a missing key: C++ map[key] silently inserts a zero, and Java''s map.get returns null. Use find, count or getOrDefault.', 'Comparing boxed Integer or Character values with == in Java. It works for small numbers and fails for large ones. Use equals or unbox first.', 'Using a key that isn''t unique to the group, like the set of letters instead of the sorted letters ("ab" and "aab" share a set).', 'Starting the inner walk from every element in Longest Consecutive Sequence, which quietly makes it O(n²).']::text[],
  array['contains-duplicate', 'valid-anagram', 'two-sum']::text[],
  1,
  $json${"version":1,"summary":"Trade memory for speed: keep what you've already seen in a hash map or set, so the question \"have I seen this, and where?\" costs O(1) instead of another scan. It turns most O(n²) pair and duplicate searches into one pass.","prerequisites":[],"walkthrough":{"problemSlug":"two-sum","input":"nums = [4, 6, 1, 9, 3, 5], target = 8","intro":"one step per index.","columns":["Map (value: index)","What happened","Answer"],"steps":[{"window":"{4: 0}","action":"4 needs 4: not stored yet; store 4","best":"none"},{"window":"{4: 0, 6: 1}","action":"6 needs 2: not stored; store 6","best":"none"},{"window":"{4: 0, 6: 1, 1: 2}","action":"1 needs 7: not stored; store 1","best":"none"},{"window":"{4: 0, 6: 1, 1: 2, 9: 3}","action":"9 needs −1: not stored; store 9","best":"none"},{"window":"{…, 9: 3, 3: 4}","action":"3 needs 5: not stored; store 3","best":"none"},{"window":"{…, 9: 3, 3: 4}","action":"5 needs 3: stored at index 4","best":"[4, 5]"}],"result":"[4, 5], since 3 + 5 = 8. At step 1 the 4 didn't match itself because the lookup ran before the insert."},"template":{"note":"Look up, then insert. Most problems in this family are this loop with a different thing stored and a different question asked.","code":{"python":"def two_sum(nums: list[int], target: int) -> list[int]:\n    # value -> index, for everything left of i\n    seen: dict[int, int] = {}\n    for i, x in enumerate(nums):\n        # 1. is the partner already stored?\n        need = target - x\n        if need in seen:\n            return [seen[need], i]\n        # 2. no: store this value for later numbers\n        seen[x] = i\n    return []\n","cpp":"vector<int> twoSum(const vector<int>& nums, int target) {\n    // value -> index, for everything left of i\n    unordered_map<int, int> seen;\n    for (int i = 0; i < (int)nums.size(); ++i) {\n        // 1. is the partner already stored?\n        int need = target - nums[i];\n        auto it = seen.find(need);\n        if (it != seen.end()) return {it->second, i};\n        // 2. no: store this value for later numbers\n        seen[nums[i]] = i;\n    }\n    return {};\n}\n","java":"class Solution {\n    public int[] twoSum(int[] nums, int target) {\n        // value -> index, for everything left of i\n        Map<Integer, Integer> seen = new HashMap<>();\n        for (int i = 0; i < nums.length; i++) {\n            // 1. is the partner already stored?\n            int need = target - nums[i];\n            if (seen.containsKey(need)) {\n                return new int[]{seen.get(need), i};\n            }\n            // 2. no: store this value for later numbers\n            seen.put(nums[i], i);\n        }\n        return new int[0];\n    }\n}\n"}},"variants":[{"name":"Count, then compare","when":"Two collections must contain the same things the same number of times.","idea":"Keep one count map. Add 1 for each letter of s and subtract 1 for each letter of t. They match only if every count ends at zero.","code":{"python":"def is_anagram(s: str, t: str) -> bool:\n    if len(s) != len(t):\n        return False\n    # letter -> (times in s) - (times in t)\n    count: dict[str, int] = {}\n    for a, b in zip(s, t):\n        count[a] = count.get(a, 0) + 1\n        count[b] = count.get(b, 0) - 1\n    # an anagram leaves every count at zero\n    return all(c == 0 for c in count.values())\n","cpp":"bool isAnagram(const string& s, const string& t) {\n    if (s.size() != t.size()) return false;\n    // letter -> (times in s) - (times in t)\n    unordered_map<char, int> count;\n    for (size_t i = 0; i < s.size(); ++i) {\n        ++count[s[i]];\n        --count[t[i]];\n    }\n    // an anagram leaves every count at zero\n    for (const auto& entry : count) {\n        if (entry.second != 0) return false;\n    }\n    return true;\n}\n","java":"class Solution {\n    public boolean isAnagram(String s, String t) {\n        if (s.length() != t.length()) return false;\n        // letter -> (times in s) - (times in t)\n        Map<Character, Integer> count = new HashMap<>();\n        for (int i = 0; i < s.length(); i++) {\n            count.merge(s.charAt(i), 1, Integer::sum);\n            count.merge(t.charAt(i), -1, Integer::sum);\n        }\n        // an anagram leaves every count at zero\n        for (int c : count.values()) {\n            if (c != 0) return false;\n        }\n        return true;\n    }\n}\n"},"problemSlug":"valid-anagram"},{"name":"Group by a shared key","when":"Items that are equivalent in some way have to end up together.","idea":"Compute a key that every member of a group shares and no outsider does, here the sorted letters. Map key → list and append each item to its list.","code":{"python":"def group_anagrams(strs: list[str]) -> list[list[str]]:\n    # sorted letters -> every word with those letters\n    groups: dict[str, list[str]] = {}\n    for w in strs:\n        key = \"\".join(sorted(w))\n        groups.setdefault(key, []).append(w)\n    return list(groups.values())\n","cpp":"vector<vector<string>> groupAnagrams(vector<string> strs) {\n    // sorted letters -> every word with those letters\n    unordered_map<string, vector<string>> groups;\n    for (const string& w : strs) {\n        string key = w;\n        sort(key.begin(), key.end());\n        groups[key].push_back(w);\n    }\n    vector<vector<string>> out;\n    for (auto& entry : groups) out.push_back(entry.second);\n    return out;\n}\n","java":"class Solution {\n    public List<List<String>> groupAnagrams(String[] strs) {\n        // sorted letters -> every word with those letters\n        Map<String, List<String>> groups = new HashMap<>();\n        for (String w : strs) {\n            char[] letters = w.toCharArray();\n            Arrays.sort(letters);\n            String key = new String(letters);\n            groups.computeIfAbsent(key, k -> new ArrayList<>())\n                  .add(w);\n        }\n        return new ArrayList<>(groups.values());\n    }\n}\n"},"problemSlug":"group-anagrams"},{"name":"Set for membership","when":"You only need to know whether a value exists, for example to walk runs of consecutive numbers.","idea":"Put everything in a set. Only start counting a run at x when x − 1 is missing, so each run is walked once from its first number and the whole thing stays O(n).","code":{"python":"def longest_consecutive(nums: list[int]) -> int:\n    present = set(nums)\n    best = 0\n    for x in present:\n        # only count from the first number of a run\n        if x - 1 in present:\n            continue\n        length = 1\n        while x + length in present:\n            length += 1\n        best = max(best, length)\n    return best\n","cpp":"int longestConsecutive(const vector<int>& nums) {\n    unordered_set<int> present(nums.begin(), nums.end());\n    int best = 0;\n    for (int x : present) {\n        // only count from the first number of a run\n        if (present.count(x - 1)) continue;\n        int length = 1;\n        while (present.count(x + length)) ++length;\n        best = max(best, length);\n    }\n    return best;\n}\n","java":"class Solution {\n    public int longestConsecutive(int[] nums) {\n        Set<Integer> present = new HashSet<>();\n        for (int x : nums) present.add(x);\n        int best = 0;\n        for (int x : present) {\n            // only count from the first number of a run\n            if (present.contains(x - 1)) continue;\n            int length = 1;\n            while (present.contains(x + length)) length++;\n            best = Math.max(best, length);\n        }\n        return best;\n    }\n}\n"},"problemSlug":"longest-consecutive-sequence"}],"complexity":{"time":"O(n) on average","space":"O(n) for the map or set","why":"Each element is looked up once and inserted once, and each of those takes O(1) on average. In Longest Consecutive Sequence the inner while loop only runs from the start of a run, so across the whole scan it adds up to n steps."},"notThis":["The array is sorted and you want a pair: two pointers from both ends finds it in O(1) extra space.","You need the smallest, largest or k-th value, or anything about order: sort, use a heap, or binary search.","The question is about a contiguous subarray's sum: prefix sums, often with a hash map of earlier prefixes, not a plain value map."],"ladder":[{"slug":"contains-duplicate","tier":"warm-up","note":"A set and one question: have I seen this before?"},{"slug":"valid-anagram","tier":"warm-up","note":"A count map instead of a set."},{"slug":"two-sum","tier":"warm-up","note":"Value → index: look up the partner, then insert."},{"slug":"isomorphic-strings","tier":"core","note":"Two maps that must agree both ways: s→t and t→s."},{"slug":"group-anagrams","tier":"core","note":"The key is computed: sorted letters, or a 26-count tuple."},{"slug":"top-k-frequent-elements","tier":"core","note":"Count first, then bucket values by their count to avoid sorting."},{"slug":"valid-sudoku","tier":"core","note":"One set per row, column and box; the box index is a key built from two numbers."},{"slug":"longest-consecutive-sequence","tier":"core","note":"A set, plus the rule that only the start of a run gets walked."},{"slug":"find-all-duplicates-in-an-array","tier":"core","note":"Values are 1..n, so the array itself can be the hash table: mark index v − 1."},{"slug":"insert-delete-getrandom-o1","tier":"core","note":"A map of value → position in an array, and swap-with-last to delete in O(1)."},{"slug":"first-missing-positive","tier":"stretch","note":"Index-as-hash with O(1) extra space: put each value v at index v − 1."},{"slug":"max-points-on-a-line","tier":"stretch","note":"The key is a slope, reduced by gcd so that equal slopes hash the same."}]}$json$::jsonb),
('backtracking',
  'Backtracking',
  'Backtracking',
  array['The question says all, every or list: all subsets, all permutations, every way to split a string.', 'Inputs are small (n up to about 10 to 20), because the number of answers itself is 2^n or n!.', 'An answer is built from a sequence of choices, and some partial answers can already be ruled out.', 'It''s a puzzle with rules to place against: N-Queens, Sudoku, a word in a grid.']::text[],
  'Picture a tree of decisions. The root is the empty answer. Each edge adds one choice, and each leaf is a finished answer. Backtracking walks this tree depth first and keeps only one path in memory. At each node it loops over the choices left: choose one (add it to the path), recurse, then un-choose it (remove it from the path) before trying the next.

The invariant: when backtrack() returns, the path is exactly what it was when it was called. That''s why one shared list is enough, and why every choose needs a matching un-choose. When you record an answer, record a copy. The path keeps changing after you save it.

Three knobs cover most problems. A start index means you only pick later elements, so {1, 2} and {2, 1} can''t both appear. That gives subsets and combinations. A used[] array lets any unused element go next, which gives permutations. Recursing with i instead of i + 1 lets an element be picked again.

The work is the size of the tree: about 2^n nodes for subsets and n · n! for permutations, times the cost of copying each answer. You can''t beat the number of answers. You can stop early with pruning: after sorting, if one candidate is already too big, every later one is too, so break. For duplicates in the input, sort and skip nums[i] when it equals nums[i − 1] at the same depth, because that branch would build the same subtree again.

It breaks down when the question only wants a count or the best value and the same subproblem comes up many times. Then use dynamic programming.',
  'def subsets(nums: list[int]) -> list[list[int]]:
    out: list[list[int]] = []
    path: list[int] = []

    def backtrack(start: int) -> None:
        # every node of the tree is a subset: record a copy
        out.append(path[:])
        for i in range(start, len(nums)):
            # choose nums[i]
            path.append(nums[i])
            # only later indexes, so each set appears once
            backtrack(i + 1)
            # un-choose: the next i starts from the same path
            path.pop()

    backtrack(0)
    return out
',
  'Time O(n · 2^n) for subsets; O(n · n!) for permutations · Space O(n) for the path and the recursion, plus the output',
  array['Appending the path itself instead of a copy. Every saved answer then points to the same list, which ends up empty.', 'Forgetting the un-choose step, or undoing only half of it (the path but not used[i]).', 'Using i instead of i + 1 (or the reverse). One allows reuse and the other doesn''t; read the problem.', 'Starting the loop from 0 for subsets or combinations. You get [1, 2] and [2, 1] as separate answers.', 'Skipping duplicates without sorting first. Equal values that aren''t next to each other are never caught.', 'Writing the duplicate skip as i > 0 instead of i > start. That also blocks [2, 2], which is a valid subset.', 'Breaking on a too-big candidate when the array isn''t sorted. Later, smaller candidates get skipped.']::text[],
  array['subsets', 'combinations', 'letter-combinations-of-a-phone-number']::text[],
  20,
  $json${"version":1,"summary":"For questions that want every arrangement: all subsets, permutations, combinations, or every board that follows the rules. Build an answer one choice at a time, recurse, then undo the choice and try the next one.","prerequisites":["tree-dfs"],"walkthrough":{"problemSlug":"subsets","input":"nums = [1, 2, 3]","intro":"one step per subset recorded. Each call records its path first, then tries every later index.","columns":["Path","Choices made","Subsets recorded"],"steps":[{"window":"[]","action":"backtrack(0) records the empty path","best":"1"},{"window":"[1]","action":"choose 1, call backtrack(1), record","best":"2"},{"window":"[1, 2]","action":"choose 2, call backtrack(2), record","best":"3"},{"window":"[1, 2, 3]","action":"choose 3, call backtrack(3), record. No index left, so return","best":"4"},{"window":"[1, 3]","action":"un-choose 3, then 2. Back in backtrack(1), choose 3 and record","best":"5"},{"window":"[2]","action":"un-choose 3, then 1. Back in backtrack(0), choose 2 and record","best":"6"},{"window":"[2, 3]","action":"choose 3, call backtrack(3), record","best":"7"},{"window":"[3]","action":"un-choose 3, then 2. Back in backtrack(0), choose 3 and record","best":"8"}],"result":"8 subsets: [], [1], [1,2], [1,2,3], [1,3], [2], [2,3], [3]. Each set appears once because a path only ever adds a later index."},"template":{"note":"Record, then for each choice: choose, recurse, un-choose. Where you record (every node or only full paths) and where the loop starts (start, 0 or i) are what change between problems.","code":{"python":"def subsets(nums: list[int]) -> list[list[int]]:\n    out: list[list[int]] = []\n    path: list[int] = []\n\n    def backtrack(start: int) -> None:\n        # every node of the tree is a subset: record a copy\n        out.append(path[:])\n        for i in range(start, len(nums)):\n            # choose nums[i]\n            path.append(nums[i])\n            # only later indexes, so each set appears once\n            backtrack(i + 1)\n            # un-choose: the next i starts from the same path\n            path.pop()\n\n    backtrack(0)\n    return out\n","cpp":"void backtrack(const vector<int>& nums, int start,\n               vector<int>& path, vector<vector<int>>& out) {\n    // every node of the tree is a subset: record a copy\n    out.push_back(path);\n    for (int i = start; i < (int)nums.size(); i++) {\n        // choose nums[i]\n        path.push_back(nums[i]);\n        // only later indexes, so each set appears once\n        backtrack(nums, i + 1, path, out);\n        // un-choose: the next i starts from the same path\n        path.pop_back();\n    }\n}\n\nvector<vector<int>> subsets(const vector<int>& nums) {\n    vector<vector<int>> out;\n    vector<int> path;\n    backtrack(nums, 0, path, out);\n    return out;\n}\n","java":"class Solution {\n    public List<List<Integer>> subsets(int[] nums) {\n        List<List<Integer>> out = new ArrayList<>();\n        backtrack(nums, 0, new ArrayList<>(), out);\n        return out;\n    }\n\n    private void backtrack(int[] nums, int start,\n                           List<Integer> path,\n                           List<List<Integer>> out) {\n        // every node of the tree is a subset: record a copy\n        out.add(new ArrayList<>(path));\n        for (int i = start; i < nums.length; i++) {\n            // choose nums[i]\n            path.add(nums[i]);\n            // only later indexes, so each set appears once\n            backtrack(nums, i + 1, path, out);\n            // un-choose: the next i starts from the same path\n            path.remove(path.size() - 1);\n        }\n    }\n}\n"}},"variants":[{"name":"Permutations: used[] instead of start","when":"Order matters, so [1, 2] and [2, 1] are different answers.","idea":"Loop over every index from 0 and skip the ones marked used. Record only when the path has n elements. Un-choose both the path and the used mark.","code":{"python":"def permute(nums: list[int]) -> list[list[int]]:\n    out: list[list[int]] = []\n    path: list[int] = []\n    used = [False] * len(nums)\n\n    def backtrack() -> None:\n        # a full-length path is one permutation\n        if len(path) == len(nums):\n            out.append(path[:])\n            return\n        # any unused element can go next, so start from 0\n        for i in range(len(nums)):\n            if used[i]:\n                continue\n            used[i] = True\n            path.append(nums[i])\n            backtrack()\n            # undo both changes before trying the next i\n            path.pop()\n            used[i] = False\n\n    backtrack()\n    return out\n","cpp":"void backtrack(const vector<int>& nums, vector<bool>& used,\n               vector<int>& path, vector<vector<int>>& out) {\n    // a full-length path is one permutation\n    if (path.size() == nums.size()) {\n        out.push_back(path);\n        return;\n    }\n    // any unused element can go next, so start from 0\n    for (int i = 0; i < (int)nums.size(); i++) {\n        if (used[i]) continue;\n        used[i] = true;\n        path.push_back(nums[i]);\n        backtrack(nums, used, path, out);\n        // undo both changes before trying the next i\n        path.pop_back();\n        used[i] = false;\n    }\n}\n\nvector<vector<int>> permute(const vector<int>& nums) {\n    vector<vector<int>> out;\n    vector<int> path;\n    vector<bool> used(nums.size(), false);\n    backtrack(nums, used, path, out);\n    return out;\n}\n","java":"class Solution {\n    public List<List<Integer>> permute(int[] nums) {\n        List<List<Integer>> out = new ArrayList<>();\n        boolean[] used = new boolean[nums.length];\n        backtrack(nums, used, new ArrayList<>(), out);\n        return out;\n    }\n\n    private void backtrack(int[] nums, boolean[] used,\n                           List<Integer> path,\n                           List<List<Integer>> out) {\n        // a full-length path is one permutation\n        if (path.size() == nums.length) {\n            out.add(new ArrayList<>(path));\n            return;\n        }\n        // any unused element can go next, so start from 0\n        for (int i = 0; i < nums.length; i++) {\n            if (used[i]) continue;\n            used[i] = true;\n            path.add(nums[i]);\n            backtrack(nums, used, path, out);\n            // undo both changes before trying the next i\n            path.remove(path.size() - 1);\n            used[i] = false;\n        }\n    }\n}\n"},"problemSlug":"permutations"},{"name":"Reuse allowed, with pruning","when":"Each number can be picked any number of times to reach a target.","idea":"Recurse with i, not i + 1, so the same number stays available. Sort first; then once nums[i] is bigger than what's left, break, because every later number is bigger too.","code":{"python":"def combination_sum(\n    candidates: list[int], target: int\n) -> list[list[int]]:\n    # sorted, so one number too big means the rest are too\n    nums = sorted(candidates)\n    out: list[list[int]] = []\n    path: list[int] = []\n\n    def backtrack(start: int, remain: int) -> None:\n        if remain == 0:\n            out.append(path[:])\n            return\n        for i in range(start, len(nums)):\n            # prune: this branch and every later one overshoot\n            if nums[i] > remain:\n                break\n            path.append(nums[i])\n            # i, not i + 1: the same number can be used again\n            backtrack(i, remain - nums[i])\n            path.pop()\n\n    backtrack(0, target)\n    return out\n","cpp":"void backtrack(const vector<int>& nums, int start, int remain,\n               vector<int>& path, vector<vector<int>>& out) {\n    if (remain == 0) {\n        out.push_back(path);\n        return;\n    }\n    for (int i = start; i < (int)nums.size(); i++) {\n        // prune: this branch and every later one overshoot\n        if (nums[i] > remain) break;\n        path.push_back(nums[i]);\n        // i, not i + 1: the same number can be used again\n        backtrack(nums, i, remain - nums[i], path, out);\n        path.pop_back();\n    }\n}\n\nvector<vector<int>> combinationSum(vector<int> candidates,\n                                   int target) {\n    // sorted, so one number too big means the rest are too\n    sort(candidates.begin(), candidates.end());\n    vector<vector<int>> out;\n    vector<int> path;\n    backtrack(candidates, 0, target, path, out);\n    return out;\n}\n","java":"class Solution {\n    public List<List<Integer>> combinationSum(int[] candidates,\n                                              int target) {\n        // sorted, so one number too big means the rest are too\n        int[] nums = candidates.clone();\n        Arrays.sort(nums);\n        List<List<Integer>> out = new ArrayList<>();\n        backtrack(nums, 0, target, new ArrayList<>(), out);\n        return out;\n    }\n\n    private void backtrack(int[] nums, int start, int remain,\n                           List<Integer> path,\n                           List<List<Integer>> out) {\n        if (remain == 0) {\n            out.add(new ArrayList<>(path));\n            return;\n        }\n        for (int i = start; i < nums.length; i++) {\n            // prune: this branch and every later one overshoot\n            if (nums[i] > remain) break;\n            path.add(nums[i]);\n            // i, not i + 1: the same number can be used again\n            backtrack(nums, i, remain - nums[i], path, out);\n            path.remove(path.size() - 1);\n        }\n    }\n}\n"},"problemSlug":"combination-sum"},{"name":"Duplicates in the input","when":"The input has repeated values, and the answer must not list the same subset twice.","idea":"Sort so equal values sit together. In the loop, skip nums[i] when i > start and nums[i] == nums[i − 1]: picking the second 2 at the same depth would rebuild the subtree the first 2 already built.","code":{"python":"def subsets_with_dup(nums: list[int]) -> list[list[int]]:\n    # sorting puts equal values next to each other\n    nums = sorted(nums)\n    out: list[list[int]] = []\n    path: list[int] = []\n\n    def backtrack(start: int) -> None:\n        out.append(path[:])\n        for i in range(start, len(nums)):\n            # same value as the last choice at this depth:\n            # its whole subtree was already built, so skip it\n            if i > start and nums[i] == nums[i - 1]:\n                continue\n            path.append(nums[i])\n            backtrack(i + 1)\n            path.pop()\n\n    backtrack(0)\n    return out\n","cpp":"void backtrack(const vector<int>& nums, int start,\n               vector<int>& path, vector<vector<int>>& out) {\n    out.push_back(path);\n    for (int i = start; i < (int)nums.size(); i++) {\n        // same value as the last choice at this depth:\n        // its whole subtree was already built, so skip it\n        if (i > start && nums[i] == nums[i - 1]) continue;\n        path.push_back(nums[i]);\n        backtrack(nums, i + 1, path, out);\n        path.pop_back();\n    }\n}\n\nvector<vector<int>> subsetsWithDup(vector<int> nums) {\n    // sorting puts equal values next to each other\n    sort(nums.begin(), nums.end());\n    vector<vector<int>> out;\n    vector<int> path;\n    backtrack(nums, 0, path, out);\n    return out;\n}\n","java":"class Solution {\n    public List<List<Integer>> subsetsWithDup(int[] input) {\n        // sorting puts equal values next to each other\n        int[] nums = input.clone();\n        Arrays.sort(nums);\n        List<List<Integer>> out = new ArrayList<>();\n        backtrack(nums, 0, new ArrayList<>(), out);\n        return out;\n    }\n\n    private void backtrack(int[] nums, int start,\n                           List<Integer> path,\n                           List<List<Integer>> out) {\n        out.add(new ArrayList<>(path));\n        for (int i = start; i < nums.length; i++) {\n            // same value as the last choice at this depth:\n            // its whole subtree was already built, so skip it\n            if (i > start && nums[i] == nums[i - 1]) continue;\n            path.add(nums[i]);\n            backtrack(nums, i + 1, path, out);\n            path.remove(path.size() - 1);\n        }\n    }\n}\n"},"problemSlug":"subsets-ii"}],"complexity":{"time":"O(n · 2^n) for subsets; O(n · n!) for permutations","space":"O(n) for the path and the recursion, plus the output","why":"There are 2^n subsets and n! permutations, and copying each one into the output costs up to n. The recursion is never deeper than n, and only one path exists at a time. Pruning cuts the tree in practice but not in the worst case."},"notThis":["The question asks how many ways, or the best total, not the list of answers: dynamic programming counts them without listing each one.","n is large, like 10^5. No list of all answers fits, so look for greedy, two pointers or DP.","You only need to visit each cell or node once to find what's connected: plain DFS or BFS, with no un-choose. See Graph BFS / DFS.","Subsets of up to about 20 items where you only check each one: a loop over bitmasks from 0 to 2^n − 1 is shorter. See Bit Manipulation."],"ladder":[{"slug":"subsets","tier":"warm-up","note":"The template: record at every node, loop from start."},{"slug":"combinations","tier":"warm-up","note":"Subsets of size k: record only when the path is full, and stop when too few numbers are left."},{"slug":"letter-combinations-of-a-phone-number","tier":"warm-up","note":"One digit per depth, one letter per branch. Choices come from a lookup table."},{"slug":"permutations","tier":"core","note":"A used[] array instead of a start index."},{"slug":"subsets-ii","tier":"core","note":"Duplicates: sort, then skip equal values at the same depth."},{"slug":"combination-sum","tier":"core","note":"Reuse with i, and prune with break once the sorted candidates are too big."},{"slug":"combination-sum-ii","tier":"core","note":"No reuse and duplicate inputs: i + 1 together with the same-depth skip."},{"slug":"permutations-ii","tier":"core","note":"Duplicates in permutations: skip nums[i] if it equals nums[i − 1] and that one isn't in use."},{"slug":"generate-parentheses","tier":"core","note":"Prune by rule: never more ')' than '(' so far, never more than n of either."},{"slug":"palindrome-partitioning","tier":"core","note":"Choose where the next piece ends, and only recurse when that piece is a palindrome."},{"slug":"word-search","tier":"core","note":"Backtracking on a grid: mark the cell as used, explore four ways, unmark it."},{"slug":"n-queens","tier":"stretch","note":"One queen per row, with sets for columns and both diagonals to prune in O(1)."},{"slug":"sudoku-solver","tier":"stretch","note":"Fill one empty cell at a time, and return as soon as one full board works."},{"slug":"word-search-ii","tier":"stretch","note":"Many words at once: walk a trie alongside the grid so dead prefixes stop early."}]}$json$::jsonb),
('binary-search',
  'Binary Search',
  'Binary Search',
  array['The input is sorted, or sorted and then rotated, and you need a position: where x is, where it would go, its first or last copy.', 'The question asks for the minimum or maximum value that still works, and checking one guess is easy ("can she finish at speed k?").', 'Making the guess bigger only ever makes the check easier, or only ever harder. That one-way change is what lets you throw half away.', 'The limits are huge (values up to 10⁹) or the expected time is O(log n) or O(n log m).']::text[],
  'Think of a yes/no question about each index (or each candidate answer) that is no, no, ..., no, yes, yes, ..., yes. For search insert position it''s "is nums[i] >= target?". Binary search finds the first yes.

Keep a range lo..hi that is known to contain the first yes, where hi can also mean "no yes at all". Look at mid. If mid is yes, the first yes is mid or to its left, so hi = mid. If mid is no, then mid and everything before it are no, so lo = mid + 1. The loop runs while lo < hi and stops with lo == hi on the answer.

That''s correct because each step keeps the answer inside lo..hi. It terminates because mid is always below hi, so either hi drops to mid or lo rises past mid: the range shrinks every time. It''s fast because the range halves, so a million items take about 20 steps.

Binary search on the answer is the same loop over values instead of indices. For Koko, speeds 1..max(piles) give no, no, ..., yes, yes as speed grows, and each check is a pass over the piles. Total cost is O(n log max).

It breaks when the question isn''t one-way. If yes can turn back into no further on, halving throws away answers. Mixed-up bounds break it too: decide once whether hi is included, and write every line to match.',
  'def search_insert(nums: list[int], target: int) -> int:
    # the answer is in lo..hi; hi itself means "at the end"
    lo, hi = 0, len(nums)
    while lo < hi:
        mid = lo + (hi - lo) // 2
        if nums[mid] >= target:
            # mid works; the first one is mid or left of it
            hi = mid
        else:
            # mid and everything left of it are too small
            lo = mid + 1
    # lo == hi: the first index with nums[i] >= target
    return lo
',
  'Time O(log n); O(n log m) on the answer, where m is the size of the value range · Space O(1)',
  array['Mixing range styles, like hi = n − 1 with while lo < hi and hi = mid − 1. Choose one meaning for hi and keep it.', 'lo = mid instead of lo = mid + 1. When hi = lo + 1, mid equals lo and the loop never ends.', 'Computing mid as (lo + hi) / 2 in C++ or Java, which can overflow when both are large. Write lo + (hi − lo) / 2.', 'Returning on the first match when the problem wants the first or last copy. Keep narrowing instead.', 'On the answer, starting hi too low so the true answer is outside the range, or counting the check in 32 bits.', 'Using binary search when the condition isn''t one-way, like a peak search that reads a single side without comparing neighbours.']::text[],
  array['binary-search', 'search-insert-position', 'sqrtx']::text[],
  5,
  $json${"version":1,"summary":"When a yes/no question flips from no to yes exactly once as you move along a sorted range, find the flip point by halving the range each step. It searches sorted arrays in O(log n), and it also searches over possible answers, like the slowest speed that still finishes in time.","prerequisites":["two-pointers"],"walkthrough":{"problemSlug":"search-insert-position","input":"nums = [1, 3, 4, 6, 8, 9, 11, 13, 15, 18], target = 12","intro":"one step per check of mid, looking for the first nums[i] >= 12.","columns":["lo, hi","Check","Answer is in"],"steps":[{"window":"lo = 0, hi = 10","action":"start: any index, or 10 for \"at the end\"","best":"0..10"},{"window":"lo = 6, hi = 10","action":"mid = 5: 9 < 12, so lo = 6","best":"6..10"},{"window":"lo = 6, hi = 8","action":"mid = 8: 15 >= 12, so hi = 8","best":"6..8"},{"window":"lo = 6, hi = 7","action":"mid = 7: 13 >= 12, so hi = 7","best":"6..7"},{"window":"lo = 7, hi = 7","action":"mid = 6: 11 < 12, so lo = 7; lo == hi, stop","best":"7"}],"result":"7: 12 goes just before 13. Four checks for ten elements."},"template":{"note":"Find the first index where a one-way condition is true. hi = mid on yes, lo = mid + 1 on no, stop when lo == hi. Swap the condition and nearly every problem on the ladder uses this same loop.","code":{"python":"def search_insert(nums: list[int], target: int) -> int:\n    # the answer is in lo..hi; hi itself means \"at the end\"\n    lo, hi = 0, len(nums)\n    while lo < hi:\n        mid = lo + (hi - lo) // 2\n        if nums[mid] >= target:\n            # mid works; the first one is mid or left of it\n            hi = mid\n        else:\n            # mid and everything left of it are too small\n            lo = mid + 1\n    # lo == hi: the first index with nums[i] >= target\n    return lo\n","cpp":"int searchInsert(const vector<int>& nums, int target) {\n    // the answer is in lo..hi; hi itself means \"at the end\"\n    int lo = 0, hi = nums.size();\n    while (lo < hi) {\n        int mid = lo + (hi - lo) / 2;\n        if (nums[mid] >= target) {\n            // mid works; the first one is mid or left of it\n            hi = mid;\n        } else {\n            // mid and everything left of it are too small\n            lo = mid + 1;\n        }\n    }\n    // lo == hi: the first index with nums[i] >= target\n    return lo;\n}\n","java":"class Solution {\n    public int searchInsert(int[] nums, int target) {\n        // the answer is in lo..hi; hi itself means \"at the end\"\n        int lo = 0, hi = nums.length;\n        while (lo < hi) {\n            int mid = lo + (hi - lo) / 2;\n            if (nums[mid] >= target) {\n                // mid works; the first one is mid or left of it\n                hi = mid;\n            } else {\n                // mid and everything left of it are too small\n                lo = mid + 1;\n            }\n        }\n        // lo == hi: the first index with nums[i] >= target\n        return lo;\n    }\n}\n"}},"variants":[{"name":"Binary search on the answer","when":"You want the smallest (or largest) value that passes a check, and passing is one-way.","idea":"Search values, not indices: lo = 1, hi = max(piles). The yes/no question is \"does speed k finish within h hours?\" Count the hours in 64 bits, since slow speeds over big piles pass 2³¹.","code":{"python":"def hours_needed(piles: list[int], k: int) -> int:\n    # each pile takes ceil(pile / k) hours\n    return sum((p + k - 1) // k for p in piles)\n\ndef min_eating_speed(piles: list[int], h: int) -> int:\n    # \"speed k is fast enough\" goes false ... false,\n    # true ... true as k grows: find the first true\n    lo, hi = 1, max(piles)\n    while lo < hi:\n        mid = lo + (hi - lo) // 2\n        if hours_needed(piles, mid) <= h:\n            hi = mid\n        else:\n            lo = mid + 1\n    return lo\n","cpp":"long long hoursNeeded(const vector<int>& piles, int k) {\n    // each pile takes ceil(pile / k) hours\n    long long hours = 0;\n    for (int p : piles) hours += (p + (long long)k - 1) / k;\n    return hours;\n}\n\nint minEatingSpeed(const vector<int>& piles, int h) {\n    // \"speed k is fast enough\" goes false ... false,\n    // true ... true as k grows: find the first true\n    int lo = 1, hi = *max_element(piles.begin(), piles.end());\n    while (lo < hi) {\n        int mid = lo + (hi - lo) / 2;\n        if (hoursNeeded(piles, mid) <= h) {\n            hi = mid;\n        } else {\n            lo = mid + 1;\n        }\n    }\n    return lo;\n}\n","java":"class Solution {\n    long hoursNeeded(int[] piles, int k) {\n        // each pile takes ceil(pile / k) hours\n        long hours = 0;\n        for (int p : piles) hours += (p + (long) k - 1) / k;\n        return hours;\n    }\n\n    public int minEatingSpeed(int[] piles, int h) {\n        // \"speed k is fast enough\" goes false ... false,\n        // true ... true as k grows: find the first true\n        int lo = 1, hi = Arrays.stream(piles).max().getAsInt();\n        while (lo < hi) {\n            int mid = lo + (hi - lo) / 2;\n            if (hoursNeeded(piles, mid) <= h) {\n                hi = mid;\n            } else {\n                lo = mid + 1;\n            }\n        }\n        return lo;\n    }\n}\n"},"problemSlug":"koko-eating-bananas"},{"name":"Rotated sorted array","when":"A sorted array was rotated, and you need the minimum (or a target).","idea":"Values <= the last element are exactly the rotated-in right part, so \"nums[i] <= last\" is no, ..., yes. The first yes is the minimum. Same loop, different question.","code":{"python":"def find_min(nums: list[int]) -> int:\n    # values <= the last one form the right, rotated part;\n    # the minimum is the first of them\n    last = nums[-1]\n    lo, hi = 0, len(nums) - 1\n    while lo < hi:\n        mid = lo + (hi - lo) // 2\n        if nums[mid] <= last:\n            hi = mid\n        else:\n            lo = mid + 1\n    return nums[lo]\n","cpp":"int findMin(const vector<int>& nums) {\n    // values <= the last one form the right, rotated part;\n    // the minimum is the first of them\n    int last = nums.back();\n    int lo = 0, hi = (int)nums.size() - 1;\n    while (lo < hi) {\n        int mid = lo + (hi - lo) / 2;\n        if (nums[mid] <= last) {\n            hi = mid;\n        } else {\n            lo = mid + 1;\n        }\n    }\n    return nums[lo];\n}\n","java":"class Solution {\n    public int findMin(int[] nums) {\n        // values <= the last one form the right, rotated part;\n        // the minimum is the first of them\n        int last = nums[nums.length - 1];\n        int lo = 0, hi = nums.length - 1;\n        while (lo < hi) {\n            int mid = lo + (hi - lo) / 2;\n            if (nums[mid] <= last) {\n                hi = mid;\n            } else {\n                lo = mid + 1;\n            }\n        }\n        return nums[lo];\n    }\n}\n"},"problemSlug":"find-minimum-in-rotated-sorted-array"},{"name":"First and last position","when":"Values repeat and you need the whole block of one value.","idea":"The first copy is the first index with nums[i] >= target. The last copy sits one before the first index with nums[i] >= target + 1. Two runs of the template, no special cases.","code":{"python":"def first_at_least(nums: list[int], x: int) -> int:\n    # first index with nums[i] >= x, or len(nums) if none\n    lo, hi = 0, len(nums)\n    while lo < hi:\n        mid = lo + (hi - lo) // 2\n        if nums[mid] >= x:\n            hi = mid\n        else:\n            lo = mid + 1\n    return lo\n\ndef search_range(nums: list[int], target: int) -> list[int]:\n    start = first_at_least(nums, target)\n    if start == len(nums) or nums[start] != target:\n        return [-1, -1]\n    # the last target is just before the first bigger value\n    end = first_at_least(nums, target + 1) - 1\n    return [start, end]\n","cpp":"int firstAtLeast(const vector<int>& nums, int x) {\n    // first index with nums[i] >= x, or len(nums) if none\n    int lo = 0, hi = nums.size();\n    while (lo < hi) {\n        int mid = lo + (hi - lo) / 2;\n        if (nums[mid] >= x) {\n            hi = mid;\n        } else {\n            lo = mid + 1;\n        }\n    }\n    return lo;\n}\n\nvector<int> searchRange(const vector<int>& nums, int target) {\n    int start = firstAtLeast(nums, target);\n    if (start == (int)nums.size() || nums[start] != target) {\n        return {-1, -1};\n    }\n    // the last target is just before the first bigger value\n    int end = firstAtLeast(nums, target + 1) - 1;\n    return {start, end};\n}\n","java":"class Solution {\n    int firstAtLeast(int[] nums, int x) {\n        // first index with nums[i] >= x, or len(nums) if none\n        int lo = 0, hi = nums.length;\n        while (lo < hi) {\n            int mid = lo + (hi - lo) / 2;\n            if (nums[mid] >= x) {\n                hi = mid;\n            } else {\n                lo = mid + 1;\n            }\n        }\n        return lo;\n    }\n\n    public int[] searchRange(int[] nums, int target) {\n        int start = firstAtLeast(nums, target);\n        if (start == nums.length || nums[start] != target) {\n            return new int[]{-1, -1};\n        }\n        // the last target is just before the first bigger value\n        int end = firstAtLeast(nums, target + 1) - 1;\n        return new int[]{start, end};\n    }\n}\n"},"problemSlug":"find-first-and-last-position-of-element-in-sorted-array"}],"complexity":{"time":"O(log n); O(n log m) on the answer, where m is the size of the value range","space":"O(1)","why":"The range halves every step, so it takes about log₂ n steps to shrink to one. On the answer, each of the log m steps runs an O(n) check."},"notThis":["Unsorted data and a single lookup: a hash set is O(1) and needs no sort.","A pair with a target sum in sorted data: two pointers is O(n) for all pairs, cheaper than a binary search per element.","The check isn't one-way as the guess grows (adding an item can make it pass, then fail): use DP or try every value."],"ladder":[{"slug":"binary-search","tier":"warm-up","note":"Find x or report −1: the loop in its simplest form."},{"slug":"search-insert-position","tier":"warm-up","note":"The template: first index with nums[i] >= target."},{"slug":"sqrtx","tier":"warm-up","note":"Search on values: the last k with k × k <= x, in 64 bits."},{"slug":"find-first-and-last-position-of-element-in-sorted-array","tier":"core","note":"Two runs of the template, for target and target + 1."},{"slug":"search-a-2d-matrix","tier":"core","note":"Treat the matrix as one sorted list: index i is row i / n, column i % n."},{"slug":"find-minimum-in-rotated-sorted-array","tier":"core","note":"The question compares with the last element instead of a target."},{"slug":"search-in-rotated-sorted-array","tier":"core","note":"Work out which half is sorted, then check whether the target is in it."},{"slug":"find-peak-element","tier":"core","note":"Unsorted, but nums[mid] < nums[mid + 1] still tells you a peak is to the right."},{"slug":"koko-eating-bananas","tier":"core","note":"Binary search on the answer: the slowest speed that finishes in time."},{"slug":"capacity-to-ship-packages-within-d-days","tier":"core","note":"On the answer again, from max(weights) up to sum(weights), with a greedy check."},{"slug":"split-array-largest-sum","tier":"stretch","note":"The same check as shipping, in a problem that hides it well."},{"slug":"median-of-two-sorted-arrays","tier":"stretch","note":"Binary search on where to cut the shorter array so both halves balance."}]}$json$::jsonb),
('bit-manipulation',
  'Bit Manipulation',
  'Bit Manipulation',
  array['Every number appears twice (or three times) except one or two, and you need O(1) extra space.', 'The question is about the binary form itself: count the 1 bits, reverse them, check for a power of two.', 'You must add, swap or compare without the usual operators, or without extra memory.', 'n is small (up to about 20) and you need to try every subset, or track a set of up to 26 letters or 32 flags as one number.']::text[],
  'A 32-bit int is 32 yes-or-no flags. Bitwise operators work on all of them at the same time, in O(1). & keeps a bit only if both sides have it, | keeps it if either side does, ^ keeps it if exactly one side does, and << and >> shift the whole row. Bit i of x is (x >> i) & 1. To set it, use x | (1 << i). To clear it, use x & ~(1 << i).

XOR is the workhorse. x ^ x = 0, x ^ 0 = x, and the order doesn''t matter. XOR a whole array together and every pair cancels, whatever order the pair comes in, so only the unpaired value is left. That''s one pass and one variable. It''s correct because XOR works bit by bit: a bit is 1 at the end only if an odd number of values had it.

Two more tricks come up again and again. n & (n − 1) clears the lowest 1 bit, because subtracting 1 flips that bit and every 0 below it. So the loop runs once per 1 bit, and n is a power of two when n > 0 and n & (n − 1) == 0. n & −n keeps only the lowest 1 bit, which is the easy way to find one place where two numbers differ.

A mask from 0 to 2^n − 1 can stand for a subset: bit i set means item i is in it. Looping over every mask lists all 2^n subsets with no recursion, in O(n · 2^n).

Watch the edges. In C++, shifting into or past the sign bit of a signed int is undefined, so use unsigned. Java has >>> for a shift that ignores the sign. Python ints have no fixed width, so a negative number has endless 1s; mask with & 0xFFFFFFFF to see the 32-bit pattern.',
  'def single_number(nums: list[int]) -> int:
    # x ^ x = 0 and x ^ 0 = x, in any order, so every pair
    # cancels and only the unpaired number is left
    acc = 0
    for x in nums:
        acc ^= x
    return acc
',
  'Time O(n) for XOR folds; O(number of 1 bits) for n & (n − 1); O(n · 2^n) to try every subset · Space O(1), apart from the input',
  array['Forgetting precedence: in C++ and Java, x & 1 == 0 means x & (1 == 0). Always put brackets around bit tests.', 'Shifting 1 << 31 or more on a signed int in C++. Use 1u or 1LL, or unsigned types.', 'Using >> on a negative Java int and expecting 0s to come in from the left. That''s >>>.', 'Looping while n != 0 on a negative Python int. It never ends; mask with & 0xFFFFFFFF first.', 'Starting the mask loop at 0 when the question wants non-empty subsets.', 'Using x & −x on INT_MIN in C++ with a signed int. −INT_MIN overflows; do it on unsigned.', 'Reaching for XOR when values appear three times. Pairs cancel and triples don''t; count each bit mod 3 instead.']::text[],
  array['single-number', 'number-of-1-bits', 'power-of-two']::text[],
  23,
  $json${"version":1,"summary":"Treat a number as a row of 32 bits and work on all of them at once. XOR cancels pairs, n & (n − 1) removes the lowest 1, and an integer from 0 to 2^n − 1 can stand for a subset of n items.","prerequisites":["arrays-hashing"],"walkthrough":{"problemSlug":"single-number","input":"nums = [4, 1, 2, 1, 2]","intro":"one step per number XORed into acc, which starts at 0.","columns":["acc in binary","What happened","acc"],"steps":[{"window":"100","action":"0 ^ 4: the 4 bit turns on","best":"4"},{"window":"101","action":"^ 1: the 1 bit turns on","best":"5"},{"window":"111","action":"^ 2: the 2 bit turns on","best":"7"},{"window":"110","action":"^ 1: the second 1 turns the 1 bit back off","best":"6"},{"window":"100","action":"^ 2: the second 2 turns the 2 bit back off","best":"4"}],"result":"4. Both pairs cancelled even though they weren't next to each other, and only the bits of the single number were left."},"template":{"note":"Fold the whole array with ^. Anything that appears an even number of times disappears. Missing Number is the same trick with the indexes 0 to n XORed in too.","code":{"python":"def single_number(nums: list[int]) -> int:\n    # x ^ x = 0 and x ^ 0 = x, in any order, so every pair\n    # cancels and only the unpaired number is left\n    acc = 0\n    for x in nums:\n        acc ^= x\n    return acc\n","cpp":"int singleNumber(const vector<int>& nums) {\n    // x ^ x = 0 and x ^ 0 = x, in any order, so every pair\n    // cancels and only the unpaired number is left\n    int acc = 0;\n    for (int x : nums) acc ^= x;\n    return acc;\n}\n","java":"class Solution {\n    public int singleNumber(int[] nums) {\n        // x ^ x = 0 and x ^ 0 = x, in any order, so every pair\n        // cancels and only the unpaired number is left\n        int acc = 0;\n        for (int x : nums) acc ^= x;\n        return acc;\n    }\n}\n"}},"variants":[{"name":"Clear the lowest 1: n & (n − 1)","when":"You count 1 bits, test for a power of two, or build bit counts for 0 to n.","idea":"Each n &= n − 1 removes one 1 bit, so the loop runs once per 1, not 32 times. In Python, mask with 0xFFFFFFFF first, or a negative number never reaches 0.","code":{"python":"def hamming_weight(n: int) -> int:\n    # Python ints have no width: keep the low 32 bits,\n    # which is the same pattern C++ and Java see\n    n &= 0xFFFFFFFF\n    count = 0\n    while n:\n        # n - 1 flips the lowest 1 and the 0s below it,\n        # so n & (n - 1) clears exactly that lowest 1\n        n &= n - 1\n        count += 1\n    return count\n","cpp":"int hammingWeight(uint32_t n) {\n    int count = 0;\n    while (n != 0) {\n        // n - 1 flips the lowest 1 and the 0s below it,\n        // so n & (n - 1) clears exactly that lowest 1\n        n &= n - 1;\n        count++;\n    }\n    return count;\n}\n","java":"class Solution {\n    // n is read as 32 bits, sign bit included\n    public int hammingWeight(int n) {\n        int count = 0;\n        while (n != 0) {\n            // n - 1 flips the lowest 1 and the 0s below it,\n            // so n & (n - 1) clears exactly that lowest 1\n            n &= n - 1;\n            count++;\n        }\n        return count;\n    }\n}\n"},"problemSlug":"number-of-1-bits"},{"name":"Split by one differing bit","when":"Two numbers appear once and everything else twice.","idea":"XOR everything to get a ^ b. Its lowest 1 bit, from x & −x, is a place where a and b differ. XOR again, using only the numbers with that bit set: pairs still cancel, and you get a. Then b = (a ^ b) ^ a.","code":{"python":"def single_number_iii(nums: list[int]) -> list[int]:\n    # pairs cancel, so both = a ^ b. Since a != b, both has\n    # a 1 bit somewhere a and b differ\n    both = 0\n    for x in nums:\n        both ^= x\n    # x & -x keeps only the lowest 1 bit of x\n    low = both & -both\n    a = 0\n    for x in nums:\n        # split on that bit: a and b go to different sides,\n        # and the two copies of a pair go to the same side\n        if x & low:\n            a ^= x\n    return [a, both ^ a]\n","cpp":"vector<int> singleNumber(const vector<int>& nums) {\n    // pairs cancel, so both = a ^ b. Since a != b, both has\n    // a 1 bit somewhere a and b differ\n    unsigned both = 0;\n    for (int x : nums) both ^= (unsigned)x;\n    // x & -x keeps only the lowest 1 bit of x (unsigned,\n    // so -x can't overflow)\n    unsigned low = both & -both;\n    unsigned a = 0;\n    for (int x : nums) {\n        // split on that bit: a and b go to different sides,\n        // and the two copies of a pair go to the same side\n        if ((unsigned)x & low) a ^= (unsigned)x;\n    }\n    return {(int)a, (int)(both ^ a)};\n}\n","java":"class Solution {\n    public int[] singleNumber(int[] nums) {\n        // pairs cancel, so both = a ^ b. Since a != b, both\n        // has a 1 bit somewhere a and b differ\n        int both = 0;\n        for (int x : nums) both ^= x;\n        // x & -x keeps only the lowest 1 bit of x\n        int low = both & -both;\n        int a = 0;\n        for (int x : nums) {\n            // split on that bit: a and b go to different sides,\n            // and the two copies of a pair go to the same side\n            if ((x & low) != 0) a ^= x;\n        }\n        return new int[]{a, both ^ a};\n    }\n}\n"},"problemSlug":"single-number-iii"},{"name":"Subsets as bitmasks","when":"n is small and you need to check every subset.","idea":"Loop mask from 1 to 2^n − 1 (0 would be the empty subset). Item i is in the subset when (mask >> i) & 1 is 1. No recursion and no list to undo.","code":{"python":"def count_max_or_subsets(nums: list[int]) -> int:\n    n = len(nums)\n    # OR only adds bits, so the OR of everything is the max\n    best = 0\n    for x in nums:\n        best |= x\n    count = 0\n    # each mask from 1 to 2^n - 1 is one non-empty subset:\n    # bit i set means nums[i] is in it\n    for mask in range(1, 1 << n):\n        acc = 0\n        for i in range(n):\n            if (mask >> i) & 1:\n                acc |= nums[i]\n        if acc == best:\n            count += 1\n    return count\n","cpp":"int countMaxOrSubsets(const vector<int>& nums) {\n    int n = nums.size();\n    // OR only adds bits, so the OR of everything is the max\n    int best = 0;\n    for (int x : nums) best |= x;\n    int count = 0;\n    // each mask from 1 to 2^n - 1 is one non-empty subset:\n    // bit i set means nums[i] is in it\n    for (int mask = 1; mask < (1 << n); mask++) {\n        int acc = 0;\n        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {\n            if ((mask >> i) & 1) acc |= nums[i];\n        }\n        if (acc == best) count++;\n    }\n    return count;\n}\n","java":"class Solution {\n    public int countMaxOrSubsets(int[] nums) {\n        int n = nums.length;\n        // OR only adds bits, so the OR of everything is the max\n        int best = 0;\n        for (int x : nums) best |= x;\n        int count = 0;\n        // each mask from 1 to 2^n - 1 is one non-empty subset:\n        // bit i set means nums[i] is in it\n        for (int mask = 1; mask < (1 << n); mask++) {\n            int acc = 0;\n            for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {\n                if (((mask >> i) & 1) == 1) acc |= nums[i];\n            }\n            if (acc == best) count++;\n        }\n        return count;\n    }\n}\n"},"problemSlug":"count-number-of-maximum-bitwise-or-subsets"}],"complexity":{"time":"O(n) for XOR folds; O(number of 1 bits) for n & (n − 1); O(n · 2^n) to try every subset","space":"O(1), apart from the input","why":"Each bitwise operation touches all 32 bits at once in O(1). XOR folds do one pass. The n & (n − 1) loop stops after one step per 1 bit. There are 2^n masks, and each one checks n bits to build its subset."},"notThis":["Duplicates aren't in neat pairs, or you need the counts themselves: a hash map is clearer and still O(n).","n is bigger than about 20, so 2^n subsets is too many: look for DP over sums, or backtracking with pruning.","You need to list subsets in a particular order or with pruning: recursive backtracking is easier to control than a mask loop."],"ladder":[{"slug":"single-number","tier":"warm-up","note":"The template: XOR everything, and pairs cancel."},{"slug":"number-of-1-bits","tier":"warm-up","note":"n & (n − 1) clears one 1 bit per step."},{"slug":"power-of-two","tier":"warm-up","note":"One 1 bit: n > 0 and n & (n − 1) == 0."},{"slug":"missing-number","tier":"warm-up","note":"XOR the values with the indexes 0 to n; only the missing one is left unpaired."},{"slug":"counting-bits","tier":"core","note":"DP over bits: bits[i] = bits[i & (i − 1)] + 1."},{"slug":"reverse-bits","tier":"core","note":"Take the low bit, shift it into the answer, 32 times. Watch the sign bit."},{"slug":"single-number-iii","tier":"core","note":"Two singles: split the array on one bit where they differ."},{"slug":"single-number-ii","tier":"core","note":"Triples don't cancel with XOR. Count each of the 32 bits mod 3."},{"slug":"count-number-of-maximum-bitwise-or-subsets","tier":"core","note":"Every subset as a mask from 1 to 2^n − 1."},{"slug":"maximum-length-of-a-concatenated-string-with-unique-characters","tier":"core","note":"A 26-bit mask per word: two words clash when their masks share a bit."},{"slug":"sum-of-two-integers","tier":"core","note":"Add with ^ for the sum and & then << for the carry, until no carry is left."},{"slug":"find-the-longest-substring-containing-vowels-in-even-counts","tier":"stretch","note":"A 5-bit mask of vowel parities, with Prefix Sum's first-index map on top."},{"slug":"maximum-xor-of-two-numbers-in-an-array","tier":"stretch","note":"Build the answer from the top bit down, using a set of prefixes or a bit trie."},{"slug":"shortest-path-visiting-all-nodes","tier":"stretch","note":"BFS where the state is (node, mask of visited nodes)."}]}$json$::jsonb),
('bst',
  'Binary Search Tree',
  'Binary Search Tree',
  array['The problem says binary search tree, or the tree is built from sorted data.', 'You need to find, insert or delete a value, or the place where two values split apart.', 'The question talks about order: the kth smallest, the next larger value, values in a range.', 'You must check whether a tree follows the BST rule, or repair one that breaks it.']::text[],
  'A binary search tree keeps one rule at every node: all values in the left subtree are smaller than the node, and all values in the right subtree are larger. The rule is about whole subtrees, not just the two children.

That rule gives two tools. First, a comparison tells you where to go. If the target is smaller than the node, it can only be on the left, so the right subtree is thrown away, like the half you drop in binary search. Search, insert, delete and the split point of two values each walk one root-to-leaf path. Second, an in-order walk (left, node, right) visits the values in sorted order, so "kth smallest" or "is this sorted?" becomes a walk with a counter.

To check the rule, pass an allowed range down the tree. The root may hold anything. Its left child must be below the root, and its right child above it. Each step narrows the range by one bound, so a node deep on the left of a right subtree must sit between two ancestors. Comparing a node only with its parent misses exactly that case.

The cost is the height h, not the size n. A balanced tree has h near log n, so a walk takes about 20 steps for a million nodes. A tree built from sorted inserts is a chain with h = n, and every walk becomes O(n). LeetCode trees are not kept balanced, so state your answers as O(h).',
  'def is_valid_bst(root: Optional[TreeNode]) -> bool:
    # every value in this subtree must be inside (lo, hi)
    def check(node: Optional[TreeNode], lo: float,
              hi: float) -> bool:
        if not node:
            return True
        if node.val <= lo or node.val >= hi:
            return False
        # left: below this node; right: above it
        return (check(node.left, lo, node.val)
                and check(node.right, node.val, hi))

    return check(root, float("-inf"), float("inf"))
',
  'Time O(h) for one walk; O(n) to validate or to visit every node · Space O(h) for the recursion or the stack',
  array['Checking a node only against its parent. The rule covers whole subtrees, so pass both bounds down.', 'Using int bounds like INT_MIN and INT_MAX. A node holding exactly that value is valid but gets rejected. Use long bounds or null for no bound.', 'Allowing equal values. LeetCode''s BSTs are strict, so a value equal to a bound is invalid unless the problem says otherwise.', 'Recursing into both children on a search. That makes it O(n); the comparison should rule one side out.', 'Forgetting to assign the result back after an insert or delete (root.left = ...), so the change is lost.', 'In a delete with two children, copying the successor''s value up but not deleting the successor itself, which leaves a duplicate.', 'Saying O(log n) for an unbalanced tree. LeetCode trees can be chains, so the honest bound is O(h).']::text[],
  array['search-in-a-binary-search-tree', 'insert-into-a-binary-search-tree', 'range-sum-of-bst']::text[],
  14,
  $json${"version":1,"summary":"For trees where every node is larger than everything on its left and smaller than everything on its right. Comparing with a node tells you which side the answer is on, so you walk one path instead of visiting the whole tree, and an in-order walk gives the values in sorted order.","prerequisites":["tree-dfs","binary-search"],"walkthrough":{"problemSlug":"validate-binary-search-tree","input":"root = [8, 4, 12, 2, 6, 10, 14, null, null, 5, 9]","intro":"one step per node checked, left subtree before right, each with the range its value must fall in.","columns":["Node: allowed range","What happened","Valid so far"],"steps":[{"window":"8: (−∞, ∞)","action":"inside; left child gets (−∞, 8), right child gets (8, ∞)","best":"yes"},{"window":"4: (−∞, 8)","action":"inside; its children get (−∞, 4) and (4, 8)","best":"yes"},{"window":"2: (−∞, 4)","action":"inside; both children are null, which is valid","best":"yes"},{"window":"6: (4, 8)","action":"inside; its children get (4, 6) and (6, 8)","best":"yes"},{"window":"5: (4, 6)","action":"inside; no children","best":"yes"},{"window":"9: (6, 8)","action":"9 is above its parent 6, but 9 >= 8: return false","best":"no"},{"window":"back at 6, 4, 8","action":"each call gets false and returns false; 12 is never checked","best":"no"}],"result":"false. 9 sits in the left subtree of 8 but is larger than 8. A parent-only check would have said true."},"template":{"note":"Carry the allowed range (lo, hi) down the tree. Going left, the node's value becomes the new hi; going right, it becomes the new lo. Use bounds wider than int (long, or infinity) so the int limits are still valid values.","code":{"python":"def is_valid_bst(root: Optional[TreeNode]) -> bool:\n    # every value in this subtree must be inside (lo, hi)\n    def check(node: Optional[TreeNode], lo: float,\n              hi: float) -> bool:\n        if not node:\n            return True\n        if node.val <= lo or node.val >= hi:\n            return False\n        # left: below this node; right: above it\n        return (check(node.left, lo, node.val)\n                and check(node.right, node.val, hi))\n\n    return check(root, float(\"-inf\"), float(\"inf\"))\n","cpp":"// every value in this subtree must be inside (lo, hi)\nbool check(TreeNode* node, long long lo, long long hi) {\n    if (!node) return true;\n    if (node->val <= lo || node->val >= hi) return false;\n    // left: below this node; right: above it\n    return check(node->left, lo, node->val)\n        && check(node->right, node->val, hi);\n}\n\nbool isValidBST(TreeNode* root) {\n    return check(root, LLONG_MIN, LLONG_MAX);\n}\n","java":"class Solution {\n    public boolean isValidBST(TreeNode root) {\n        return check(root, Long.MIN_VALUE, Long.MAX_VALUE);\n    }\n\n    // every value in this subtree must be inside (lo, hi)\n    private boolean check(TreeNode node, long lo, long hi) {\n        if (node == null) return true;\n        if (node.val <= lo || node.val >= hi) return false;\n        // left: below this node; right: above it\n        return check(node.left, lo, node.val)\n            && check(node.right, node.val, hi);\n    }\n}\n"}},"variants":[{"name":"Walk one path","when":"You're looking for one place in the tree: a value, where to insert, or where two values split.","idea":"Compare and move left or right, never both. For the lowest common ancestor, keep going while p and q are on the same side. The first node where they split (or that is one of them) is the answer. No recursion, O(h) time and O(1) space.","code":{"python":"def lowest_common_ancestor(root: TreeNode, p: TreeNode,\n                           q: TreeNode) -> TreeNode:\n    node = root\n    while True:\n        if p.val < node.val and q.val < node.val:\n            # both smaller: the split is on the left\n            node = node.left\n        elif p.val > node.val and q.val > node.val:\n            # both larger: the split is on the right\n            node = node.right\n        else:\n            # they split here, or one of them is this node\n            return node\n","cpp":"TreeNode* lowestCommonAncestor(TreeNode* root, TreeNode* p,\n                               TreeNode* q) {\n    TreeNode* node = root;\n    while (true) {\n        if (p->val < node->val && q->val < node->val) {\n            // both smaller: the split is on the left\n            node = node->left;\n        } else if (p->val > node->val && q->val > node->val) {\n            // both larger: the split is on the right\n            node = node->right;\n        } else {\n            // they split here, or one of them is this node\n            return node;\n        }\n    }\n}\n","java":"class Solution {\n    public TreeNode lowestCommonAncestor(TreeNode root,\n            TreeNode p, TreeNode q) {\n        TreeNode node = root;\n        while (true) {\n            if (p.val < node.val && q.val < node.val) {\n                // both smaller: the split is on the left\n                node = node.left;\n            } else if (p.val > node.val && q.val > node.val) {\n                // both larger: the split is on the right\n                node = node.right;\n            } else {\n                // they split here, or one of them is this node\n                return node;\n            }\n        }\n    }\n}\n"},"problemSlug":"lowest-common-ancestor-of-a-binary-search-tree"},{"name":"In-order is sorted","when":"The question is about rank or order: the kth smallest, the next value, the smallest gap.","idea":"Walk left, node, right with an explicit stack, and count each node as it pops. The kth pop is the kth smallest, so you stop early instead of collecting all n values.","code":{"python":"def kth_smallest(root: Optional[TreeNode], k: int) -> int:\n    stack: list[TreeNode] = []\n    node = root\n    while True:\n        # go as far left as possible\n        while node:\n            stack.append(node)\n            node = node.left\n        # the next value in sorted order\n        node = stack.pop()\n        k -= 1\n        if k == 0:\n            return node.val\n        node = node.right\n","cpp":"int kthSmallest(TreeNode* root, int k) {\n    stack<TreeNode*> st;\n    TreeNode* node = root;\n    while (true) {\n        // go as far left as possible\n        while (node) {\n            st.push(node);\n            node = node->left;\n        }\n        // the next value in sorted order\n        node = st.top();\n        st.pop();\n        k--;\n        if (k == 0) return node->val;\n        node = node->right;\n    }\n}\n","java":"class Solution {\n    public int kthSmallest(TreeNode root, int k) {\n        Deque<TreeNode> stack = new ArrayDeque<>();\n        TreeNode node = root;\n        while (true) {\n            // go as far left as possible\n            while (node != null) {\n                stack.push(node);\n                node = node.left;\n            }\n            // the next value in sorted order\n            node = stack.pop();\n            k--;\n            if (k == 0) return node.val;\n            node = node.right;\n        }\n    }\n}\n"},"problemSlug":"kth-smallest-element-in-a-bst"},{"name":"Return the new subtree root","when":"The tree's shape changes: insert, delete or trim.","idea":"Let each call return the root of its subtree after the change, and assign it back: root.left = delete(root.left, key). A node with two children copies up its successor (the smallest value on its right) and then deletes that value from the right subtree.","code":{"python":"# returns the root of this subtree after the delete\ndef delete_node(root: Optional[TreeNode],\n                key: int) -> Optional[TreeNode]:\n    if not root:\n        return None\n    if key < root.val:\n        # go one way and re-attach what comes back\n        root.left = delete_node(root.left, key)\n    elif key > root.val:\n        root.right = delete_node(root.right, key)\n    elif not root.left:\n        # found it, with at most one child: lift that child\n        return root.right\n    elif not root.right:\n        return root.left\n    else:\n        # two children: copy the next larger value up,\n        # then delete that value from the right subtree\n        succ = root.right\n        while succ.left:\n            succ = succ.left\n        root.val = succ.val\n        root.right = delete_node(root.right, succ.val)\n    return root\n","cpp":"// returns the root of this subtree after the delete\nTreeNode* deleteNode(TreeNode* root, int key) {\n    if (!root) return nullptr;\n    if (key < root->val) {\n        // go one way and re-attach what comes back\n        root->left = deleteNode(root->left, key);\n    } else if (key > root->val) {\n        root->right = deleteNode(root->right, key);\n    } else if (!root->left) {\n        // found it, with at most one child: lift that child\n        return root->right;\n    } else if (!root->right) {\n        return root->left;\n    } else {\n        // two children: copy the next larger value up,\n        // then delete that value from the right subtree\n        TreeNode* succ = root->right;\n        while (succ->left) succ = succ->left;\n        root->val = succ->val;\n        root->right = deleteNode(root->right, succ->val);\n    }\n    return root;\n}\n","java":"class Solution {\n    // returns the root of this subtree after the delete\n    public TreeNode deleteNode(TreeNode root, int key) {\n        if (root == null) return null;\n        if (key < root.val) {\n            // go one way and re-attach what comes back\n            root.left = deleteNode(root.left, key);\n        } else if (key > root.val) {\n            root.right = deleteNode(root.right, key);\n        } else if (root.left == null) {\n            // found it, with at most one child: lift that child\n            return root.right;\n        } else if (root.right == null) {\n            return root.left;\n        } else {\n            // two children: copy the next larger value up,\n            // then delete that value from the right subtree\n            TreeNode succ = root.right;\n            while (succ.left != null) succ = succ.left;\n            root.val = succ.val;\n            root.right = deleteNode(root.right, succ.val);\n        }\n        return root;\n    }\n}\n"},"problemSlug":"delete-node-in-a-bst"}],"complexity":{"time":"O(h) for one walk; O(n) to validate or to visit every node","space":"O(h) for the recursion or the stack","why":"A walk does constant work per level and moves down one level each step, so it costs the height. Validation has to look at every node once. The stack only holds one root-to-node path. For a balanced tree h is about log n; for a chain it is n."},"notThis":["A plain binary tree with no ordering: the comparison tells you nothing, so visit both sides. See Tree DFS.","A sorted array you only search: binary search on the array is simpler and needs no tree. See Binary Search.","You need sorted order while values keep arriving and leaving in your own code: use the language's ordered set (TreeMap, std::set, or SortedList) instead of writing a BST.","Only the smallest or largest is needed each time: a heap is simpler. See Heap / Top-K."],"ladder":[{"slug":"search-in-a-binary-search-tree","tier":"warm-up","note":"The plain walk: compare, go one way, stop at the value or null."},{"slug":"insert-into-a-binary-search-tree","tier":"warm-up","note":"Walk to the null where the value belongs and hang a new node there."},{"slug":"range-sum-of-bst","tier":"warm-up","note":"Skip a whole subtree when it is all below low or all above high."},{"slug":"validate-binary-search-tree","tier":"core","note":"The template: carry (lo, hi) down so ancestors are checked too."},{"slug":"lowest-common-ancestor-of-a-binary-search-tree","tier":"core","note":"Walk while both values are on one side; the split point is the answer."},{"slug":"kth-smallest-element-in-a-bst","tier":"core","note":"In-order with a stack and a counter, stopping at k."},{"slug":"convert-sorted-array-to-binary-search-tree","tier":"core","note":"Build in reverse: the middle element is the root, recurse on each half."},{"slug":"delete-node-in-a-bst","tier":"core","note":"Return the new subtree root; two children means copy up the successor."},{"slug":"trim-a-binary-search-tree","tier":"core","note":"A node below low means its whole left side goes too: return the trimmed right side."},{"slug":"binary-search-tree-iterator","tier":"core","note":"The in-order stack, paused between calls: next() is one pop plus a walk left."},{"slug":"recover-binary-search-tree","tier":"stretch","note":"In-order should be sorted; find the two places it drops and swap those values back."},{"slug":"maximum-sum-bst-in-binary-tree","tier":"stretch","note":"Tree DFS that returns min, max, sum and is-BST for each subtree, then combines them."}]}$json$::jsonb),
('dp-1d',
  'Dynamic Programming',
  '1D Dynamic Programming',
  array['The question asks for a maximum, a minimum, a count of ways, or whether something is possible, not for the list of answers.', 'A choice at each position (take it or skip it, which coin, where to cut) affects what''s allowed next.', 'The brute-force recursion calls itself on the same smaller input many times, like f(n − 1) and f(n − 2) both calling f(n − 3).', 'Greedy almost works but a small example breaks it: coins [1, 3, 4] for 6, or houses [2, 1, 1, 2].']::text[],
  'Dynamic programming is recursion that remembers. If a problem''s best answer is built from best answers to smaller versions of itself, and those smaller versions repeat, solve each one once and store it. Use the same four-part recipe every time.

State: say in words what dp[i] means, for example "the most money from the first i houses". Transition: write dp[i] from smaller entries by asking what the last decision was. Either house i is skipped, giving dp[i − 1], or it is robbed, giving dp[i − 2] + nums[i − 1]. Take the larger. Base cases: the entries the transition can''t build, here dp[0] = 0 and dp[1] = nums[0]. Order: fill i so that everything dp[i] reads is already done, which is left to right here.

Why it''s correct: the transition covers every possible last decision, and each option uses a smaller entry that is already the best for its own range. Why it''s fast: n states, each built from a few others, so O(n) time instead of the exponential tree of plain recursion.

You can write the same thing top-down. Recurse from dp(n), and cache each result in a memo the first time you compute it. Memo is easier to get right, because the recursion works out the order for you. A table avoids deep recursion and makes the next step easy: if dp[i] only reads dp[i − 1] and dp[i − 2], keep two variables instead of the array, and space drops from O(n) to O(1).

When the state needs two numbers, like a position in each of two strings, it is 2D DP. When there are no repeated subproblems and you need every answer listed, use backtracking. When one local choice is provably always safe, greedy is simpler.',
  'def rob(nums: list[int]) -> int:
    n = len(nums)
    # state: dp[i] = the most from the first i houses
    dp = [0] * (n + 1)
    # base cases: no houses gives 0, one house gives itself
    if n > 0:
        dp[1] = nums[0]
    # order: left to right, as dp[i] reads i - 1 and i - 2
    for i in range(2, n + 1):
        # skip house i, or rob it and add dp[i - 2]
        dp[i] = max(dp[i - 1], dp[i - 2] + nums[i - 1])
    return dp[n]
',
  'Time O(n) for house robber; O(n · k) for k coins; O(n²) for LIS · Space O(n) for the table, or O(1) with rolling variables',
  array['Starting to code before writing the state in words. If you can''t say what dp[i] means, the transition will be wrong.', 'Filling in the wrong order, so dp[i] reads an entry that hasn''t been computed yet.', 'Off-by-one between dp and nums: with dp over the first i items, item i is nums[i − 1].', 'Using 0 or −1 for unreachable states in a min problem. Use a value larger than any real answer and check for it at the end.', 'Returning dp[n − 1] when the state is "ends at i". The best run can end anywhere; take the max.', 'Rolling to two variables and overwriting prev1 before prev2 has read it. Compute the new value first, then shift.', 'Memoising on a state that leaves something out (like the circle in House Robber II). Two calls that look the same then need different answers.']::text[],
  array['climbing-stairs', 'min-cost-climbing-stairs', 'house-robber']::text[],
  21,
  $json${"version":1,"summary":"For questions that ask for the best total, the fewest steps or the number of ways, where the answer at position i is built from answers at smaller positions. Fill one array from the base cases up, and each subproblem is solved once.","prerequisites":["backtracking"],"walkthrough":{"problemSlug":"house-robber","input":"nums = [2, 7, 9, 3, 1]","intro":"one step per table entry, filling dp[i] = the most from the first i houses, left to right.","columns":["dp so far","How dp[i] was filled","dp[i]"],"steps":[{"window":"[0]","action":"base: dp[0] = 0, no houses","best":"0"},{"window":"[0, 2]","action":"base: dp[1] = nums[0] = 2","best":"2"},{"window":"[0, 2, 7]","action":"i = 2: skip gives dp[1] = 2; rob 7 gives dp[0] + 7 = 7","best":"7"},{"window":"[0, 2, 7, 11]","action":"i = 3: skip gives 7; rob 9 gives dp[1] + 9 = 11","best":"11"},{"window":"[0, 2, 7, 11, 11]","action":"i = 4: skip gives 11; rob 3 gives dp[2] + 3 = 10","best":"11"},{"window":"[0, 2, 7, 11, 11, 12]","action":"i = 5: skip gives 11; rob 1 gives dp[3] + 1 = 12","best":"12"}],"result":"12, from houses 2 + 9 + 1. At i = 4 the table kept 11 instead of taking 3; that's the step greedy can't make."},"template":{"note":"Write the state as a comment first, then the base cases, then the loop with the transition. If the loop only reads the last one or two entries, swap the array for that many variables.","code":{"python":"def rob(nums: list[int]) -> int:\n    n = len(nums)\n    # state: dp[i] = the most from the first i houses\n    dp = [0] * (n + 1)\n    # base cases: no houses gives 0, one house gives itself\n    if n > 0:\n        dp[1] = nums[0]\n    # order: left to right, as dp[i] reads i - 1 and i - 2\n    for i in range(2, n + 1):\n        # skip house i, or rob it and add dp[i - 2]\n        dp[i] = max(dp[i - 1], dp[i - 2] + nums[i - 1])\n    return dp[n]\n","cpp":"int rob(const vector<int>& nums) {\n    int n = nums.size();\n    // state: dp[i] = the most from the first i houses\n    vector<int> dp(n + 1, 0);\n    // base cases: no houses gives 0, one house gives itself\n    if (n > 0) dp[1] = nums[0];\n    // order: left to right, as dp[i] reads i - 1 and i - 2\n    for (int i = 2; i <= n; i++) {\n        // skip house i, or rob it and add dp[i - 2]\n        dp[i] = max(dp[i - 1], dp[i - 2] + nums[i - 1]);\n    }\n    return dp[n];\n}\n","java":"class Solution {\n    public int rob(int[] nums) {\n        int n = nums.length;\n        // state: dp[i] = the most from the first i houses\n        int[] dp = new int[n + 1];\n        // base cases: no houses gives 0, one house gives itself\n        if (n > 0) dp[1] = nums[0];\n        // order: left to right, as dp[i] reads i - 1 and i - 2\n        for (int i = 2; i <= n; i++) {\n            // skip house i, or rob it and add dp[i - 2]\n            dp[i] = Math.max(dp[i - 1],\n                             dp[i - 2] + nums[i - 1]);\n        }\n        return dp[n];\n    }\n}\n"}},"variants":[{"name":"Two variables instead of the array","when":"dp[i] reads only the last one or two entries, or the input is a circle that you split into two lines.","idea":"Keep prev2 and prev1 for dp[i − 2] and dp[i − 1] and shift them each step, for O(1) space. For a circle, the first and last houses can't both be robbed, so solve the line without the last house and the line without the first, and take the better.","code":{"python":"def rob(nums: list[int]) -> int:\n    n = len(nums)\n    # one house has no neighbour, so take it\n    if n == 1:\n        return nums[0]\n\n    def rob_line(lo: int, hi: int) -> int:\n        # houses lo .. hi - 1 in a row. dp[i] only reads\n        # dp[i - 1] and dp[i - 2], so keep just those two\n        prev2, prev1 = 0, 0\n        for i in range(lo, hi):\n            cur = max(prev1, prev2 + nums[i])\n            prev2, prev1 = prev1, cur\n        return prev1\n\n    # first and last are neighbours: leave one of them out\n    return max(rob_line(0, n - 1), rob_line(1, n))\n","cpp":"int robLine(const vector<int>& nums, int lo, int hi) {\n    // houses lo .. hi - 1 in a row. dp[i] only reads\n    // dp[i - 1] and dp[i - 2], so keep just those two\n    int prev2 = 0, prev1 = 0;\n    for (int i = lo; i < hi; i++) {\n        int cur = max(prev1, prev2 + nums[i]);\n        prev2 = prev1;\n        prev1 = cur;\n    }\n    return prev1;\n}\n\nint rob(const vector<int>& nums) {\n    int n = nums.size();\n    // one house has no neighbour, so take it\n    if (n == 1) return nums[0];\n    // first and last are neighbours: leave one of them out\n    return max(robLine(nums, 0, n - 1), robLine(nums, 1, n));\n}\n","java":"class Solution {\n    public int rob(int[] nums) {\n        int n = nums.length;\n        // one house has no neighbour, so take it\n        if (n == 1) return nums[0];\n        // first and last are neighbours: leave one of them out\n        return Math.max(robLine(nums, 0, n - 1),\n                        robLine(nums, 1, n));\n    }\n\n    private int robLine(int[] nums, int lo, int hi) {\n        // houses lo .. hi - 1 in a row. dp[i] only reads\n        // dp[i - 1] and dp[i - 2], so keep just those two\n        int prev2 = 0, prev1 = 0;\n        for (int i = lo; i < hi; i++) {\n            int cur = Math.max(prev1, prev2 + nums[i]);\n            prev2 = prev1;\n            prev1 = cur;\n        }\n        return prev1;\n    }\n}\n"},"problemSlug":"house-robber-ii"},{"name":"Minimum over choices, with an impossible marker","when":"Each step picks one of several options (which coin) and some totals can't be reached at all.","idea":"dp[a] = min over coins c ≤ a of dp[a − c] + 1. Fill with a value that can never be a real answer (amount + 1), so unreachable states stay unreachable instead of looking like 0.","code":{"python":"def coin_change(coins: list[int], amount: int) -> int:\n    # state: dp[a] = fewest coins that make exactly a.\n    # amount + 1 coins is impossible, so it means \"can't\"\n    big = amount + 1\n    dp = [big] * (amount + 1)\n    # base case: 0 needs no coins\n    dp[0] = 0\n    # order: small amounts first; dp[a] reads dp[a - c]\n    for a in range(1, amount + 1):\n        for c in coins:\n            if c <= a:\n                # coin c last, on top of the best for a - c\n                dp[a] = min(dp[a], dp[a - c] + 1)\n    return -1 if dp[amount] == big else dp[amount]\n","cpp":"int coinChange(const vector<int>& coins, int amount) {\n    // state: dp[a] = fewest coins that make exactly a.\n    // amount + 1 coins is impossible, so it means \"can't\"\n    int big = amount + 1;\n    vector<int> dp(amount + 1, big);\n    // base case: 0 needs no coins\n    dp[0] = 0;\n    // order: small amounts first; dp[a] reads dp[a - c]\n    for (int a = 1; a <= amount; a++) {\n        for (int c : coins) {\n            if (c <= a) {\n                // coin c last, on top of the best for a - c\n                dp[a] = min(dp[a], dp[a - c] + 1);\n            }\n        }\n    }\n    return dp[amount] == big ? -1 : dp[amount];\n}\n","java":"class Solution {\n    public int coinChange(int[] coins, int amount) {\n        // state: dp[a] = fewest coins that make exactly a.\n        // amount + 1 coins is impossible, so it means \"can't\"\n        int big = amount + 1;\n        int[] dp = new int[amount + 1];\n        Arrays.fill(dp, big);\n        // base case: 0 needs no coins\n        dp[0] = 0;\n        // order: small amounts first; dp[a] reads dp[a - c]\n        for (int a = 1; a <= amount; a++) {\n            for (int c : coins) {\n                if (c <= a) {\n                    // coin c last, on top of the best for a - c\n                    dp[a] = Math.min(dp[a], dp[a - c] + 1);\n                }\n            }\n        }\n        return dp[amount] == big ? -1 : dp[amount];\n    }\n}\n"},"problemSlug":"coin-change"},{"name":"State that ends at i","when":"The answer is a subsequence that can end anywhere, like the longest increasing one.","idea":"Define dp[i] as the best that ends exactly at i. The transition looks at every j < i that can come before i. The answer is max(dp), not dp[n − 1].","code":{"python":"def length_of_lis(nums: list[int]) -> int:\n    n = len(nums)\n    # state: dp[i] = longest increasing run ending at i\n    # base case: nums[i] alone is a run of length 1\n    dp = [1] * n\n    for i in range(n):\n        for j in range(i):\n            # nums[i] can extend any run that ends lower\n            if nums[j] < nums[i]:\n                dp[i] = max(dp[i], dp[j] + 1)\n    # the run can end anywhere, so take the best of all i\n    return max(dp)\n","cpp":"int lengthOfLIS(const vector<int>& nums) {\n    int n = nums.size();\n    // state: dp[i] = longest increasing run ending at i\n    // base case: nums[i] alone is a run of length 1\n    vector<int> dp(n, 1);\n    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {\n        for (int j = 0; j < i; j++) {\n            // nums[i] can extend any run that ends lower\n            if (nums[j] < nums[i]) {\n                dp[i] = max(dp[i], dp[j] + 1);\n            }\n        }\n    }\n    // the run can end anywhere, so take the best of all i\n    return *max_element(dp.begin(), dp.end());\n}\n","java":"class Solution {\n    public int lengthOfLIS(int[] nums) {\n        int n = nums.length;\n        // state: dp[i] = longest increasing run ending at i\n        // base case: nums[i] alone is a run of length 1\n        int[] dp = new int[n];\n        Arrays.fill(dp, 1);\n        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {\n            for (int j = 0; j < i; j++) {\n                // nums[i] can extend any run that ends lower\n                if (nums[j] < nums[i]) {\n                    dp[i] = Math.max(dp[i], dp[j] + 1);\n                }\n            }\n        }\n        // the run can end anywhere, so take the best of all i\n        return Arrays.stream(dp).max().getAsInt();\n    }\n}\n"},"problemSlug":"longest-increasing-subsequence"}],"complexity":{"time":"O(n) for house robber; O(n · k) for k coins; O(n²) for LIS","space":"O(n) for the table, or O(1) with rolling variables","why":"Each state is filled once, and the cost of filling it is the number of options the transition checks: 2 for house robber, one per coin for coin change, every earlier index for LIS. The table holds one entry per state; when the transition only reads the last two entries, two variables are enough."},"notThis":["The question wants every arrangement listed, not a count or a best: use backtracking.","The state needs two indices, like positions in two strings or a row and a column: that's 2D DP.","A local choice is provably always safe, like taking the earliest-ending interval: greedy, with no table.","You need the best contiguous subarray sum and numbers can be negative: Kadane's algorithm is this DP with one variable, so write it that way rather than building a table."],"ladder":[{"slug":"climbing-stairs","tier":"warm-up","note":"dp[i] = dp[i − 1] + dp[i − 2]: count ways, then roll to two variables."},{"slug":"min-cost-climbing-stairs","tier":"warm-up","note":"The same shape with a min and a cost per step. Get the base cases right."},{"slug":"house-robber","tier":"core","note":"The template: take or skip, max of the two."},{"slug":"house-robber-ii","tier":"core","note":"A circle: run the line twice, once without each end."},{"slug":"delete-and-earn","tier":"core","note":"Turn it into House Robber over values: count each value, then take or skip v."},{"slug":"decode-ways","tier":"core","note":"Count ways with conditions: one-digit and two-digit steps, and '0' can't stand alone."},{"slug":"coin-change","tier":"core","note":"Minimum over choices, with an impossible marker for amounts you can't make."},{"slug":"word-break","tier":"core","note":"A boolean state: dp[i] is true if some word ends at i after a true dp[j]."},{"slug":"maximum-product-subarray","tier":"core","note":"Keep the best and the worst ending at i, because a negative flips them."},{"slug":"longest-increasing-subsequence","tier":"core","note":"State that ends at i, answer is the max over all i. Then try the O(n log n) version."},{"slug":"best-time-to-buy-and-sell-stock-with-cooldown","tier":"stretch","note":"Several states per day (holding, sold, resting), each with its own transition."},{"slug":"maximum-profit-in-job-scheduling","tier":"stretch","note":"Sort by end time; dp[i] takes job i plus the best before its start, found by binary search."},{"slug":"russian-doll-envelopes","tier":"stretch","note":"LIS in disguise: sort one side, break ties the right way, then LIS in O(n log n)."}]}$json$::jsonb),
('dp-2d',
  'Dynamic Programming',
  '2D Dynamic Programming',
  array['Two strings are compared or transformed: a common subsequence, an edit distance, whether one interleaves the others.', 'A grid where you move only right and down, and want the number of paths or the cheapest one.', 'Each item is taken or left once, under a limit on total weight or sum: 0/1 knapsack, subset sum, partition.', 'A 1D state isn''t enough because the answer also depends on a second thing, like how much budget is left.']::text[],
  'The recipe is the same as 1D DP, with one more index. State: say what dp[i][j] means, in words. For two strings it''s usually "the answer for the first i letters of one and the first j letters of the other". For a grid it''s "the answer for reaching cell (i, j)". For knapsack it''s "using only the first i items, with capacity j".

Transition: look at the last decision. In LCS, if the last letters match, use them both: dp[i − 1][j − 1] + 1. If not, one of the two letters isn''t in the answer, so take the better of dp[i − 1][j] and dp[i][j − 1]. Base cases: row 0 and column 0, where one side is empty. Order: row by row, left to right, because each cell reads the cell above, the one to its left and the one diagonally up-left.

Why it''s correct: every option for the last step is covered, and each one points to a smaller prefix whose cell already holds its best answer. Why it''s fast: m · n cells, each filled in O(1), so O(m · n) time. That replaces an exponential number of subsequences or paths.

Space: if row i reads only row i − 1, keep one row of length n + 1. In a grid, dp[c] still holds the cell above until you overwrite it, and dp[c − 1] already holds the cell to the left. In 0/1 knapsack, loop the capacity from high to low. A left-to-right loop would read values from this row and let one item be used twice.

It''s the wrong tool when the state needs more than you can store: two indices of 10^5 each is 10^10 cells. Then look for a greedy rule, a smaller state, or a different structure.',
  'def longest_common_subsequence(text1: str, text2: str) -> int:
    m, n = len(text1), len(text2)
    # state: dp[i][j] = LCS of text1[:i] and text2[:j]
    # base cases: row 0 and column 0 stay 0 (an empty side)
    dp = [[0] * (n + 1) for _ in range(m + 1)]
    # order: row by row, left to right. A cell reads the one
    # above, the one to its left and the one up-left
    for i in range(1, m + 1):
        for j in range(1, n + 1):
            if text1[i - 1] == text2[j - 1]:
                # the last letters match: use them both
                dp[i][j] = dp[i - 1][j - 1] + 1
            else:
                # drop the last letter of one side
                dp[i][j] = max(dp[i - 1][j], dp[i][j - 1])
    return dp[m][n]
',
  'Time O(m · n) cells, each filled in O(1); O(n · target) for knapsack · Space O(m · n) for the full table, or O(n) with one row',
  array['Mixing up dp indexes and string indexes. With dp over the first i letters, the letter is text1[i − 1].', 'Leaving row 0 and column 0 at 0 for edit distance. They are i and j: deletes and inserts.', 'Looping capacity left to right in 0/1 knapsack, which quietly turns it into unbounded knapsack.', 'Shrinking to one row when the transition needs the diagonal, and overwriting it before it''s read. Save it in a variable first.', 'In a grid, forgetting the first row and first column have only one way in.', 'Allocating m × n when m and n are both large. Check the limits before you build the table.', 'Skipping the quick check that makes a knapsack impossible, such as an odd total in Partition Equal Subset Sum.']::text[],
  array['unique-paths', 'unique-paths-ii', 'minimum-path-sum']::text[],
  22,
  $json${"version":1,"summary":"Dynamic programming where the state needs two numbers: a row and a column of a grid, a position in each of two strings, or an item and a remaining capacity. Fill a table cell by cell so every cell's inputs are already done.","prerequisites":["dp-1d"],"walkthrough":{"problemSlug":"longest-common-subsequence","input":"text1 = \"abcde\", text2 = \"ace\"","intro":"one step per row of the table. Row i is text1's first i letters; columns j = 0 to 3 are text2's first j letters.","columns":["Row (dp[i][0..3])","How the row filled","dp[i][3]"],"steps":[{"window":"row 0: 0 0 0 0","action":"base: with no letters of text1 there is nothing in common","best":"0"},{"window":"'a': 0 1 1 1","action":"i = 1: 'a' matches text2's 'a' at j = 1, so dp[0][0] + 1 = 1. j = 2 and 3 take 1 from the left","best":"1"},{"window":"'b': 0 1 1 1","action":"i = 2: 'b' matches nothing. Each cell is the max of above and left","best":"1"},{"window":"'c': 0 1 2 2","action":"i = 3: 'c' matches at j = 2, so dp[2][1] + 1 = 2. j = 3 takes 2 from the left","best":"2"},{"window":"'d': 0 1 2 2","action":"i = 4: 'd' matches nothing, so the row repeats the one above","best":"2"},{"window":"'e': 0 1 2 3","action":"i = 5: 'e' matches at j = 3, so dp[4][2] + 1 = 3","best":"3"}],"result":"3, the subsequence \"ace\". Each match came from the diagonal, and every other cell copied the better of its two neighbours."},"template":{"note":"An (m + 1) × (n + 1) table with row 0 and column 0 as the empty base cases. Match: diagonal + 1. No match: best of above and left. Change the meaning and the transition, and edit distance, interleaving and palindromic subsequence use the same loop.","code":{"python":"def longest_common_subsequence(text1: str, text2: str) -> int:\n    m, n = len(text1), len(text2)\n    # state: dp[i][j] = LCS of text1[:i] and text2[:j]\n    # base cases: row 0 and column 0 stay 0 (an empty side)\n    dp = [[0] * (n + 1) for _ in range(m + 1)]\n    # order: row by row, left to right. A cell reads the one\n    # above, the one to its left and the one up-left\n    for i in range(1, m + 1):\n        for j in range(1, n + 1):\n            if text1[i - 1] == text2[j - 1]:\n                # the last letters match: use them both\n                dp[i][j] = dp[i - 1][j - 1] + 1\n            else:\n                # drop the last letter of one side\n                dp[i][j] = max(dp[i - 1][j], dp[i][j - 1])\n    return dp[m][n]\n","cpp":"int longestCommonSubsequence(const string& text1,\n                             const string& text2) {\n    int m = text1.size(), n = text2.size();\n    // state: dp[i][j] = LCS of text1[:i] and text2[:j]\n    // base cases: row 0 and column 0 stay 0 (an empty side)\n    vector<vector<int>> dp(m + 1, vector<int>(n + 1, 0));\n    // order: row by row, left to right. A cell reads the one\n    // above, the one to its left and the one up-left\n    for (int i = 1; i <= m; i++) {\n        for (int j = 1; j <= n; j++) {\n            if (text1[i - 1] == text2[j - 1]) {\n                // the last letters match: use them both\n                dp[i][j] = dp[i - 1][j - 1] + 1;\n            } else {\n                // drop the last letter of one side\n                dp[i][j] = max(dp[i - 1][j], dp[i][j - 1]);\n            }\n        }\n    }\n    return dp[m][n];\n}\n","java":"class Solution {\n    public int longestCommonSubsequence(String text1,\n                                        String text2) {\n        int m = text1.length(), n = text2.length();\n        // state: dp[i][j] = LCS of text1[:i] and text2[:j]\n        // base cases: row 0 and column 0 stay 0 (an empty side)\n        int[][] dp = new int[m + 1][n + 1];\n        // order: row by row, left to right. A cell reads the\n        // one above, the one to its left and the one up-left\n        for (int i = 1; i <= m; i++) {\n            for (int j = 1; j <= n; j++) {\n                char a = text1.charAt(i - 1);\n                char b = text2.charAt(j - 1);\n                if (a == b) {\n                    // the last letters match: use them both\n                    dp[i][j] = dp[i - 1][j - 1] + 1;\n                } else {\n                    // drop the last letter of one side\n                    dp[i][j] = Math.max(dp[i - 1][j],\n                                        dp[i][j - 1]);\n                }\n            }\n        }\n        return dp[m][n];\n    }\n}\n"}},"variants":[{"name":"Grid paths with one row","when":"You move right or down through a grid and want the cheapest path or the number of paths.","idea":"dp[c] = grid[r][c] + min(above, left). Keep one row: before you overwrite dp[c] it is the cell above, and dp[c − 1] is the cell to the left in this row. Start with a pretend 0 above the first cell.","code":{"python":"def min_path_sum(grid: list[list[int]]) -> int:\n    m, n = len(grid), len(grid[0])\n    inf = float(\"inf\")\n    # state: dp[r][c] = cheapest path to (r, c).\n    # Only row r - 1 is ever read, so keep one row: before\n    # dp[c] is updated it still holds the cell above\n    dp = [inf] * n\n    # base case: a pretend cell above (0, 0) that costs 0\n    dp[0] = 0\n    for r in range(m):\n        for c in range(n):\n            # left: dp[c - 1], already updated for this row\n            left = dp[c - 1] if c > 0 else inf\n            # above: dp[c], still last row's value\n            dp[c] = grid[r][c] + min(dp[c], left)\n    return dp[n - 1]\n","cpp":"int minPathSum(const vector<vector<int>>& grid) {\n    int m = grid.size(), n = grid[0].size();\n    // state: dp[r][c] = cheapest path to (r, c).\n    // Only row r - 1 is ever read, so keep one row: before\n    // dp[c] is updated it still holds the cell above\n    vector<int> dp(n, INT_MAX);\n    // base case: a pretend cell above (0, 0) that costs 0\n    dp[0] = 0;\n    for (int r = 0; r < m; r++) {\n        for (int c = 0; c < n; c++) {\n            // left: dp[c - 1], already updated for this row\n            int left = c > 0 ? dp[c - 1] : INT_MAX;\n            // above: dp[c], still last row's value\n            dp[c] = grid[r][c] + min(dp[c], left);\n        }\n    }\n    return dp[n - 1];\n}\n","java":"class Solution {\n    public int minPathSum(int[][] grid) {\n        int m = grid.length, n = grid[0].length;\n        // state: dp[r][c] = cheapest path to (r, c).\n        // Only row r - 1 is ever read, so keep one row: before\n        // dp[c] is updated it still holds the cell above\n        int[] dp = new int[n];\n        Arrays.fill(dp, Integer.MAX_VALUE);\n        // base case: a pretend cell above (0, 0) that costs 0\n        dp[0] = 0;\n        for (int r = 0; r < m; r++) {\n            for (int c = 0; c < n; c++) {\n                // left: dp[c - 1], already updated for this row\n                int left = c > 0 ? dp[c - 1]\n                                 : Integer.MAX_VALUE;\n                // above: dp[c], still last row's value\n                dp[c] = grid[r][c] + Math.min(dp[c], left);\n            }\n        }\n        return dp[n - 1];\n    }\n}\n"},"problemSlug":"minimum-path-sum"},{"name":"Three choices: edit distance","when":"You turn one string into another with insert, delete and replace, each costing 1.","idea":"Base cases are no longer 0: turning i letters into none costs i deletes, and none into j letters costs j inserts. On a mismatch, take 1 + the min of delete (above), insert (left) and replace (diagonal).","code":{"python":"def min_distance(word1: str, word2: str) -> int:\n    m, n = len(word1), len(word2)\n    # state: dp[i][j] = edits from word1[:i] to word2[:j]\n    dp = [[0] * (n + 1) for _ in range(m + 1)]\n    # base cases: i letters to none is i deletes,\n    # none to j letters is j inserts\n    for i in range(m + 1):\n        dp[i][0] = i\n    for j in range(n + 1):\n        dp[0][j] = j\n    for i in range(1, m + 1):\n        for j in range(1, n + 1):\n            if word1[i - 1] == word2[j - 1]:\n                # same last letter: it needs no edit\n                dp[i][j] = dp[i - 1][j - 1]\n            else:\n                # one edit, then the cheapest of: delete\n                # (up), insert (left), replace (up-left)\n                dp[i][j] = 1 + min(\n                    dp[i - 1][j], dp[i][j - 1], dp[i - 1][j - 1]\n                )\n    return dp[m][n]\n","cpp":"int minDistance(const string& word1, const string& word2) {\n    int m = word1.size(), n = word2.size();\n    // state: dp[i][j] = edits from word1[:i] to word2[:j]\n    vector<vector<int>> dp(m + 1, vector<int>(n + 1, 0));\n    // base cases: i letters to none is i deletes,\n    // none to j letters is j inserts\n    for (int i = 0; i <= m; i++) dp[i][0] = i;\n    for (int j = 0; j <= n; j++) dp[0][j] = j;\n    for (int i = 1; i <= m; i++) {\n        for (int j = 1; j <= n; j++) {\n            if (word1[i - 1] == word2[j - 1]) {\n                // same last letter: it needs no edit\n                dp[i][j] = dp[i - 1][j - 1];\n            } else {\n                // one edit, then the cheapest of: delete\n                // (up), insert (left), replace (up-left)\n                dp[i][j] = 1 + min({dp[i - 1][j], dp[i][j - 1],\n                                    dp[i - 1][j - 1]});\n            }\n        }\n    }\n    return dp[m][n];\n}\n","java":"class Solution {\n    public int minDistance(String word1, String word2) {\n        int m = word1.length(), n = word2.length();\n        // state: dp[i][j] = edits from word1[:i] to word2[:j]\n        int[][] dp = new int[m + 1][n + 1];\n        // base cases: i letters to none is i deletes,\n        // none to j letters is j inserts\n        for (int i = 0; i <= m; i++) dp[i][0] = i;\n        for (int j = 0; j <= n; j++) dp[0][j] = j;\n        for (int i = 1; i <= m; i++) {\n            for (int j = 1; j <= n; j++) {\n                char a = word1.charAt(i - 1);\n                char b = word2.charAt(j - 1);\n                if (a == b) {\n                    // same last letter: it needs no edit\n                    dp[i][j] = dp[i - 1][j - 1];\n                } else {\n                    // one edit, then the cheapest of: delete\n                    // (up), insert (left), replace (up-left)\n                    dp[i][j] = 1 + Math.min(\n                        Math.min(dp[i - 1][j], dp[i][j - 1]),\n                        dp[i - 1][j - 1]);\n                }\n            }\n        }\n        return dp[m][n];\n    }\n}\n"},"problemSlug":"edit-distance"},{"name":"0/1 knapsack in one row","when":"Each number is used at most once and you need a target sum, or the best value within a capacity.","idea":"The table is dp[item][sum]. Keep one row and loop the sum from high to low, so can[s − x] is still the previous item's row. Looping low to high would let the same item count twice.","code":{"python":"def can_partition(nums: list[int]) -> bool:\n    total = sum(nums)\n    # an odd total can't split into two equal halves\n    if total % 2 == 1:\n        return False\n    target = total // 2\n    # state: dp[i][s] = some of the first i items sum to s.\n    # Row i only reads row i - 1, so keep a single row\n    can = [False] * (target + 1)\n    # base case: the empty choice sums to 0\n    can[0] = True\n    for x in nums:\n        # right to left: can[s - x] is still last row's value,\n        # so x is used at most once\n        for s in range(target, x - 1, -1):\n            if can[s - x]:\n                can[s] = True\n    return can[target]\n","cpp":"bool canPartition(const vector<int>& nums) {\n    int total = accumulate(nums.begin(), nums.end(), 0);\n    // an odd total can't split into two equal halves\n    if (total % 2 == 1) return false;\n    int target = total / 2;\n    // state: dp[i][s] = some of the first i items sum to s.\n    // Row i only reads row i - 1, so keep a single row\n    vector<bool> can(target + 1, false);\n    // base case: the empty choice sums to 0\n    can[0] = true;\n    for (int x : nums) {\n        // right to left: can[s - x] is still last row's value,\n        // so x is used at most once\n        for (int s = target; s >= x; s--) {\n            if (can[s - x]) can[s] = true;\n        }\n    }\n    return can[target];\n}\n","java":"class Solution {\n    public boolean canPartition(int[] nums) {\n        int total = 0;\n        for (int x : nums) total += x;\n        // an odd total can't split into two equal halves\n        if (total % 2 == 1) return false;\n        int target = total / 2;\n        // state: dp[i][s] = some of the first i items sum to s.\n        // Row i only reads row i - 1, so keep a single row\n        boolean[] can = new boolean[target + 1];\n        // base case: the empty choice sums to 0\n        can[0] = true;\n        for (int x : nums) {\n            // right to left: can[s - x] is still last row's\n            // value, so x is used at most once\n            for (int s = target; s >= x; s--) {\n                if (can[s - x]) can[s] = true;\n            }\n        }\n        return can[target];\n    }\n}\n"},"problemSlug":"partition-equal-subset-sum"}],"complexity":{"time":"O(m · n) cells, each filled in O(1); O(n · target) for knapsack","space":"O(m · n) for the full table, or O(n) with one row","why":"There is one cell per pair of indices, and each looks at a fixed number of neighbours (two or three). When a row reads only the row above, one row of storage is enough. For LCS and edit distance you can only keep one row if you don't need to rebuild the actual string afterwards."},"notThis":["The state is a single index, like a position in one array: that's 1D DP, with one array or two variables.","Moves can go in any direction on a grid, or cells have weights and you want the cheapest route: Dijkstra or BFS, because the grid has no fixed order to fill. See Shortest Path.","Items can be used any number of times and order doesn't matter: unbounded knapsack, which loops the sum low to high, or plain 1D DP over the amount (Coin Change).","You need every arrangement listed, not a count or a best: use backtracking."],"ladder":[{"slug":"unique-paths","tier":"warm-up","note":"Count grid paths: above + left, with the first row and column all 1."},{"slug":"unique-paths-ii","tier":"warm-up","note":"Obstacles set a cell to 0, including on the first row and column."},{"slug":"minimum-path-sum","tier":"warm-up","note":"Min instead of sum, then shrink the table to one row."},{"slug":"longest-common-subsequence","tier":"core","note":"The template for two strings: diagonal on a match, best neighbour otherwise."},{"slug":"edit-distance","tier":"core","note":"Three operations, and base cases that aren't 0."},{"slug":"partition-equal-subset-sum","tier":"core","note":"0/1 knapsack as true or false, one row, capacity looped downwards."},{"slug":"target-sum","tier":"core","note":"Turn the signs into a subset-sum count, then 0/1 knapsack that counts ways."},{"slug":"coin-change-ii","tier":"core","note":"Unbounded knapsack that counts combinations: coins in the outer loop, so order doesn't matter."},{"slug":"longest-palindromic-subsequence","tier":"core","note":"dp[i][j] over a range of one string, filled from short ranges to long ones."},{"slug":"maximal-square","tier":"core","note":"A cell's square is 1 + the min of its three neighbours' squares."},{"slug":"interleaving-string","tier":"core","note":"A boolean table over two prefixes: the next letter of s3 comes from s1 or from s2."},{"slug":"distinct-subsequences","tier":"stretch","note":"Count, not best: on a match, add the ways that use the letter and the ways that skip it."},{"slug":"longest-increasing-path-in-a-matrix","tier":"stretch","note":"No fixed fill order: memoised DFS, where each cell's answer depends on its larger neighbours."},{"slug":"regular-expression-matching","tier":"stretch","note":"Two strings with '*' meaning zero or more of the letter before it: the transition branches on the pattern."}]}$json$::jsonb),
('graphs-bfs',
  'Graph Theory',
  'Graph BFS / DFS',
  array['The input is a grid and the question is about connected regions (islands, rooms, areas) or moving from one cell to another.', 'The input is n nodes plus a list of edges, or an adjacency list, and asks what is reachable or how things are grouped.', 'The question asks for the fewest moves, steps or minutes, and every move costs the same.', 'Something spreads from several places at once (rot, fire, water) and you want the time until it reaches everywhere.']::text[],
  'A graph is nodes and edges. A grid is a graph too: each cell is a node, and its edges go to the up, down, left and right cells that are inside the grid and open. You rarely build this graph; you work out the neighbours when you need them.

Unlike a tree, a graph can have cycles, so the same node can be reached twice. Keep a visited set (or a boolean grid, or overwrite the cell) and never process a node that is already in it. With that rule, every node is handled once and every edge is looked at once or twice, so the whole search is O(V + E). On a grid that is O(rows × cols).

BFS uses a queue and explores in rings: first everything 1 step away, then everything 2 steps away, and so on. So the first time BFS reaches a node, it got there by a shortest path. Process the queue one level at a time and count the levels to get the distance. Mark a node visited when you add it to the queue, not when you take it out; otherwise the same cell can be added many times by different neighbours.

DFS goes as deep as it can before backing up, using recursion or a stack. It does not find shortest paths, but it is the shortest code for "visit this whole component", which is what counting islands needs. If there are several starting points, put them all in the queue at step 0. That multi-source BFS gives each cell its distance to the nearest source in one pass.

BFS breaks when edges have different costs. Then the path with fewer edges is not always the cheapest, and you need Dijkstra.',
  'def nearest_exit(maze: list[list[str]],
                 entrance: list[int]) -> int:
    rows, cols = len(maze), len(maze[0])
    seen = [[False] * cols for _ in range(rows)]
    r0, c0 = entrance
    queue = deque([(r0, c0)])
    # mark it seen when it joins the queue, not later
    seen[r0][c0] = True
    steps = 0
    while queue:
        # everything in the queue now is `steps` moves away
        for _ in range(len(queue)):
            r, c = queue.popleft()
            edge = r in (0, rows - 1) or c in (0, cols - 1)
            if edge and (r, c) != (r0, c0):
                return steps
            for dr, dc in ((-1, 0), (1, 0), (0, -1), (0, 1)):
                nr, nc = r + dr, c + dc
                if (0 <= nr < rows and 0 <= nc < cols
                        and maze[nr][nc] == "."
                        and not seen[nr][nc]):
                    seen[nr][nc] = True
                    queue.append((nr, nc))
        steps += 1
    return -1
',
  'Time O(V + E); O(rows × cols) on a grid · Space O(V) for the visited set and the queue or stack',
  array['Marking a cell visited when it leaves the queue. Mark it when it goes in, or one cell gets queued again by each of its neighbours.', 'Forgetting the visited set on a graph with cycles, so the search runs forever.', 'Using DFS for fewest steps. DFS finds a path, not the shortest one; use BFS.', 'Counting levels wrong: take exactly the cells that were in the queue when the level started (save the size first), then add one.', 'Running a BFS from each source when many cells spread at once. Put them all in the queue at the start instead.', 'Swapping rows and columns in the bounds check, or checking the bounds after reading grid[r][c].', 'Recursive DFS on a large grid in Python, which passes the recursion limit. Use an explicit stack there.']::text[],
  array['flood-fill', 'find-if-path-exists-in-graph', 'keys-and-rooms']::text[],
  16,
  $json${"version":1,"summary":"For anything made of things and connections between them: cities and roads, people and friendships, or the cells of a grid and their neighbours. Visit everything you can reach once, using a visited set so cycles don't trap you. BFS gives the fewest steps; DFS is the simplest way to explore a whole region.","prerequisites":["tree-bfs","tree-dfs"],"walkthrough":{"problemSlug":"nearest-exit-from-entrance-in-maze","input":"maze rows = \"++++++\", \"+....+\", \"+.++..\", \"++++++\"; entrance = [1, 1]","intro":"one step per cell taken from the queue, with the number of moves it took to reach it. Neighbours are tried up, down, left, right.","columns":["Cell taken (moves)","What happened","Queue after"],"steps":[{"window":"(1, 1) at 0","action":"the entrance, so not an exit; (2, 1) and (1, 2) are open: mark them seen and add them","best":"(2, 1), (1, 2)"},{"window":"(2, 1) at 1","action":"not on the edge; (1, 1) is seen and the rest are walls, so nothing is added","best":"(1, 2)"},{"window":"(1, 2) at 1","action":"not on the edge; add (1, 3)","best":"(1, 3)"},{"window":"(1, 3) at 2","action":"not on the edge; add (1, 4)","best":"(1, 4)"},{"window":"(1, 4) at 3","action":"not on the edge; (1, 5) is a wall, add (2, 4)","best":"(2, 4)"},{"window":"(2, 4) at 4","action":"not on the edge; add (2, 5)","best":"(2, 5)"},{"window":"(2, 5) at 5","action":"on the right edge and not the entrance: return 5","best":"(empty)"}],"result":"5. BFS tried the dead end at (2, 1) after one move and dropped it; no cell was added twice."},"template":{"note":"Queue the start and mark it seen. Then, one level at a time, take each cell out, stop if it's the goal, and add every open neighbour that isn't seen yet, marking it as you add it. The level count is the number of moves.","code":{"python":"def nearest_exit(maze: list[list[str]],\n                 entrance: list[int]) -> int:\n    rows, cols = len(maze), len(maze[0])\n    seen = [[False] * cols for _ in range(rows)]\n    r0, c0 = entrance\n    queue = deque([(r0, c0)])\n    # mark it seen when it joins the queue, not later\n    seen[r0][c0] = True\n    steps = 0\n    while queue:\n        # everything in the queue now is `steps` moves away\n        for _ in range(len(queue)):\n            r, c = queue.popleft()\n            edge = r in (0, rows - 1) or c in (0, cols - 1)\n            if edge and (r, c) != (r0, c0):\n                return steps\n            for dr, dc in ((-1, 0), (1, 0), (0, -1), (0, 1)):\n                nr, nc = r + dr, c + dc\n                if (0 <= nr < rows and 0 <= nc < cols\n                        and maze[nr][nc] == \".\"\n                        and not seen[nr][nc]):\n                    seen[nr][nc] = True\n                    queue.append((nr, nc))\n        steps += 1\n    return -1\n","cpp":"int nearestExit(vector<vector<char>>& maze,\n                vector<int>& entrance) {\n    int rows = maze.size(), cols = maze[0].size();\n    vector<vector<bool>> seen(rows, vector<bool>(cols, false));\n    int r0 = entrance[0], c0 = entrance[1];\n    queue<pair<int, int>> q;\n    q.push({r0, c0});\n    // mark it seen when it joins the queue, not later\n    seen[r0][c0] = true;\n    int dr[] = {-1, 1, 0, 0}, dc[] = {0, 0, -1, 1};\n    int steps = 0;\n    while (!q.empty()) {\n        // everything in the queue now is `steps` moves away\n        for (int k = q.size(); k > 0; k--) {\n            auto [r, c] = q.front();\n            q.pop();\n            bool edge = r == 0 || r == rows - 1\n                     || c == 0 || c == cols - 1;\n            if (edge && !(r == r0 && c == c0)) return steps;\n            for (int d = 0; d < 4; d++) {\n                int nr = r + dr[d], nc = c + dc[d];\n                if (nr >= 0 && nr < rows\n                        && nc >= 0 && nc < cols\n                        && maze[nr][nc] == '.'\n                        && !seen[nr][nc]) {\n                    seen[nr][nc] = true;\n                    q.push({nr, nc});\n                }\n            }\n        }\n        steps++;\n    }\n    return -1;\n}\n","java":"class Solution {\n    public int nearestExit(char[][] maze, int[] entrance) {\n        int rows = maze.length, cols = maze[0].length;\n        boolean[][] seen = new boolean[rows][cols];\n        int r0 = entrance[0], c0 = entrance[1];\n        Deque<int[]> queue = new ArrayDeque<>();\n        queue.add(new int[] {r0, c0});\n        // mark it seen when it joins the queue, not later\n        seen[r0][c0] = true;\n        int[][] dirs = {{-1, 0}, {1, 0}, {0, -1}, {0, 1}};\n        int steps = 0;\n        while (!queue.isEmpty()) {\n            // everything in the queue now is `steps` moves away\n            for (int k = queue.size(); k > 0; k--) {\n                int[] cell = queue.poll();\n                int r = cell[0], c = cell[1];\n                boolean edge = r == 0 || r == rows - 1\n                            || c == 0 || c == cols - 1;\n                if (edge && !(r == r0 && c == c0)) return steps;\n                for (int[] d : dirs) {\n                    int nr = r + d[0], nc = c + d[1];\n                    if (nr >= 0 && nr < rows\n                            && nc >= 0 && nc < cols\n                            && maze[nr][nc] == '.'\n                            && !seen[nr][nc]) {\n                        seen[nr][nc] = true;\n                        queue.add(new int[] {nr, nc});\n                    }\n                }\n            }\n            steps++;\n        }\n        return -1;\n    }\n}\n"}},"variants":[{"name":"DFS for connected components","when":"You need to count or measure regions, not find distances.","idea":"Scan every cell. Each unvisited land cell starts a new island: count it, then DFS from it and mark the whole island visited. Here the grid is the visited set: a visited cell is set to \"0\".","code":{"python":"def num_islands(grid: list[list[str]]) -> int:\n    rows, cols = len(grid), len(grid[0])\n\n    # sink the whole island that (r, c) belongs to\n    def dfs(r: int, c: int) -> None:\n        if r < 0 or r >= rows or c < 0 or c >= cols:\n            return\n        if grid[r][c] != \"1\":\n            return\n        # \"0\" now means water or already visited\n        grid[r][c] = \"0\"\n        dfs(r - 1, c)\n        dfs(r + 1, c)\n        dfs(r, c - 1)\n        dfs(r, c + 1)\n\n    count = 0\n    for r in range(rows):\n        for c in range(cols):\n            # land not sunk yet starts a new island\n            if grid[r][c] == \"1\":\n                count += 1\n                dfs(r, c)\n    return count\n","cpp":"// sink the whole island that (r, c) belongs to\nvoid dfs(vector<vector<char>>& grid, int r, int c) {\n    int rows = grid.size(), cols = grid[0].size();\n    if (r < 0 || r >= rows || c < 0 || c >= cols) return;\n    if (grid[r][c] != '1') return;\n    // '0' now means water or already visited\n    grid[r][c] = '0';\n    dfs(grid, r - 1, c);\n    dfs(grid, r + 1, c);\n    dfs(grid, r, c - 1);\n    dfs(grid, r, c + 1);\n}\n\nint numIslands(vector<vector<char>>& grid) {\n    int rows = grid.size(), cols = grid[0].size();\n    int count = 0;\n    for (int r = 0; r < rows; r++) {\n        for (int c = 0; c < cols; c++) {\n            // land not sunk yet starts a new island\n            if (grid[r][c] == '1') {\n                count++;\n                dfs(grid, r, c);\n            }\n        }\n    }\n    return count;\n}\n","java":"class Solution {\n    public int numIslands(char[][] grid) {\n        int count = 0;\n        for (int r = 0; r < grid.length; r++) {\n            for (int c = 0; c < grid[0].length; c++) {\n                // land not sunk yet starts a new island\n                if (grid[r][c] == '1') {\n                    count++;\n                    dfs(grid, r, c);\n                }\n            }\n        }\n        return count;\n    }\n\n    // sink the whole island that (r, c) belongs to\n    private void dfs(char[][] grid, int r, int c) {\n        if (r < 0 || r >= grid.length) return;\n        if (c < 0 || c >= grid[0].length) return;\n        if (grid[r][c] != '1') return;\n        // '0' now means water or already visited\n        grid[r][c] = '0';\n        dfs(grid, r - 1, c);\n        dfs(grid, r + 1, c);\n        dfs(grid, r, c - 1);\n        dfs(grid, r, c + 1);\n    }\n}\n"},"problemSlug":"number-of-islands"},{"name":"Multi-source BFS","when":"Something spreads from many starting cells at the same time.","idea":"Put every source in the queue before the loop, all at time 0, and count fresh cells. Each level of the BFS is one minute. If fresh cells remain when the queue is empty, some can never be reached, so return -1.","code":{"python":"def oranges_rotting(grid: list[list[int]]) -> int:\n    rows, cols = len(grid), len(grid[0])\n    queue: deque[tuple[int, int]] = deque()\n    fresh = 0\n    # every rotten orange starts in the queue at minute 0\n    for r in range(rows):\n        for c in range(cols):\n            if grid[r][c] == 2:\n                queue.append((r, c))\n            elif grid[r][c] == 1:\n                fresh += 1\n    minutes = 0\n    while queue and fresh > 0:\n        # one round of the loop is one minute\n        for _ in range(len(queue)):\n            r, c = queue.popleft()\n            for dr, dc in ((-1, 0), (1, 0), (0, -1), (0, 1)):\n                nr, nc = r + dr, c + dc\n                if (0 <= nr < rows and 0 <= nc < cols\n                        and grid[nr][nc] == 1):\n                    # rotting it also marks it as seen\n                    grid[nr][nc] = 2\n                    fresh -= 1\n                    queue.append((nr, nc))\n        minutes += 1\n    return minutes if fresh == 0 else -1\n","cpp":"int orangesRotting(vector<vector<int>>& grid) {\n    int rows = grid.size(), cols = grid[0].size();\n    queue<pair<int, int>> q;\n    int fresh = 0;\n    // every rotten orange starts in the queue at minute 0\n    for (int r = 0; r < rows; r++) {\n        for (int c = 0; c < cols; c++) {\n            if (grid[r][c] == 2) {\n                q.push({r, c});\n            } else if (grid[r][c] == 1) {\n                fresh++;\n            }\n        }\n    }\n    int dr[] = {-1, 1, 0, 0}, dc[] = {0, 0, -1, 1};\n    int minutes = 0;\n    while (!q.empty() && fresh > 0) {\n        // one round of the loop is one minute\n        for (int k = q.size(); k > 0; k--) {\n            auto [r, c] = q.front();\n            q.pop();\n            for (int d = 0; d < 4; d++) {\n                int nr = r + dr[d], nc = c + dc[d];\n                if (nr >= 0 && nr < rows\n                        && nc >= 0 && nc < cols\n                        && grid[nr][nc] == 1) {\n                    // rotting it also marks it as seen\n                    grid[nr][nc] = 2;\n                    fresh--;\n                    q.push({nr, nc});\n                }\n            }\n        }\n        minutes++;\n    }\n    return fresh == 0 ? minutes : -1;\n}\n","java":"class Solution {\n    public int orangesRotting(int[][] grid) {\n        int rows = grid.length, cols = grid[0].length;\n        Deque<int[]> queue = new ArrayDeque<>();\n        int fresh = 0;\n        // every rotten orange starts in the queue at minute 0\n        for (int r = 0; r < rows; r++) {\n            for (int c = 0; c < cols; c++) {\n                if (grid[r][c] == 2) {\n                    queue.add(new int[] {r, c});\n                } else if (grid[r][c] == 1) {\n                    fresh++;\n                }\n            }\n        }\n        int[][] dirs = {{-1, 0}, {1, 0}, {0, -1}, {0, 1}};\n        int minutes = 0;\n        while (!queue.isEmpty() && fresh > 0) {\n            // one round of the loop is one minute\n            for (int k = queue.size(); k > 0; k--) {\n                int[] cell = queue.poll();\n                int r = cell[0], c = cell[1];\n                for (int[] d : dirs) {\n                    int nr = r + d[0], nc = c + d[1];\n                    if (nr >= 0 && nr < rows\n                            && nc >= 0 && nc < cols\n                            && grid[nr][nc] == 1) {\n                        // rotting it also marks it as seen\n                        grid[nr][nc] = 2;\n                        fresh--;\n                        queue.add(new int[] {nr, nc});\n                    }\n                }\n            }\n            minutes++;\n        }\n        return fresh == 0 ? minutes : -1;\n    }\n}\n"},"problemSlug":"rotting-oranges"},{"name":"Edge list to adjacency list","when":"The graph comes as n and a list of edges.","idea":"Build graph[u] as a list of u's neighbours first, adding both directions for an undirected edge. Then search with a seen array of size n. A stack gives DFS, a queue gives BFS; for \"is it reachable\" either works.","code":{"python":"def valid_path(n: int, edges: list[list[int]],\n               source: int, destination: int) -> bool:\n    # adjacency list: graph[u] holds u's neighbours\n    graph: list[list[int]] = [[] for _ in range(n)]\n    for u, v in edges:\n        graph[u].append(v)\n        graph[v].append(u)\n    seen = [False] * n\n    seen[source] = True\n    # a stack makes this DFS; a queue would make it BFS\n    stack = [source]\n    while stack:\n        u = stack.pop()\n        if u == destination:\n            return True\n        for v in graph[u]:\n            if not seen[v]:\n                seen[v] = True\n                stack.append(v)\n    return False\n","cpp":"bool validPath(int n, vector<vector<int>>& edges,\n               int source, int destination) {\n    // adjacency list: graph[u] holds u's neighbours\n    vector<vector<int>> graph(n);\n    for (auto& e : edges) {\n        graph[e[0]].push_back(e[1]);\n        graph[e[1]].push_back(e[0]);\n    }\n    vector<bool> seen(n, false);\n    seen[source] = true;\n    // a stack makes this DFS; a queue would make it BFS\n    vector<int> stack = {source};\n    while (!stack.empty()) {\n        int u = stack.back();\n        stack.pop_back();\n        if (u == destination) return true;\n        for (int v : graph[u]) {\n            if (!seen[v]) {\n                seen[v] = true;\n                stack.push_back(v);\n            }\n        }\n    }\n    return false;\n}\n","java":"class Solution {\n    public boolean validPath(int n, int[][] edges,\n                             int source, int destination) {\n        // adjacency list: graph.get(u) holds u's neighbours\n        List<List<Integer>> graph = new ArrayList<>();\n        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {\n            graph.add(new ArrayList<>());\n        }\n        for (int[] e : edges) {\n            graph.get(e[0]).add(e[1]);\n            graph.get(e[1]).add(e[0]);\n        }\n        boolean[] seen = new boolean[n];\n        seen[source] = true;\n        // a stack makes this DFS; a queue would make it BFS\n        Deque<Integer> stack = new ArrayDeque<>();\n        stack.push(source);\n        while (!stack.isEmpty()) {\n            int u = stack.pop();\n            if (u == destination) return true;\n            for (int v : graph.get(u)) {\n                if (!seen[v]) {\n                    seen[v] = true;\n                    stack.push(v);\n                }\n            }\n        }\n        return false;\n    }\n}\n"},"problemSlug":"find-if-path-exists-in-graph"}],"complexity":{"time":"O(V + E); O(rows × cols) on a grid","space":"O(V) for the visited set and the queue or stack","why":"The visited check means each node enters the queue or the call stack at most once, and each edge is looked at once from each end. A grid cell has at most 4 edges, so the edges add only a constant factor. The queue can hold a whole level at once, and a recursive DFS can go as deep as the number of cells."},"notThis":["Edges have different weights or costs: the fewest edges isn't the cheapest path. Use Dijkstra. See Shortest Path.","Edges arrive one at a time and you keep asking whether two nodes are connected: use Union-Find.","The graph is directed and you need an order where every node comes after its prerequisites: see Topological Sort.","You need every path, not just whether one exists: that is backtracking, and the visited mark is undone on the way back. See Backtracking."],"ladder":[{"slug":"flood-fill","tier":"warm-up","note":"One DFS from one cell: recolour the region it belongs to."},{"slug":"find-if-path-exists-in-graph","tier":"warm-up","note":"Build the adjacency list from edges, then search from the source."},{"slug":"keys-and-rooms","tier":"warm-up","note":"The adjacency list is given; check that one search visits every room."},{"slug":"number-of-islands","tier":"core","note":"Count components: every fresh DFS start is a new island."},{"slug":"max-area-of-island","tier":"core","note":"The same DFS, returning the size of each island."},{"slug":"nearest-exit-from-entrance-in-maze","tier":"core","note":"The template: BFS by levels for the fewest moves to any edge cell."},{"slug":"rotting-oranges","tier":"core","note":"Multi-source BFS: every rotten orange starts at minute 0."},{"slug":"01-matrix","tier":"core","note":"Multi-source BFS from every 0 gives each cell its distance to the nearest 0."},{"slug":"surrounded-regions","tier":"core","note":"Flip the question: search from the border, then capture whatever wasn't reached."},{"slug":"pacific-atlantic-water-flow","tier":"core","note":"Search uphill from each ocean's edge; the answer is the cells both searches reach."},{"slug":"clone-graph","tier":"core","note":"The visited map stores each node's copy, so cycles reuse it."},{"slug":"word-ladder","tier":"stretch","note":"The nodes are words and the edges are one-letter changes: BFS on a graph you never build."},{"slug":"shortest-path-in-a-grid-with-obstacles-elimination","tier":"stretch","note":"The state is (row, col, removals left), so visited must track all three."}]}$json$::jsonb),
('greedy',
  'Greedy',
  'Greedy',
  array['The question asks for the minimum or maximum count or cost, and choices are made one after another.', 'Sorting by one key (end time, deadline, difference in cost) seems to make the decision obvious at each step.', 'You only ever need one running number to decide the next step: the farthest index reachable, the gas left, the last end kept.', 'A DP over states would work but looks like overkill, because one choice clearly dominates the others.', 'You can argue: whatever the best answer does first, doing the greedy thing first instead is no worse.']::text[],
  'A greedy algorithm makes one choice at a time by a fixed rule and never takes it back. That is only correct if each choice is safe: some best answer agrees with it. So every greedy solution needs a reason, not just a feeling that the rule is sensible.

The usual reason is an exchange argument. Take any optimal answer. Find the first place it differs from the greedy choice, and swap the greedy choice in. Show the result is still valid and no worse. Then some optimal answer starts with the greedy choice, and the same argument repeats on what''s left of the problem.

Interval scheduling shows it. To keep as many non-overlapping intervals as possible, sort by end time and keep every interval that starts at or after the last kept end. Exchange: let g be the interval that ends first, and let o be the first interval in some optimal answer. g ends no later than o, so g overlaps nothing that o didn''t overlap. Swapping o for g gives an answer of the same size. So keeping g is safe. Remove everything that overlaps g and repeat.

The proof also tells you which key to sort by. Sorting by start fails: [1,100] starts first and blocks [2,3] and [4,5]. Sorting by length fails too: [4,6] is shortest but can block both [1,5] and [5,10]. Before you code a rule, try to break it with a two or three item example. If you can''t find a proof or a safe swap, the problem probably needs DP.

The cost is usually the sort, O(n log n), followed by an O(n) pass. That speed is why greedy is worth the proof: when the rule is safe, it beats trying every option.',
  'def erase_overlap_intervals(intervals: list[list[int]]) -> int:
    # earliest end first: it leaves the most room after it
    intervals.sort(key=lambda iv: iv[1])
    removed = 0
    end = float("-inf")
    for start, finish in intervals:
        if start >= end:
            # fits after the last kept interval: keep it
            end = finish
        else:
            # clashes with one that ends sooner: drop it
            removed += 1
    return removed
',
  'Time O(n log n) with a sort, O(n) without · Space O(1) beyond the sort',
  array['Coding a rule that feels right without trying to break it. Test two or three small cases against brute force first.', 'Sorting by the wrong key: start instead of end, length instead of end, cost instead of cost difference.', 'Getting the touch case wrong. In non-overlapping intervals, [1,2] and [2,3] don''t overlap, so the test is start ≥ end.', 'Using greedy on 0/1 knapsack or coin change with arbitrary coins. Taking the best ratio or the biggest coin first can be wrong there; use DP.', 'In Jump Game II, counting a jump at every index, or looping to the last index and adding a jump you don''t need.', 'In Gas Station, resetting the start without keeping the separate total that says whether any start works.', 'Starting the last kept end at 0 when intervals can have negative starts. Start it at minus infinity.']::text[],
  array['assign-cookies', 'lemonade-change', 'maximum-units-on-a-truck']::text[],
  11,
  $json${"version":1,"summary":"For optimisation problems where one local rule, applied again and again, gives the best overall answer: earliest end first, farthest reach, cheapest relative cost. The work is in proving the rule is safe; the code is usually a sort and one pass.","prerequisites":["intervals"],"walkthrough":{"problemSlug":"non-overlapping-intervals","input":"intervals = [[1,10],[2,3],[4,5],[6,7]]","intro":"one step per interval, after sorting by end.","columns":["Kept so far","What happened","Removed"],"steps":[{"window":"(none), end = −∞","action":"sort by end: [2,3] [4,5] [6,7] [1,10]","best":"0"},{"window":"[2,3], end = 3","action":"2 ≥ −∞: keep [2,3]","best":"0"},{"window":"[2,3] [4,5], end = 5","action":"4 ≥ 3: keep [4,5]","best":"0"},{"window":"[2,3] [4,5] [6,7], end = 7","action":"6 ≥ 5: keep [6,7]","best":"0"},{"window":"[2,3] [4,5] [6,7], end = 7","action":"1 < 7: [1,10] clashes, drop it","best":"1"}],"result":"1. Sorting by start would have kept [1,10] first and removed the other three."},"template":{"note":"Sort by the key your exchange argument picked, then make the choice in one pass with a single running value (here, the last kept end). The comment at each branch is the proof in one line.","code":{"python":"def erase_overlap_intervals(intervals: list[list[int]]) -> int:\n    # earliest end first: it leaves the most room after it\n    intervals.sort(key=lambda iv: iv[1])\n    removed = 0\n    end = float(\"-inf\")\n    for start, finish in intervals:\n        if start >= end:\n            # fits after the last kept interval: keep it\n            end = finish\n        else:\n            # clashes with one that ends sooner: drop it\n            removed += 1\n    return removed\n","cpp":"int eraseOverlapIntervals(vector<vector<int>> intervals) {\n    // earliest end first: it leaves the most room after it\n    sort(intervals.begin(), intervals.end(),\n         [](const vector<int>& a, const vector<int>& b) {\n             return a[1] < b[1];\n         });\n    int removed = 0;\n    int end = INT_MIN;\n    for (const auto& iv : intervals) {\n        int start = iv[0], finish = iv[1];\n        if (start >= end) {\n            // fits after the last kept interval: keep it\n            end = finish;\n        } else {\n            // clashes with one that ends sooner: drop it\n            ++removed;\n        }\n    }\n    return removed;\n}\n","java":"class Solution {\n    public int eraseOverlapIntervals(int[][] intervals) {\n        // earliest end first: it leaves the most room after it\n        Arrays.sort(intervals,\n            (a, b) -> Integer.compare(a[1], b[1]));\n        int removed = 0;\n        int end = Integer.MIN_VALUE;\n        for (int[] iv : intervals) {\n            int start = iv[0], finish = iv[1];\n            if (start >= end) {\n                // fits after the last kept interval: keep it\n                end = finish;\n            } else {\n                // clashes with one that ends sooner: drop it\n                removed++;\n            }\n        }\n        return removed;\n    }\n}\n"}},"variants":[{"name":"Farthest reach","when":"Each position lets you move up to some distance, and you want the fewest moves or whether the end is reachable.","idea":"Track the farthest index any move so far could reach. A jump only has to be counted when you walk past the edge of the current jump's reach. It's BFS by levels without a queue: every index up to end is reachable in jumps moves.","code":{"python":"def jump(nums: list[int]) -> int:\n    jumps = 0\n    # this many jumps reach up to index end\n    end = 0\n    # one more jump could reach up to farthest\n    farthest = 0\n    for i in range(len(nums) - 1):\n        farthest = max(farthest, i + nums[i])\n        # at the edge of this jump's reach: take another\n        if i == end:\n            jumps += 1\n            end = farthest\n    return jumps\n","cpp":"int jump(const vector<int>& nums) {\n    int jumps = 0;\n    // this many jumps reach up to index end\n    int end = 0;\n    // one more jump could reach up to farthest\n    int farthest = 0;\n    for (int i = 0; i + 1 < (int)nums.size(); ++i) {\n        farthest = max(farthest, i + nums[i]);\n        // at the edge of this jump's reach: take another\n        if (i == end) {\n            ++jumps;\n            end = farthest;\n        }\n    }\n    return jumps;\n}\n","java":"class Solution {\n    public int jump(int[] nums) {\n        int jumps = 0;\n        // this many jumps reach up to index end\n        int end = 0;\n        // one more jump could reach up to farthest\n        int farthest = 0;\n        for (int i = 0; i + 1 < nums.length; i++) {\n            farthest = Math.max(farthest, i + nums[i]);\n            // at the edge of this jump's reach: take another\n            if (i == end) {\n                jumps++;\n                end = farthest;\n            }\n        }\n        return jumps;\n    }\n}\n"},"problemSlug":"jump-game-ii"},{"name":"Reset when the running total fails","when":"A circular or linear run where a running balance must never go negative.","idea":"If the tank goes negative at i, then no start between the current start and i can work. Starting later only drops a prefix whose balance was never negative, which can only hurt. Jump the start to i + 1. A separate total decides whether any start works at all.","code":{"python":"def can_complete_circuit(\n    gas: list[int], cost: list[int]\n) -> int:\n    total = tank = start = 0\n    for i in range(len(gas)):\n        total += gas[i] - cost[i]\n        tank += gas[i] - cost[i]\n        # stuck before i + 1: no start from start..i works\n        if tank < 0:\n            start = i + 1\n            tank = 0\n    # enough gas overall means the last start survives\n    return start if total >= 0 else -1\n","cpp":"int canCompleteCircuit(const vector<int>& gas,\n                       const vector<int>& cost) {\n    int total = 0, tank = 0, start = 0;\n    for (int i = 0; i < (int)gas.size(); ++i) {\n        total += gas[i] - cost[i];\n        tank += gas[i] - cost[i];\n        // stuck before i + 1: no start from start..i works\n        if (tank < 0) {\n            start = i + 1;\n            tank = 0;\n        }\n    }\n    // enough gas overall means the last start survives\n    return total >= 0 ? start : -1;\n}\n","java":"class Solution {\n    public int canCompleteCircuit(int[] gas, int[] cost) {\n        int total = 0, tank = 0, start = 0;\n        for (int i = 0; i < gas.length; i++) {\n            total += gas[i] - cost[i];\n            tank += gas[i] - cost[i];\n            // stuck before i + 1: no start from start..i works\n            if (tank < 0) {\n                start = i + 1;\n                tank = 0;\n            }\n        }\n        // enough gas overall means the last start survives\n        return total >= 0 ? start : -1;\n    }\n}\n"},"problemSlug":"gas-station"},{"name":"Sort by a key you derive","when":"Each item has two options, and a fixed number must take each one.","idea":"Swap two people and compare costs. Sending x to A and y to B beats the reverse exactly when x's A-minus-B cost is lower than y's. So sort by A − B and send the first half to A. The exchange argument picks the key.","code":{"python":"def two_city_sched_cost(costs: list[list[int]]) -> int:\n    # most money saved by flying to A, relative to B, first\n    costs.sort(key=lambda c: c[0] - c[1])\n    n = len(costs) // 2\n    total = 0\n    for i, (a, b) in enumerate(costs):\n        # first half flies to A, second half to B\n        total += a if i < n else b\n    return total\n","cpp":"int twoCitySchedCost(vector<vector<int>> costs) {\n    // most money saved by flying to A, relative to B, first\n    sort(costs.begin(), costs.end(),\n         [](const vector<int>& x, const vector<int>& y) {\n             return x[0] - x[1] < y[0] - y[1];\n         });\n    int n = costs.size() / 2;\n    int total = 0;\n    for (int i = 0; i < (int)costs.size(); ++i) {\n        // first half flies to A, second half to B\n        total += i < n ? costs[i][0] : costs[i][1];\n    }\n    return total;\n}\n","java":"class Solution {\n    public int twoCitySchedCost(int[][] costs) {\n        // most money saved by flying to A, relative to B, first\n        Arrays.sort(costs, (x, y) ->\n            Integer.compare(x[0] - x[1], y[0] - y[1]));\n        int n = costs.length / 2;\n        int total = 0;\n        for (int i = 0; i < costs.length; i++) {\n            // first half flies to A, second half to B\n            total += i < n ? costs[i][0] : costs[i][1];\n        }\n        return total;\n    }\n}\n"},"problemSlug":"two-city-scheduling"}],"complexity":{"time":"O(n log n) with a sort, O(n) without","space":"O(1) beyond the sort","why":"The algorithm commits to each item once and never revisits a choice, so after sorting it's a single pass. That's the whole advantage over DP, which keeps every partial answer. Jump Game II and Gas Station need no sort: they are one pass with a couple of running values."},"notThis":["Choices interact so that a locally worse step can pay off later (coin change with coins 1, 3, 4 and amount 6): use DP.","You need every valid arrangement, not the best one: use backtracking.","Merging or counting overlaps rather than choosing which to keep: see Intervals.","The best item changes as items are added and removed: keep a heap and make the greedy choice from it. See Heap / Top-K."],"ladder":[{"slug":"assign-cookies","tier":"warm-up","note":"Sort both sides; give each child the smallest cookie that's enough."},{"slug":"lemonade-change","tier":"warm-up","note":"Give change with the bigger bill first and save the 5s, which fit more cases."},{"slug":"maximum-units-on-a-truck","tier":"warm-up","note":"Take the boxes worth the most per slot first."},{"slug":"non-overlapping-intervals","tier":"core","note":"The template: earliest end first, proven by the exchange argument."},{"slug":"minimum-number-of-arrows-to-burst-balloons","tier":"core","note":"The same sort by end; one arrow at each kept end."},{"slug":"jump-game","tier":"core","note":"Track the farthest index reachable; fail if you stand beyond it."},{"slug":"jump-game-ii","tier":"core","note":"Count jumps as levels of reach, like BFS without a queue."},{"slug":"gas-station","tier":"core","note":"Reset the start when the tank goes negative; the total decides if any start works."},{"slug":"partition-labels","tier":"core","note":"Stretch the current part to the last occurrence of every letter in it."},{"slug":"two-city-scheduling","tier":"core","note":"Sort by a key you derive from a swap: A cost minus B cost."},{"slug":"boats-to-save-people","tier":"core","note":"The heaviest person goes now; pair them with the lightest if they fit."},{"slug":"candy","tier":"stretch","note":"Two greedy passes, one per direction, then take the max of both."},{"slug":"minimum-number-of-taps-to-open-to-water-a-garden","tier":"stretch","note":"Jump Game II in disguise: turn each tap into a reach."},{"slug":"patching-array","tier":"stretch","note":"Track the range of sums you can already make; patch with the smallest missing one."}]}$json$::jsonb),
('heap-top-k',
  'Heap (Priority Queue)',
  'Heap / Top-K',
  array['The question says k: kth largest, top k frequent, k closest, k smallest pairs.', 'You repeatedly need the current smallest or largest item while items keep being added.', 'Several sorted lists or streams must be combined in order.', 'Sorting everything would work, but k is much smaller than n, or the data arrives as a stream.']::text[],
  'A heap is a tree kept in an array where every parent is smaller than its children (a min-heap). The smallest item is always at the root. Push and pop both cost O(log size), and reading the root is O(1). Nothing else is ordered, so it''s cheaper to keep than a sorted list.

For the k largest items, keep a min-heap of at most k items. Push each new item; if the heap grows to k + 1, pop the root. The invariant: after each step, the heap holds the k largest items seen so far. The root is the smallest of them, which is exactly the kth largest. It flips for the k smallest: keep a max-heap and evict the largest.

Why it''s correct: the popped item is smaller than k others already seen, so it can never be among the k largest. Why it''s fast: the heap never holds more than k + 1 items, so each step is O(log k), and the whole pass is O(n log k) with O(k) memory. That beats sorting when k is small, and it works on a stream you can''t store.

The same tool merges k sorted lists. Put the first item of each list in a min-heap. Pop the smallest, then push the next item from the list it came from. The heap only ever holds one item per list.

It''s the wrong tool when you need the items in a range, or the median of a window that slides (you can''t delete from the middle of a heap cheaply). If you need all of the top k sorted, pop them at the end.',
  'def find_kth_largest(nums: list[int], k: int) -> int:
    # min-heap of the k largest values seen so far
    heap = []
    for x in nums:
        heapq.heappush(heap, x)
        # one too many: the smallest can''t be in the top k
        if len(heap) > k:
            heapq.heappop(heap)
    # the smallest of the k largest is the kth largest
    return heap[0]
',
  'Time O(n log k) · Space O(k)',
  array['Using a max-heap for the k largest. Then the root is the biggest, and popping it throws away the answer.', 'Letting the heap grow to n and popping k times at the end. It''s correct but O(n log n), not O(n log k).', 'Forgetting that Python''s heapq is a min-heap only. For a max-heap, push −value and negate again when you read it.', 'Pushing objects that can''t be compared. In Python, put a tiebreaker such as an index before the node in the tuple.', 'Comparing with a − b in a Java comparator. It overflows on large values; use Integer.compare.', 'Expecting the heap''s array to be sorted. Only the root is guaranteed; pop repeatedly to get sorted order.', 'Getting the tie order wrong when the problem specifies one (top k frequent words breaks ties alphabetically).']::text[],
  array['last-stone-weight', 'kth-largest-element-in-a-stream', 'kth-largest-element-in-an-array']::text[],
  10,
  $json${"version":1,"summary":"For questions about the k largest, smallest, most frequent or closest items, or the smallest of several sorted streams. A heap capped at k keeps the best k seen so far, so you never sort everything.","prerequisites":["arrays-hashing"],"walkthrough":{"problemSlug":"kth-largest-element-in-an-array","input":"nums = [3,2,1,5,6,4], k = 2","intro":"one step per number pushed.","columns":["Heap (smallest first)","What happened","kth largest so far"],"steps":[{"window":"{3}","action":"push 3","best":"fewer than k items"},{"window":"{2, 3}","action":"push 2","best":"2"},{"window":"{2, 3}","action":"push 1 → 3 items; pop the root 1","best":"2"},{"window":"{3, 5}","action":"push 5 → 3 items; pop the root 2","best":"3"},{"window":"{5, 6}","action":"push 6 → 3 items; pop the root 3","best":"5"},{"window":"{5, 6}","action":"push 4 → 3 items; pop the root 4","best":"5"}],"result":"5, the root of the heap holding the two largest values {5, 6}"},"template":{"note":"Push, then pop if the heap holds more than k. Use a min-heap for the k largest and a max-heap for the k smallest: the root is always the one about to be evicted.","code":{"python":"def find_kth_largest(nums: list[int], k: int) -> int:\n    # min-heap of the k largest values seen so far\n    heap = []\n    for x in nums:\n        heapq.heappush(heap, x)\n        # one too many: the smallest can't be in the top k\n        if len(heap) > k:\n            heapq.heappop(heap)\n    # the smallest of the k largest is the kth largest\n    return heap[0]\n","cpp":"int findKthLargest(const vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n    // min-heap of the k largest values seen so far\n    priority_queue<int, vector<int>, greater<int>> heap;\n    for (int x : nums) {\n        heap.push(x);\n        // one too many: the smallest can't be in the top k\n        if ((int)heap.size() > k) heap.pop();\n    }\n    // the smallest of the k largest is the kth largest\n    return heap.top();\n}\n","java":"class Solution {\n    public int findKthLargest(int[] nums, int k) {\n        // min-heap of the k largest values seen so far\n        PriorityQueue<Integer> heap = new PriorityQueue<>();\n        for (int x : nums) {\n            heap.offer(x);\n            // one too many: the smallest can't be in the top k\n            if (heap.size() > k) heap.poll();\n        }\n        // the smallest of the k largest is the kth largest\n        return heap.peek();\n    }\n}\n"}},"variants":[{"name":"Top k by a score","when":"You rank items by something you compute first: a frequency, a count, a cost.","idea":"Count with a hash map, then run the template on (score, item) pairs. The heap compares the score first, so the item only breaks ties.","code":{"python":"def top_k_frequent(nums: list[int], k: int) -> list[int]:\n    # how often each value appears\n    count = {}\n    for x in nums:\n        count[x] = count.get(x, 0) + 1\n    # min-heap of (frequency, value), at most k entries\n    heap = []\n    for x, c in count.items():\n        heapq.heappush(heap, (c, x))\n        # drop the least frequent of the k + 1\n        if len(heap) > k:\n            heapq.heappop(heap)\n    return [x for c, x in heap]\n","cpp":"vector<int> topKFrequent(const vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n    // how often each value appears\n    unordered_map<int, int> count;\n    for (int x : nums) ++count[x];\n    // min-heap of (frequency, value), at most k entries\n    priority_queue<pair<int, int>, vector<pair<int, int>>,\n                   greater<pair<int, int>>> heap;\n    for (const auto& [x, c] : count) {\n        heap.push({c, x});\n        // drop the least frequent of the k + 1\n        if ((int)heap.size() > k) heap.pop();\n    }\n    vector<int> out;\n    while (!heap.empty()) {\n        out.push_back(heap.top().second);\n        heap.pop();\n    }\n    return out;\n}\n","java":"class Solution {\n    public int[] topKFrequent(int[] nums, int k) {\n        // how often each value appears\n        Map<Integer, Integer> count = new HashMap<>();\n        for (int x : nums) count.merge(x, 1, Integer::sum);\n        // min-heap of (frequency, value), at most k entries\n        PriorityQueue<int[]> heap = new PriorityQueue<>(\n            (a, b) -> a[0] != b[0]\n                ? Integer.compare(a[0], b[0])\n                : Integer.compare(a[1], b[1]));\n        for (Map.Entry<Integer, Integer> e : count.entrySet()) {\n            heap.offer(new int[]{e.getValue(), e.getKey()});\n            // drop the least frequent of the k + 1\n            if (heap.size() > k) heap.poll();\n        }\n        int[] out = new int[heap.size()];\n        for (int i = 0; i < out.length; i++) {\n            out[i] = heap.poll()[1];\n        }\n        return out;\n    }\n}\n"},"problemSlug":"top-k-frequent-elements"},{"name":"k smallest: flip to a max-heap","when":"You want the k closest, cheapest or smallest items.","idea":"Keep a max-heap of size k so the worst of the current k sits on top and is the one evicted. In Python, heapq is min-only, so push the negated distance.","code":{"python":"def k_closest(\n    points: list[list[int]], k: int\n) -> list[list[int]]:\n    # max-heap of (distance, index): the k closest so far\n    heap = []\n    for i, (x, y) in enumerate(points):\n        d = x * x + y * y\n        heapq.heappush(heap, (-d, i))\n        # one too many: drop the farthest\n        if len(heap) > k:\n            heapq.heappop(heap)\n    return [points[i] for _, i in heap]\n","cpp":"vector<vector<int>> kClosest(const vector<vector<int>>& points,\n                             int k) {\n    // max-heap of (distance, index): the k closest so far\n    priority_queue<pair<int, int>> heap;\n    for (int i = 0; i < (int)points.size(); ++i) {\n        int x = points[i][0], y = points[i][1];\n        int d = x * x + y * y;\n        heap.push({d, i});\n        // one too many: drop the farthest\n        if ((int)heap.size() > k) heap.pop();\n    }\n    vector<vector<int>> out;\n    while (!heap.empty()) {\n        out.push_back(points[heap.top().second]);\n        heap.pop();\n    }\n    return out;\n}\n","java":"class Solution {\n    public int[][] kClosest(int[][] points, int k) {\n        // max-heap of (distance, index): the k closest so far\n        PriorityQueue<int[]> heap = new PriorityQueue<>(\n            (a, b) -> Integer.compare(b[0], a[0]));\n        for (int i = 0; i < points.length; i++) {\n            int x = points[i][0], y = points[i][1];\n            int d = x * x + y * y;\n            heap.offer(new int[]{d, i});\n            // one too many: drop the farthest\n            if (heap.size() > k) heap.poll();\n        }\n        int[][] out = new int[heap.size()][];\n        for (int j = 0; j < out.length; j++) {\n            out[j] = points[heap.poll()[1]];\n        }\n        return out;\n    }\n}\n"},"problemSlug":"k-closest-points-to-origin"},{"name":"K-way merge","when":"k lists are each sorted and you need them merged, or the kth smallest across them.","idea":"Seed a min-heap with the head of each list, as (value, list index). Pop the smallest, append it, and push the next node from the same list. The heap never holds more than k entries.","code":{"python":"def merge_k_lists(\n    lists: list[Optional[ListNode]],\n) -> Optional[ListNode]:\n    # (value, list index): one entry per non-empty list\n    heap = []\n    for i, node in enumerate(lists):\n        if node:\n            heapq.heappush(heap, (node.val, i))\n    dummy = ListNode()\n    tail = dummy\n    while heap:\n        # the smallest head among all the lists\n        _, i = heapq.heappop(heap)\n        tail.next = lists[i]\n        tail = tail.next\n        # that list's next node takes its place\n        lists[i] = lists[i].next\n        if lists[i]:\n            heapq.heappush(heap, (lists[i].val, i))\n    return dummy.next\n","cpp":"ListNode* mergeKLists(vector<ListNode*>& lists) {\n    // (value, list index): one entry per non-empty list\n    priority_queue<pair<int, int>, vector<pair<int, int>>,\n                   greater<pair<int, int>>> heap;\n    for (int i = 0; i < (int)lists.size(); ++i) {\n        if (lists[i]) heap.push({lists[i]->val, i});\n    }\n    ListNode dummy;\n    ListNode* tail = &dummy;\n    while (!heap.empty()) {\n        // the smallest head among all the lists\n        int i = heap.top().second;\n        heap.pop();\n        tail->next = lists[i];\n        tail = tail->next;\n        // that list's next node takes its place\n        lists[i] = lists[i]->next;\n        if (lists[i]) heap.push({lists[i]->val, i});\n    }\n    return dummy.next;\n}\n","java":"class Solution {\n    public ListNode mergeKLists(ListNode[] lists) {\n        // (value, list index): one entry per non-empty list\n        PriorityQueue<int[]> heap = new PriorityQueue<>(\n            (a, b) -> a[0] != b[0]\n                ? Integer.compare(a[0], b[0])\n                : Integer.compare(a[1], b[1]));\n        for (int i = 0; i < lists.length; i++) {\n            if (lists[i] != null) {\n                heap.offer(new int[]{lists[i].val, i});\n            }\n        }\n        ListNode dummy = new ListNode();\n        ListNode tail = dummy;\n        while (!heap.isEmpty()) {\n            // the smallest head among all the lists\n            int i = heap.poll()[1];\n            tail.next = lists[i];\n            tail = tail.next;\n            // that list's next node takes its place\n            lists[i] = lists[i].next;\n            if (lists[i] != null) {\n                heap.offer(new int[]{lists[i].val, i});\n            }\n        }\n        return dummy.next;\n    }\n}\n"},"problemSlug":"merge-k-sorted-lists"}],"complexity":{"time":"O(n log k)","space":"O(k)","why":"Each of the n items is pushed once and popped at most once, and the heap never holds more than k + 1 items, so every operation is O(log k). The k-way merge is the same: N total nodes, each pushed and popped once, in a heap of at most k entries. The top-k-frequent variant adds an O(n) counting pass and O(d) map space for d distinct values."},"notThis":["You need the kth largest once and can modify the array: quickselect is O(n) on average. Mention it, then use the heap if worst-case time matters.","The max or min of a sliding window of fixed size: a monotonic deque is O(n). See Sliding Window.","Values are small integers or frequencies are bounded by n: bucket sort by count gives top k in O(n).","You need to remove arbitrary items or query ranges: use a balanced tree or a sorted container instead."],"ladder":[{"slug":"last-stone-weight","tier":"warm-up","note":"A max-heap you pop twice and push once. Get used to the API."},{"slug":"kth-largest-element-in-a-stream","tier":"warm-up","note":"The template kept alive between calls: a min-heap capped at k."},{"slug":"kth-largest-element-in-an-array","tier":"core","note":"The template on a fixed array. Compare it with sorting and quickselect."},{"slug":"top-k-frequent-elements","tier":"core","note":"Count first, then keep the k most frequent."},{"slug":"k-closest-points-to-origin","tier":"core","note":"k smallest: flip to a max-heap so the worst is on top."},{"slug":"sort-characters-by-frequency","tier":"core","note":"Pop everything by frequency: the heap as a sorter."},{"slug":"top-k-frequent-words","tier":"core","note":"Ties matter: frequency first, then alphabetical, and the comparator must agree."},{"slug":"kth-smallest-element-in-a-sorted-matrix","tier":"core","note":"K-way merge over the rows, stopping at the kth pop."},{"slug":"find-k-pairs-with-smallest-sums","tier":"core","note":"Lazy expansion: push a pair's neighbour only when that pair is popped."},{"slug":"reorganize-string","tier":"core","note":"Greedy with a heap: place the most common letter that isn't the one you just placed."},{"slug":"merge-k-sorted-lists","tier":"stretch","note":"K-way merge on linked lists: one head per list in the heap."},{"slug":"find-median-from-data-stream","tier":"stretch","note":"Two heaps: a max-heap for the lower half, a min-heap for the upper half, kept balanced."},{"slug":"ipo","tier":"stretch","note":"Two heaps over time: unlock projects by capital, then take the most profitable one."}]}$json$::jsonb),
('intervals',
  'Sorting',
  'Intervals',
  array['The input is a list of [start, end] pairs: meetings, bookings, balloons, trips.', 'The question says overlap, merge, conflict, free time, or how many at once.', 'Comparing every pair would work, but n is large enough that O(n²) is too slow.', 'The answer depends on the order of events in time, not on the order the intervals were given in.']::text[],
  'Two intervals [a, b] and [c, d] overlap when each one starts before the other ends: a ≤ d and c ≤ b. Checking that for every pair is O(n²). Sorting by start removes most of the pairs from consideration.

After sorting by start, keep the merged blocks in a list. For each new interval, only the last block can overlap it. Every earlier block ended before the last block began, and the new interval starts even later. So one comparison per interval is enough: if it starts after the last block''s end, it begins a new block; otherwise it joins the last block, whose end becomes the larger of the two ends.

That''s why the sort is the whole cost: O(n log n) to sort, then O(n) for the pass. If the input is already sorted, as in insert interval, you skip the sort and the pass alone is O(n).

For "how many overlap at once", stop thinking about intervals and think about events. Each interval adds +1 at its start and −1 when it ends. Sort the events by time and keep a running total; its peak is the answer. That is the sweep line. Ties matter: decide whether an interval ending at time t and another starting at t overlap, and order the events to match.

Sorting by start doesn''t fit every interval question. To keep as many non-overlapping intervals as possible, sort by end instead. That''s a greedy choice with its own proof (see Greedy).',
  'def merge(intervals: list[list[int]]) -> list[list[int]]:
    # sort by start: overlaps end up side by side
    intervals.sort(key=lambda iv: iv[0])
    merged = []
    for start, end in intervals:
        # starts after the last block ends: a new block
        if not merged or start > merged[-1][1]:
            merged.append([start, end])
        else:
            # overlaps: grow the end, never shrink it
            merged[-1][1] = max(merged[-1][1], end)
    return merged
',
  'Time O(n log n), or O(n) if the input is already sorted · Space O(n) for the output or the event list',
  array['Setting the merged end to the new interval''s end instead of the max of both. [1,10] then [2,3] must stay [1,10].', 'Getting the touch case wrong. Read whether [1,4] and [4,5] overlap in this problem, and use < or ≤ to match.', 'Sorting by start when the question is about keeping or removing the most intervals. That needs sort by end.', 'In the sweep line, letting a start come before an end at the same time when they shouldn''t overlap. Sort the tie on purpose.', 'In Java, sorting with (a, b) -> a[0] - b[0]. It overflows for large negative and positive values; use Integer.compare.', 'Comparing the new interval with the first merged block instead of the last one.']::text[],
  array['determine-if-two-events-have-conflict', 'teemo-attacking', 'maximum-population-year']::text[],
  9,
  $json${"version":1,"summary":"For problems about ranges with a start and an end: meetings, bookings, time slots. Sort them so that overlapping ranges sit next to each other, then merge, insert or count in one pass.","prerequisites":["prefix-sum","two-pointers"],"walkthrough":{"problemSlug":"merge-intervals","input":"intervals = [[2,3],[1,5],[6,8],[7,7]]","intro":"one step per interval, after the sort.","columns":["Interval","What happened","Merged so far"],"steps":[{"window":"[1,5] [2,3] [6,8] [7,7]","action":"sort by start","best":"(empty)"},{"window":"[1,5]","action":"nothing merged yet: start a block","best":"[1,5]"},{"window":"[2,3]","action":"2 ≤ 5, overlaps: end = max(5, 3) = 5","best":"[1,5]"},{"window":"[6,8]","action":"6 > 5: start a new block","best":"[1,5] [6,8]"},{"window":"[7,7]","action":"7 ≤ 8, overlaps: end = max(8, 7) = 8","best":"[1,5] [6,8]"}],"result":"[[1,5],[6,8]]. Setting end = 3 instead of max(5, 3) at the second step would have split [1,5] wrongly."},"template":{"note":"Sort by start, then for each interval either start a new block or extend the last one. Always take the max of the two ends: an interval can sit entirely inside the block.","code":{"python":"def merge(intervals: list[list[int]]) -> list[list[int]]:\n    # sort by start: overlaps end up side by side\n    intervals.sort(key=lambda iv: iv[0])\n    merged = []\n    for start, end in intervals:\n        # starts after the last block ends: a new block\n        if not merged or start > merged[-1][1]:\n            merged.append([start, end])\n        else:\n            # overlaps: grow the end, never shrink it\n            merged[-1][1] = max(merged[-1][1], end)\n    return merged\n","cpp":"vector<vector<int>> merge(vector<vector<int>> intervals) {\n    // sort by start: overlaps end up side by side\n    sort(intervals.begin(), intervals.end(),\n         [](const vector<int>& a, const vector<int>& b) {\n             return a[0] < b[0];\n         });\n    vector<vector<int>> merged;\n    for (const auto& iv : intervals) {\n        int start = iv[0], end = iv[1];\n        // starts after the last block ends: a new block\n        if (merged.empty() || start > merged.back()[1]) {\n            merged.push_back({start, end});\n        } else {\n            // overlaps: grow the end, never shrink it\n            merged.back()[1] = max(merged.back()[1], end);\n        }\n    }\n    return merged;\n}\n","java":"class Solution {\n    public int[][] merge(int[][] intervals) {\n        // sort by start: overlaps end up side by side\n        Arrays.sort(intervals,\n            (a, b) -> Integer.compare(a[0], b[0]));\n        List<int[]> merged = new ArrayList<>();\n        for (int[] iv : intervals) {\n            int start = iv[0], end = iv[1];\n            int[] last = merged.isEmpty()\n                ? null : merged.get(merged.size() - 1);\n            // starts after the last block ends: a new block\n            if (last == null || start > last[1]) {\n                merged.add(new int[]{start, end});\n            } else {\n                // overlaps: grow the end, never shrink it\n                last[1] = Math.max(last[1], end);\n            }\n        }\n        return merged.toArray(new int[0][]);\n    }\n}\n"}},"variants":[{"name":"Insert into a sorted list","when":"The intervals are already sorted and disjoint, and one new interval arrives.","idea":"No sort. Three loops: copy everything that ends before the new interval starts, absorb everything that starts before it ends (stretching its start and end), then copy the rest.","code":{"python":"def insert(\n    intervals: list[list[int]], new_interval: list[int]\n) -> list[list[int]]:\n    out = []\n    i, n = 0, len(intervals)\n    # 1. ends before new starts: copy it\n    while i < n and intervals[i][1] < new_interval[0]:\n        out.append(intervals[i])\n        i += 1\n    # 2. starts before new ends: absorb it into new\n    start, end = new_interval\n    while i < n and intervals[i][0] <= end:\n        start = min(start, intervals[i][0])\n        end = max(end, intervals[i][1])\n        i += 1\n    out.append([start, end])\n    # 3. everything left starts after new ends\n    while i < n:\n        out.append(intervals[i])\n        i += 1\n    return out\n","cpp":"vector<vector<int>> insert(const vector<vector<int>>& intervals,\n                           const vector<int>& newInterval) {\n    vector<vector<int>> out;\n    int i = 0, n = intervals.size();\n    // 1. ends before new starts: copy it\n    while (i < n && intervals[i][1] < newInterval[0]) {\n        out.push_back(intervals[i]);\n        ++i;\n    }\n    // 2. starts before new ends: absorb it into new\n    int start = newInterval[0], end = newInterval[1];\n    while (i < n && intervals[i][0] <= end) {\n        start = min(start, intervals[i][0]);\n        end = max(end, intervals[i][1]);\n        ++i;\n    }\n    out.push_back({start, end});\n    // 3. everything left starts after new ends\n    while (i < n) {\n        out.push_back(intervals[i]);\n        ++i;\n    }\n    return out;\n}\n","java":"class Solution {\n    public int[][] insert(int[][] intervals,\n                          int[] newInterval) {\n        List<int[]> out = new ArrayList<>();\n        int i = 0, n = intervals.length;\n        // 1. ends before new starts: copy it\n        while (i < n && intervals[i][1] < newInterval[0]) {\n            out.add(intervals[i]);\n            i++;\n        }\n        // 2. starts before new ends: absorb it into new\n        int start = newInterval[0], end = newInterval[1];\n        while (i < n && intervals[i][0] <= end) {\n            start = Math.min(start, intervals[i][0]);\n            end = Math.max(end, intervals[i][1]);\n            i++;\n        }\n        out.add(new int[]{start, end});\n        // 3. everything left starts after new ends\n        while (i < n) {\n            out.add(intervals[i]);\n            i++;\n        }\n        return out.toArray(new int[0][]);\n    }\n}\n"},"problemSlug":"insert-interval"},{"name":"Sweep line: the most at once","when":"You need the peak number of overlapping intervals: rooms, groups, cars, people alive in a year.","idea":"Turn each interval into a +1 event at its start and a −1 event where it stops. Sort the events and keep a running sum; the answer is its peak. Here the ends are inclusive, so the −1 goes at right + 1, and at equal times the −1 sorts first.","code":{"python":"def min_groups(intervals: list[list[int]]) -> int:\n    events = []\n    for left, right in intervals:\n        events.append((left, 1))\n        # ends are inclusive: it's gone from right + 1 on\n        events.append((right + 1, -1))\n    # by time; at a tie, -1 sorts first, so ends go first\n    events.sort()\n    active = best = 0\n    for _, delta in events:\n        active += delta\n        # most intervals alive at one moment\n        best = max(best, active)\n    return best\n","cpp":"int minGroups(const vector<vector<int>>& intervals) {\n    vector<pair<int, int>> events;\n    for (const auto& iv : intervals) {\n        events.push_back({iv[0], 1});\n        // ends are inclusive: it's gone from right + 1 on\n        events.push_back({iv[1] + 1, -1});\n    }\n    // by time; at a tie, -1 sorts first, so ends go first\n    sort(events.begin(), events.end());\n    int active = 0, best = 0;\n    for (const auto& [time, delta] : events) {\n        active += delta;\n        // most intervals alive at one moment\n        best = max(best, active);\n    }\n    return best;\n}\n","java":"class Solution {\n    public int minGroups(int[][] intervals) {\n        int[][] events = new int[2 * intervals.length][];\n        int k = 0;\n        for (int[] iv : intervals) {\n            events[k++] = new int[]{iv[0], 1};\n            // ends are inclusive: it's gone from right + 1 on\n            events[k++] = new int[]{iv[1] + 1, -1};\n        }\n        // by time; at a tie, -1 sorts first, so ends go first\n        Arrays.sort(events, (a, b) -> a[0] != b[0]\n            ? Integer.compare(a[0], b[0])\n            : Integer.compare(a[1], b[1]));\n        int active = 0, best = 0;\n        for (int[] e : events) {\n            active += e[1];\n            // most intervals alive at one moment\n            best = Math.max(best, active);\n        }\n        return best;\n    }\n}\n"},"problemSlug":"divide-intervals-into-minimum-number-of-groups"},{"name":"Two sorted lists","when":"You get two lists, each sorted and disjoint, and need where they overlap.","idea":"Walk both lists with two pointers. The overlap of the current pair is [max of starts, min of ends], if that's not empty. Then move past whichever interval ends first: it can't overlap anything later in the other list.","code":{"python":"def interval_intersection(\n    a: list[list[int]], b: list[list[int]]\n) -> list[list[int]]:\n    i = j = 0\n    out = []\n    while i < len(a) and j < len(b):\n        # the shared part, if there is one\n        lo = max(a[i][0], b[j][0])\n        hi = min(a[i][1], b[j][1])\n        if lo <= hi:\n            out.append([lo, hi])\n        # the one that ends first can't meet anything else\n        if a[i][1] < b[j][1]:\n            i += 1\n        else:\n            j += 1\n    return out\n","cpp":"vector<vector<int>> intervalIntersection(\n    const vector<vector<int>>& a,\n    const vector<vector<int>>& b) {\n    size_t i = 0, j = 0;\n    vector<vector<int>> out;\n    while (i < a.size() && j < b.size()) {\n        // the shared part, if there is one\n        int lo = max(a[i][0], b[j][0]);\n        int hi = min(a[i][1], b[j][1]);\n        if (lo <= hi) out.push_back({lo, hi});\n        // the one that ends first can't meet anything else\n        if (a[i][1] < b[j][1]) {\n            ++i;\n        } else {\n            ++j;\n        }\n    }\n    return out;\n}\n","java":"class Solution {\n    public int[][] intervalIntersection(int[][] a, int[][] b) {\n        int i = 0, j = 0;\n        List<int[]> out = new ArrayList<>();\n        while (i < a.length && j < b.length) {\n            // the shared part, if there is one\n            int lo = Math.max(a[i][0], b[j][0]);\n            int hi = Math.min(a[i][1], b[j][1]);\n            if (lo <= hi) out.add(new int[]{lo, hi});\n            // the one that ends first can't meet anything else\n            if (a[i][1] < b[j][1]) {\n                i++;\n            } else {\n                j++;\n            }\n        }\n        return out.toArray(new int[0][]);\n    }\n}\n"},"problemSlug":"interval-list-intersections"}],"complexity":{"time":"O(n log n), or O(n) if the input is already sorted","space":"O(n) for the output or the event list","why":"The sort dominates. After it, each interval is looked at once, and each comparison is only against the last merged block. The sweep line sorts 2n events, which is still O(n log n). The two-list walk moves one pointer every step, so it ends within len(a) + len(b) steps."},"notThis":["Keep the most non-overlapping intervals, or remove the fewest: sort by end and take greedily. See Greedy.","Many point queries (how many intervals cover time t?): sort starts and ends separately and binary search, or build a difference array if the range is small.","Intervals keep arriving and you need the current overlap each time: a sorted map or a heap of end times, not a full re-sort."],"ladder":[{"slug":"determine-if-two-events-have-conflict","tier":"warm-up","note":"The overlap test on its own: each starts before the other ends."},{"slug":"teemo-attacking","tier":"warm-up","note":"Merging on the fly when the intervals arrive already sorted."},{"slug":"maximum-population-year","tier":"warm-up","note":"A tiny sweep line: +1 at birth, −1 at death, find the peak."},{"slug":"merge-intervals","tier":"core","note":"The template: sort by start, then extend or append."},{"slug":"insert-interval","tier":"core","note":"Already sorted, so three phases and no sort."},{"slug":"count-days-without-meetings","tier":"core","note":"Merge first, then count the gaps between blocks."},{"slug":"remove-covered-intervals","tier":"core","note":"Break start ties by longer first, then track the furthest end."},{"slug":"interval-list-intersections","tier":"core","note":"Two sorted lists walked with two pointers."},{"slug":"divide-intervals-into-minimum-number-of-groups","tier":"core","note":"The sweep line peak. This is Meeting Rooms II without the paywall."},{"slug":"car-pooling","tier":"core","note":"The same sweep with weights: add passengers at pickup, remove them at drop-off."},{"slug":"my-calendar-i","tier":"core","note":"Intervals arrive one at a time: test each against what's booked."},{"slug":"number-of-flowers-in-full-bloom","tier":"stretch","note":"Many point queries: sorted starts and ends plus binary search."},{"slug":"minimum-interval-to-include-each-query","tier":"stretch","note":"Sort the queries too, and keep the open intervals in a heap."},{"slug":"the-skyline-problem","tier":"stretch","note":"A sweep line where the state is a max-heap of live heights."}]}$json$::jsonb),
('linked-list',
  'Linked List',
  'Linked List',
  array['The input is a ListNode head and the answer is a rearranged list: reversed, merged, reordered, with nodes removed.', 'You need a position you can''t index: the middle, the kth node from the end, where a loop starts.', 'The problem asks for O(1) extra space, so copying the values into an array isn''t allowed.', 'Two sorted lists (or k of them) have to become one sorted list.', 'The head itself might be removed or replaced, so you keep writing special cases for it.']::text[],
  'A linked list gives you no indexes, only next pointers. Almost every problem comes down to three moves: walk with two pointers at different speeds, turn links around one at a time, and stitch nodes onto a growing tail. The skill is keeping the right references alive while you change links.

Reversal keeps three pointers. prev is the head of the part already reversed, curr is the node being turned, and nxt saves the rest of the list before curr''s link is overwritten. After each step, everything before curr points backwards and everything from curr on is untouched. When curr falls off the end, prev is the new head.

Fast and slow pointers start together; fast moves two nodes per step, slow moves one. When fast reaches the end, slow has gone half as far, so it sits in the middle. If the list loops, fast can never reach the end. Once both are inside the loop, the gap between them shrinks by one node per step, so they must meet within one lap.

Merging uses a dummy node in front of the answer and a tail pointer. Each step links the smaller of the two heads onto the tail. The dummy means the first node isn''t a special case, and it handles a head that gets removed. Return dummy.next.

All of this is O(n) time and O(1) space because nodes are relinked, never copied. It breaks down when you need random access, like a binary search over positions. Then copy the values into an array first.',
  'def reverse_list(
    head: Optional[ListNode],
) -> Optional[ListNode]:
    prev = None
    curr = head
    while curr:
        # 1. save the rest before cutting the link
        nxt = curr.next
        # 2. turn this node''s link backwards
        curr.next = prev
        # 3. step both pointers forward
        prev = curr
        curr = nxt
    # curr fell off the end; prev is the old tail
    return prev
',
  'Time O(n) · Space O(1)',
  array['Overwriting curr.next before saving it. The rest of the list is then unreachable.', 'Checking fast.next.next without checking fast.next first. Test with lists of length 0, 1 and 2.', 'Not setting the new tail''s next to null after a cut or a reversal, which leaves a loop that hangs any later traversal.', 'Special-casing the head instead of putting a dummy node in front of it. Removing the first node is where the bugs hide.', 'Picking the wrong middle for even lengths. Decide whether slow should stop on the first or the second middle, and test [1,2,3,4].', 'Returning the old head after a reversal. The new head is prev.', 'Comparing values when you mean nodes. Two different nodes can hold the same value; compare references.']::text[],
  array['reverse-linked-list', 'merge-two-sorted-lists', 'middle-of-the-linked-list']::text[],
  8,
  $json${"version":1,"summary":"For problems that rewire a chain of nodes in place: reverse it, find its middle or its loop, merge two sorted chains, or cut and rejoin pieces. A few named pointers walk the list, and each node is touched a constant number of times.","prerequisites":["two-pointers"],"walkthrough":{"problemSlug":"reverse-linked-list","input":"head = [1,2,3,4,5]","intro":"one step per pass of the loop.","columns":["Still to turn (curr)","What happened","Reversed so far (prev)"],"steps":[{"window":"2 → 3 → 4 → 5","action":"save 2; point 1 at null; prev = 1","best":"1"},{"window":"3 → 4 → 5","action":"save 3; point 2 at 1; prev = 2","best":"2 → 1"},{"window":"4 → 5","action":"save 4; point 3 at 2; prev = 3","best":"3 → 2 → 1"},{"window":"5","action":"save 5; point 4 at 3; prev = 4","best":"4 → 3 → 2 → 1"},{"window":"null","action":"save null; point 5 at 4; prev = 5; curr is null, stop","best":"5 → 4 → 3 → 2 → 1"}],"result":"[5,4,3,2,1], with prev as the new head"},"template":{"note":"Save, turn, step. Write the three lines in this order every time: if you turn the link before saving next, the rest of the list is lost.","code":{"python":"def reverse_list(\n    head: Optional[ListNode],\n) -> Optional[ListNode]:\n    prev = None\n    curr = head\n    while curr:\n        # 1. save the rest before cutting the link\n        nxt = curr.next\n        # 2. turn this node's link backwards\n        curr.next = prev\n        # 3. step both pointers forward\n        prev = curr\n        curr = nxt\n    # curr fell off the end; prev is the old tail\n    return prev\n","cpp":"ListNode* reverseList(ListNode* head) {\n    ListNode* prev = nullptr;\n    ListNode* curr = head;\n    while (curr) {\n        // 1. save the rest before cutting the link\n        ListNode* nxt = curr->next;\n        // 2. turn this node's link backwards\n        curr->next = prev;\n        // 3. step both pointers forward\n        prev = curr;\n        curr = nxt;\n    }\n    // curr fell off the end; prev is the old tail\n    return prev;\n}\n","java":"class Solution {\n    public ListNode reverseList(ListNode head) {\n        ListNode prev = null;\n        ListNode curr = head;\n        while (curr != null) {\n            // 1. save the rest before cutting the link\n            ListNode nxt = curr.next;\n            // 2. turn this node's link backwards\n            curr.next = prev;\n            // 3. step both pointers forward\n            prev = curr;\n            curr = nxt;\n        }\n        // curr fell off the end; prev is the old tail\n        return prev;\n    }\n}\n"}},"variants":[{"name":"Fast and slow: where the loop starts","when":"You need the middle of a list, whether it loops, or the first node of the loop.","idea":"Run fast (two steps) and slow (one step) until they meet. Then move one of them back to head and step both one node at a time; they meet again at the loop's first node. If the part before the loop is a nodes long, the math works out so that a steps forward from the meeting point (maybe after a few full laps) also lands on the loop's start.","code":{"python":"def detect_cycle(\n    head: Optional[ListNode],\n) -> Optional[ListNode]:\n    slow = fast = head\n    while fast and fast.next:\n        slow = slow.next\n        fast = fast.next.next\n        # they met somewhere inside the loop\n        if slow is fast:\n            # restart one from head; both move one step\n            slow = head\n            while slow is not fast:\n                slow = slow.next\n                fast = fast.next\n            # they meet again at the loop's first node\n            return slow\n    # fast reached the end: there is no loop\n    return None\n","cpp":"ListNode* detectCycle(ListNode* head) {\n    ListNode* slow = head;\n    ListNode* fast = head;\n    while (fast && fast->next) {\n        slow = slow->next;\n        fast = fast->next->next;\n        // they met somewhere inside the loop\n        if (slow == fast) {\n            // restart one from head; both move one step\n            slow = head;\n            while (slow != fast) {\n                slow = slow->next;\n                fast = fast->next;\n            }\n            // they meet again at the loop's first node\n            return slow;\n        }\n    }\n    // fast reached the end: there is no loop\n    return nullptr;\n}\n","java":"class Solution {\n    public ListNode detectCycle(ListNode head) {\n        ListNode slow = head;\n        ListNode fast = head;\n        while (fast != null && fast.next != null) {\n            slow = slow.next;\n            fast = fast.next.next;\n            // they met somewhere inside the loop\n            if (slow == fast) {\n                // restart one from head; both move one step\n                slow = head;\n                while (slow != fast) {\n                    slow = slow.next;\n                    fast = fast.next;\n                }\n                // they meet again at the loop's first node\n                return slow;\n            }\n        }\n        // fast reached the end: there is no loop\n        return null;\n    }\n}\n"},"problemSlug":"linked-list-cycle-ii"},{"name":"Merge with a dummy head","when":"Two sorted lists become one, or you build a new list node by node.","idea":"Keep a tail pointer that starts at a dummy node. Link the smaller head onto the tail and advance that list. When one list runs out, attach the rest of the other in one step: it's already sorted.","code":{"python":"def merge_two_lists(\n    a: Optional[ListNode], b: Optional[ListNode]\n) -> Optional[ListNode]:\n    # a dummy head: the first node is no special case\n    dummy = ListNode()\n    tail = dummy\n    while a and b:\n        # link the smaller head; ties take from a\n        if a.val <= b.val:\n            tail.next = a\n            a = a.next\n        else:\n            tail.next = b\n            b = b.next\n        tail = tail.next\n    # one list ran out: the other is already sorted\n    tail.next = a if a else b\n    return dummy.next\n","cpp":"ListNode* mergeTwoLists(ListNode* a, ListNode* b) {\n    // a dummy head: the first node is no special case\n    ListNode dummy;\n    ListNode* tail = &dummy;\n    while (a && b) {\n        // link the smaller head; ties take from a\n        if (a->val <= b->val) {\n            tail->next = a;\n            a = a->next;\n        } else {\n            tail->next = b;\n            b = b->next;\n        }\n        tail = tail->next;\n    }\n    // one list ran out: the other is already sorted\n    tail->next = a ? a : b;\n    return dummy.next;\n}\n","java":"class Solution {\n    public ListNode mergeTwoLists(ListNode a, ListNode b) {\n        // a dummy head: the first node is no special case\n        ListNode dummy = new ListNode();\n        ListNode tail = dummy;\n        while (a != null && b != null) {\n            // link the smaller head; ties take from a\n            if (a.val <= b.val) {\n                tail.next = a;\n                a = a.next;\n            } else {\n                tail.next = b;\n                b = b.next;\n            }\n            tail = tail.next;\n        }\n        // one list ran out: the other is already sorted\n        tail.next = (a != null) ? a : b;\n        return dummy.next;\n    }\n}\n"},"problemSlug":"merge-two-sorted-lists"},{"name":"Middle, reverse, weave","when":"The answer pairs the front of the list with the back: reorder, palindrome check, twin sums.","idea":"Find the middle with fast and slow, cut the list there, reverse the second half, then walk both halves together. All three moves from this primer in one problem, still in O(1) space.","code":{"python":"def reorder_list(head: Optional[ListNode]) -> None:\n    if not head:\n        return\n    # 1. middle: slow stops at the end of the first half\n    slow = fast = head\n    while fast.next and fast.next.next:\n        slow = slow.next\n        fast = fast.next.next\n    # 2. cut after slow, then reverse the second half\n    prev, curr = None, slow.next\n    slow.next = None\n    while curr:\n        nxt = curr.next\n        curr.next = prev\n        prev, curr = curr, nxt\n    # 3. weave: one node from each half in turn\n    first, second = head, prev\n    while second:\n        n1, n2 = first.next, second.next\n        first.next = second\n        second.next = n1\n        first, second = n1, n2\n","cpp":"void reorderList(ListNode* head) {\n    if (!head) return;\n    // 1. middle: slow stops at the end of the first half\n    ListNode* slow = head;\n    ListNode* fast = head;\n    while (fast->next && fast->next->next) {\n        slow = slow->next;\n        fast = fast->next->next;\n    }\n    // 2. cut after slow, then reverse the second half\n    ListNode* prev = nullptr;\n    ListNode* curr = slow->next;\n    slow->next = nullptr;\n    while (curr) {\n        ListNode* nxt = curr->next;\n        curr->next = prev;\n        prev = curr;\n        curr = nxt;\n    }\n    // 3. weave: one node from each half in turn\n    ListNode* first = head;\n    ListNode* second = prev;\n    while (second) {\n        ListNode* n1 = first->next;\n        ListNode* n2 = second->next;\n        first->next = second;\n        second->next = n1;\n        first = n1;\n        second = n2;\n    }\n}\n","java":"class Solution {\n    public void reorderList(ListNode head) {\n        if (head == null) return;\n        // 1. middle: slow stops at the end of the first half\n        ListNode slow = head;\n        ListNode fast = head;\n        while (fast.next != null && fast.next.next != null) {\n            slow = slow.next;\n            fast = fast.next.next;\n        }\n        // 2. cut after slow, then reverse the second half\n        ListNode prev = null;\n        ListNode curr = slow.next;\n        slow.next = null;\n        while (curr != null) {\n            ListNode nxt = curr.next;\n            curr.next = prev;\n            prev = curr;\n            curr = nxt;\n        }\n        // 3. weave: one node from each half in turn\n        ListNode first = head;\n        ListNode second = prev;\n        while (second != null) {\n            ListNode n1 = first.next;\n            ListNode n2 = second.next;\n            first.next = second;\n            second.next = n1;\n            first = n1;\n            second = n2;\n        }\n    }\n}\n"},"problemSlug":"reorder-list"}],"complexity":{"time":"O(n)","space":"O(1)","why":"Each pointer only moves forward and each link is rewritten at most once, so every node is touched a constant number of times. Nothing is copied: the answer reuses the input's nodes. In the loop case, slow enters the loop within n steps and fast catches it within one more lap."},"notThis":["You need the kth element many times or a binary search over positions: copy the values into an array.","The question is about values only, not the links (is there a duplicate value?): a hash set over the values is simpler.","Merging k sorted lists at once: pairwise merging works, but a min-heap of the k heads is the standard tool. See Heap / Top-K."],"ladder":[{"slug":"reverse-linked-list","tier":"warm-up","note":"The template: save, turn, step."},{"slug":"merge-two-sorted-lists","tier":"warm-up","note":"A dummy head and a tail pointer. The merge step of merge sort."},{"slug":"middle-of-the-linked-list","tier":"warm-up","note":"Fast and slow pointers in their simplest form."},{"slug":"linked-list-cycle","tier":"warm-up","note":"The same two pointers. If they ever meet, the list loops."},{"slug":"remove-nth-node-from-end-of-list","tier":"core","note":"Give fast an n-node head start; a dummy covers removing the head."},{"slug":"linked-list-cycle-ii","tier":"core","note":"Floyd's second phase: reset one pointer to find where the loop starts."},{"slug":"palindrome-linked-list","tier":"core","note":"Middle, then reverse the second half and compare, in O(1) space."},{"slug":"reverse-linked-list-ii","tier":"core","note":"Reverse only a stretch, then reconnect both ends of it."},{"slug":"reorder-list","tier":"core","note":"All three moves in one problem: middle, reverse, weave."},{"slug":"add-two-numbers","tier":"core","note":"Build a new list behind a dummy head while carrying a digit."},{"slug":"sort-list","tier":"core","note":"Merge sort on a list: split with fast and slow, merge with a dummy."},{"slug":"reverse-nodes-in-k-group","tier":"stretch","note":"Reverse k nodes at a time and stitch each group to the next; leave a short tail alone."},{"slug":"find-the-duplicate-number","tier":"stretch","note":"Floyd's cycle start on an array: treat i → nums[i] as a next pointer."}]}$json$::jsonb),
('monotonic-stack',
  'Monotonic Stack',
  'Monotonic Stack',
  array['For every element you need the next (or previous) greater or smaller element, or the distance to it.', 'The brute force is, for each i, scanning right until something beats it, which is O(n²) on sorted input.', 'Each bar or value matters only until something bigger (or smaller) comes along and blocks it: temperatures, stock spans, people in a queue who can see each other.', 'You want the widest range in which an element is the minimum (or maximum): histogram rectangles, sums of subarray minimums.']::text[],
  'Scan left to right and keep a stack of indices that are still waiting for their answer. For "next warmer day", their temperatures never rise from the bottom of the stack to the top. When a new day i arrives, it is warmer than every colder day on top, and it is the first such day those days have seen. So pop each of them and record i as its answer. Then push i, which now waits too.

The order on the stack is what makes this work. If the top isn''t colder than today, nothing below it is either, because the stack only gets warmer as you go down. So you stop at the first non-colder top, and you never look deeper than you have to.

It''s correct because a day is popped exactly when the first warmer day appears. Every day between them was not warmer, or it would have popped this one sooner. It''s O(n) even with a while loop inside the for loop, because each index is pushed once and popped at most once. That''s 2n operations in total.

Pick the comparison from the question. Next greater: pop while the top is smaller. Next smaller: pop while the top is bigger. Previous greater or smaller: read the top after popping, because it''s the nearest survivor to the left. With equal values, decide whether equal counts as "greater" and use < or <= to match. Getting this wrong is the most common bug.

The pattern doesn''t fit questions about a range that moves, like the max of each window of size k. Old indices also have to leave from the bottom then, so use a monotonic deque (Sliding Window).',
  'def daily_temperatures(temps: list[int]) -> list[int]:
    ans = [0] * len(temps)
    # days still waiting for a warmer one; their temps
    # never rise from the bottom of the stack to the top
    stack: list[int] = []
    for i, t in enumerate(temps):
        # today answers every colder day on top
        while stack and temps[stack[-1]] < t:
            j = stack.pop()
            ans[j] = i - j
        stack.append(i)
    # days left on the stack never warm up: they stay 0
    return ans
',
  'Time O(n), or O(2n) for the circular version · Space O(n) for the stack and the answers',
  array['Pushing values when the answer needs a distance or a position. Push indices and read values through them.', 'Using < where you need <= (or the reverse), which gets equal values wrong. Test with ties, like [70, 70, 71].', 'Forgetting what''s left on the stack at the end. Those elements never found an answer, so give them the default (0 or −1), or flush them with a final sentinel.', 'Reading the left boundary before popping instead of after. The previous smaller element is the new top once the current one is gone.', 'In the circular version, pushing indices on both laps, which can put an element on the stack twice.', 'In Remove K Digits, forgetting the removals still owed after the scan, or the leading zeros ("10200", k = 1 gives "200").']::text[],
  array['next-greater-element-i', 'final-prices-with-a-special-discount-in-a-shop', 'daily-temperatures']::text[],
  7,
  $json${"version":1,"summary":"A stack of indices whose values stay sorted, used to find, for every element, the nearest bigger or smaller element to its left or right. One pass answers all n questions in O(n) total.","prerequisites":["stack"],"walkthrough":{"problemSlug":"daily-temperatures","input":"temperatures = [73, 74, 75, 71, 69, 72, 76, 73]","intro":"one step per day. The stack shows temperatures, but it holds their indices.","columns":["Stack (bottom to top)","What happened","Answer so far"],"steps":[{"window":"73","action":"nothing to pop; push day 0","best":"[·,·,·,·,·,·,·,·]"},{"window":"74","action":"74 > 73: pop day 0 (1 day); push","best":"[1,·,·,·,·,·,·,·]"},{"window":"75","action":"75 > 74: pop day 1 (1 day); push","best":"[1,1,·,·,·,·,·,·]"},{"window":"75 71","action":"71 is colder than 75; push","best":"[1,1,·,·,·,·,·,·]"},{"window":"75 71 69","action":"69 is colder than 71; push","best":"[1,1,·,·,·,·,·,·]"},{"window":"75 72","action":"72 pops day 4 (1 day) and day 3 (2 days); stops at 75; push","best":"[1,1,·,2,1,·,·,·]"},{"window":"76","action":"76 pops day 5 (1 day) and day 2 (4 days); push","best":"[1,1,4,2,1,1,·,·]"},{"window":"76 73","action":"73 is colder than 76; push","best":"[1,1,4,2,1,1,·,·]"}],"result":"[1, 1, 4, 2, 1, 1, 0, 0]. Days 6 and 7 are still on the stack at the end, so they never get a warmer day and keep 0."},"template":{"note":"For each i: pop while the top loses to i and answer it, then push i. Change the comparison for greater or smaller, and read the top after popping when you want the previous element instead of the next.","code":{"python":"def daily_temperatures(temps: list[int]) -> list[int]:\n    ans = [0] * len(temps)\n    # days still waiting for a warmer one; their temps\n    # never rise from the bottom of the stack to the top\n    stack: list[int] = []\n    for i, t in enumerate(temps):\n        # today answers every colder day on top\n        while stack and temps[stack[-1]] < t:\n            j = stack.pop()\n            ans[j] = i - j\n        stack.append(i)\n    # days left on the stack never warm up: they stay 0\n    return ans\n","cpp":"vector<int> dailyTemperatures(const vector<int>& temps) {\n    vector<int> ans(temps.size(), 0);\n    // days still waiting for a warmer one; their temps\n    // never rise from the bottom of the stack to the top\n    vector<int> stack;\n    for (int i = 0; i < (int)temps.size(); ++i) {\n        // today answers every colder day on top\n        while (!stack.empty()\n               && temps[stack.back()] < temps[i]) {\n            int j = stack.back();\n            stack.pop_back();\n            ans[j] = i - j;\n        }\n        stack.push_back(i);\n    }\n    // days left on the stack never warm up: they stay 0\n    return ans;\n}\n","java":"class Solution {\n    public int[] dailyTemperatures(int[] temps) {\n        int[] ans = new int[temps.length];\n        // days still waiting for a warmer one; their temps\n        // never rise from the bottom of the stack to the top\n        Deque<Integer> stack = new ArrayDeque<>();\n        for (int i = 0; i < temps.length; i++) {\n            // today answers every colder day on top\n            while (!stack.isEmpty()\n                   && temps[stack.peek()] < temps[i]) {\n                int j = stack.pop();\n                ans[j] = i - j;\n            }\n            stack.push(i);\n        }\n        // days left on the stack never warm up: they stay 0\n        return ans;\n    }\n}\n"}},"variants":[{"name":"Circular array","when":"The array wraps around, so the next greater element can be before i.","idea":"Loop i from 0 to 2n − 1 and use nums[i % n]. Push indices only on the first lap; the second lap only pops, so elements near the end can find answers near the start.","code":{"python":"def next_greater_elements(nums: list[int]) -> list[int]:\n    n = len(nums)\n    ans = [-1] * n\n    # indices still waiting for a greater value\n    stack: list[int] = []\n    # go round twice so the end can see the start\n    for i in range(2 * n):\n        x = nums[i % n]\n        while stack and nums[stack[-1]] < x:\n            ans[stack.pop()] = x\n        # push each index once, on the first lap only\n        if i < n:\n            stack.append(i)\n    return ans\n","cpp":"vector<int> nextGreaterElements(const vector<int>& nums) {\n    int n = nums.size();\n    vector<int> ans(n, -1);\n    // indices still waiting for a greater value\n    vector<int> stack;\n    // go round twice so the end can see the start\n    for (int i = 0; i < 2 * n; ++i) {\n        int x = nums[i % n];\n        while (!stack.empty() && nums[stack.back()] < x) {\n            ans[stack.back()] = x;\n            stack.pop_back();\n        }\n        // push each index once, on the first lap only\n        if (i < n) stack.push_back(i);\n    }\n    return ans;\n}\n","java":"class Solution {\n    public int[] nextGreaterElements(int[] nums) {\n        int n = nums.length;\n        int[] ans = new int[n];\n        Arrays.fill(ans, -1);\n        // indices still waiting for a greater value\n        Deque<Integer> stack = new ArrayDeque<>();\n        // go round twice so the end can see the start\n        for (int i = 0; i < 2 * n; i++) {\n            int x = nums[i % n];\n            while (!stack.isEmpty() && nums[stack.peek()] < x) {\n                ans[stack.pop()] = x;\n            }\n            // push each index once, on the first lap only\n            if (i < n) stack.push(i);\n        }\n        return ans;\n    }\n}\n"},"problemSlug":"next-greater-element-ii"},{"name":"Both neighbours at once: widest span","when":"Each element's answer depends on how far it can stretch left and right before hitting something smaller.","idea":"Keep heights rising up the stack. When bar i is not taller than the top, pop it: i is its first smaller bar on the right, and the new top is its first smaller bar on the left. The width is i − left − 1. A final bar of height 0 empties the stack.","code":{"python":"def largest_rectangle_area(heights: list[int]) -> int:\n    n = len(heights)\n    # bar indices; heights rise from bottom to top\n    stack: list[int] = []\n    best = 0\n    # one extra bar of height 0 at i = n empties the stack\n    for i in range(n + 1):\n        h = heights[i] if i < n else 0\n        while stack and heights[stack[-1]] >= h:\n            # the popped bar can't stretch right past i-1,\n            # nor left past the bar now on top\n            top = heights[stack.pop()]\n            left = stack[-1] if stack else -1\n            best = max(best, top * (i - left - 1))\n        stack.append(i)\n    return best\n","cpp":"int largestRectangleArea(const vector<int>& heights) {\n    int n = heights.size();\n    // bar indices; heights rise from bottom to top\n    vector<int> stack;\n    int best = 0;\n    // one extra bar of height 0 at i = n empties the stack\n    for (int i = 0; i <= n; ++i) {\n        int h = i < n ? heights[i] : 0;\n        while (!stack.empty() && heights[stack.back()] >= h) {\n            // the popped bar can't stretch right past i-1,\n            // nor left past the bar now on top\n            int top = heights[stack.back()];\n            stack.pop_back();\n            int left = stack.empty() ? -1 : stack.back();\n            best = max(best, top * (i - left - 1));\n        }\n        stack.push_back(i);\n    }\n    return best;\n}\n","java":"class Solution {\n    public int largestRectangleArea(int[] heights) {\n        int n = heights.length;\n        // bar indices; heights rise from bottom to top\n        Deque<Integer> stack = new ArrayDeque<>();\n        int best = 0;\n        // one extra bar of height 0 at i = n empties the stack\n        for (int i = 0; i <= n; i++) {\n            int h = i < n ? heights[i] : 0;\n            while (!stack.isEmpty()\n                   && heights[stack.peek()] >= h) {\n                // the popped bar can't stretch right past i-1,\n                // nor left past the bar now on top\n                int top = heights[stack.pop()];\n                int left = stack.isEmpty() ? -1 : stack.peek();\n                best = Math.max(best, top * (i - left - 1));\n            }\n            stack.push(i);\n        }\n        return best;\n    }\n}\n"},"problemSlug":"largest-rectangle-in-histogram"},{"name":"Greedy removal","when":"You build the smallest (or largest) sequence by deleting up to k items, keeping the order.","idea":"A bigger digit just before a smaller one should go. Pop while k > 0 and the top is bigger than the new digit. Any removals left come off the end, then strip leading zeros.","code":{"python":"def remove_kdigits(num: str, k: int) -> str:\n    # digits kept so far; they never fall bottom to top\n    stack: list[str] = []\n    for d in num:\n        # a bigger digit just before a smaller one: drop it\n        while k > 0 and stack and stack[-1] > d:\n            stack.pop()\n            k -= 1\n        stack.append(d)\n    # any removals still owed come off the (largest) end\n    stack = stack[: len(stack) - k]\n    # strip leading zeros; nothing left means \"0\"\n    out = \"\".join(stack).lstrip(\"0\")\n    return out if out else \"0\"\n","cpp":"string removeKdigits(const string& num, int k) {\n    // digits kept so far; they never fall bottom to top\n    string stack;\n    for (char d : num) {\n        // a bigger digit just before a smaller one: drop it\n        while (k > 0 && !stack.empty() && stack.back() > d) {\n            stack.pop_back();\n            --k;\n        }\n        stack.push_back(d);\n    }\n    // any removals still owed come off the (largest) end\n    stack.resize(stack.size() - k);\n    // strip leading zeros; nothing left means \"0\"\n    size_t start = stack.find_first_not_of('0');\n    return start == string::npos ? \"0\" : stack.substr(start);\n}\n","java":"class Solution {\n    public String removeKdigits(String num, int k) {\n        // digits kept so far; they never fall bottom to top\n        StringBuilder stack = new StringBuilder();\n        for (char d : num.toCharArray()) {\n            // a bigger digit just before a smaller one: drop it\n            while (k > 0 && stack.length() > 0\n                   && stack.charAt(stack.length() - 1) > d) {\n                stack.deleteCharAt(stack.length() - 1);\n                k--;\n            }\n            stack.append(d);\n        }\n        // any removals still owed come off the (largest) end\n        stack.setLength(stack.length() - k);\n        // strip leading zeros; nothing left means \"0\"\n        int start = 0;\n        while (start < stack.length()\n               && stack.charAt(start) == '0') start++;\n        String out = stack.substring(start);\n        return out.isEmpty() ? \"0\" : out;\n    }\n}\n"},"problemSlug":"remove-k-digits"}],"complexity":{"time":"O(n), or O(2n) for the circular version","space":"O(n) for the stack and the answers","why":"Each index is pushed once and popped at most once, so all the inner while loops together do at most n pops. The circular version pushes n times and scans 2n positions."},"notThis":["You need the max or min of every window of size k: a monotonic deque, since old indices must leave from the front (Sliding Window).","You only need the single largest or smallest value overall: one pass with a variable, no stack.","Questions about the k largest values, not the nearest larger one: use a heap."],"ladder":[{"slug":"next-greater-element-i","tier":"warm-up","note":"Next greater for every value, stored in a map for lookups."},{"slug":"final-prices-with-a-special-discount-in-a-shop","tier":"warm-up","note":"Next smaller or equal to the right: the comparison flips."},{"slug":"daily-temperatures","tier":"core","note":"The template, with distances instead of values."},{"slug":"next-greater-element-ii","tier":"core","note":"Circular: two laps, pushing only on the first."},{"slug":"online-stock-span","tier":"core","note":"Previous greater, one call at a time: keep (price, span) pairs on the stack."},{"slug":"car-fleet","tier":"core","note":"Sort by position, then keep a stack of arrival times that only grows."},{"slug":"remove-k-digits","tier":"core","note":"Greedy removal while the stack stays non-decreasing."},{"slug":"132-pattern","tier":"core","note":"Scan right to left, tracking the largest popped value as the \"2\"."},{"slug":"sum-of-subarray-minimums","tier":"core","note":"Each element's span on both sides, with < on one side and <= on the other so ties count once."},{"slug":"largest-rectangle-in-histogram","tier":"stretch","note":"Left and right boundaries in one pass, plus a sentinel bar."},{"slug":"maximal-rectangle","tier":"stretch","note":"Build a histogram per row, then run the histogram solution."},{"slug":"number-of-visible-people-in-a-queue","tier":"stretch","note":"Count how many shorter people each person pops, plus the one who blocks them."}]}$json$::jsonb),
('prefix-sum',
  'Prefix Sum',
  'Prefix Sum',
  array['You need the sum of many different ranges of an array that doesn''t change.', 'The question asks how many subarrays, or the longest one, with sum equal to k, divisible by k, or balanced between two kinds of element.', 'The array can contain negative numbers or zeros, so a sliding window can''t decide when to shrink.', 'Each answer depends on everything to the left and everything to the right of i, like a product of all the other elements.']::text[],
  'Let pre[i] be the sum of the first i elements, with pre[0] = 0 for the empty prefix. Then the sum of nums[l..r] is pre[r + 1] − pre[l]: the total up to r, minus the part before l. Building pre takes one pass, and every range sum after that is O(1).

Flip it around to count subarrays. A subarray ending at r has sum k exactly when some earlier prefix equals pre[r + 1] − k. So walk left to right, keep a hash map from each prefix sum to how many times it has appeared, and at each index add the count of prefix − k. Every earlier prefix with that value is the start of one subarray that ends here.

Why it''s correct: every subarray is a pair (earlier prefix, current prefix), and each pair is checked exactly once, when you reach its right end. Why it''s fast: one pass, one lookup and one insert per element, so O(n). Negative numbers don''t matter, because nothing is ever shrunk or skipped.

The {0: 1} seed is the empty prefix. Without it you miss every subarray that starts at index 0. Look up before you record the current prefix, or a subarray of length zero gets counted when k = 0.

The same idea works for anything you can undo. XOR, and counts of each letter, both work. Remainders mod k work, because two prefixes with the same remainder differ by a multiple of k. Max and min don''t: you can''t subtract a maximum back out, so range maximum needs a different structure.',
  'def subarray_sum(nums: list[int], k: int) -> int:
    # prefix sum -> how many times it has appeared;
    # 0 is the empty prefix, before index 0
    seen = {0: 1}
    prefix = count = 0
    for x in nums:
        prefix += x
        # 1. earlier prefixes == prefix - k: one match each
        count += seen.get(prefix - k, 0)
        # 2. then record this prefix for later indices
        seen[prefix] = seen.get(prefix, 0) + 1
    return count
',
  'Time O(n) to build or scan; O(1) per range query · Space O(n) for the prefix array or the map',
  array['Forgetting the {0: 1} seed (or {0: −1} for first indices), which drops every subarray that starts at index 0.', 'Recording the current prefix before looking up prefix − k. With k = 0 every element then matches itself.', 'Off-by-one between nums and pre: pre has n + 1 entries, and the sum of nums[l..r] is pre[r + 1] − pre[l], not pre[r] − pre[l].', 'Overwriting the first index when you want the longest stretch. Only store a prefix the first time you see it.', 'Taking % of a negative prefix in C++ or Java and getting a negative remainder that never matches.', 'Overflow: sums of many large values can exceed 32 bits. Use long long or long when the limits allow it.']::text[],
  array['running-sum-of-1d-array', 'range-sum-query-immutable', 'find-pivot-index']::text[],
  2,
  $json${"version":1,"summary":"Store the running total up to each index once, and the sum of any subarray becomes one subtraction. Paired with a hash map of earlier totals, it counts or finds subarrays with a given sum in one pass, negatives included.","prerequisites":["arrays-hashing"],"walkthrough":{"problemSlug":"subarray-sum-equals-k","input":"nums = [1, 2, 3, -3, 3], k = 3","intro":"one step per index, starting from {0: 1} for the empty prefix.","columns":["Prefix and map","Look up prefix − 3","Count"],"steps":[{"window":"1; {0:1, 1:1}","action":"take 1; −2 not seen","best":"0"},{"window":"3; {0:1, 1:1, 3:1}","action":"take 2; 0 seen once: [1, 2]","best":"1"},{"window":"6; {…, 3:1, 6:1}","action":"take 3; 3 seen once: [3]","best":"2"},{"window":"3; {…, 3:2, 6:1}","action":"take −3; 0 seen once: [1, 2, 3, −3]","best":"3"},{"window":"6; {…, 3:2, 6:2}","action":"take 3; 3 seen twice: [3, −3, 3] and [3]","best":"5"}],"result":"5. The −3 brought the prefix back down to 3, which a sliding window could never have handled."},"template":{"note":"Running prefix, look up prefix − k, then record prefix. Change what the map stores (a count, the first index) and what you look up, and most of the ladder falls out.","code":{"python":"def subarray_sum(nums: list[int], k: int) -> int:\n    # prefix sum -> how many times it has appeared;\n    # 0 is the empty prefix, before index 0\n    seen = {0: 1}\n    prefix = count = 0\n    for x in nums:\n        prefix += x\n        # 1. earlier prefixes == prefix - k: one match each\n        count += seen.get(prefix - k, 0)\n        # 2. then record this prefix for later indices\n        seen[prefix] = seen.get(prefix, 0) + 1\n    return count\n","cpp":"int subarraySum(const vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n    // prefix sum -> how many times it has appeared;\n    // 0 is the empty prefix, before index 0\n    unordered_map<int, int> seen{{0, 1}};\n    int prefix = 0, count = 0;\n    for (int x : nums) {\n        prefix += x;\n        // 1. earlier prefixes == prefix - k: one match each\n        auto it = seen.find(prefix - k);\n        if (it != seen.end()) count += it->second;\n        // 2. then record this prefix for later indices\n        ++seen[prefix];\n    }\n    return count;\n}\n","java":"class Solution {\n    public int subarraySum(int[] nums, int k) {\n        // prefix sum -> how many times it has appeared;\n        // 0 is the empty prefix, before index 0\n        Map<Integer, Integer> seen = new HashMap<>();\n        seen.put(0, 1);\n        int prefix = 0, count = 0;\n        for (int x : nums) {\n            prefix += x;\n            // 1. earlier prefixes == prefix - k: one match each\n            count += seen.getOrDefault(prefix - k, 0);\n            // 2. then record this prefix for later indices\n            seen.merge(prefix, 1, Integer::sum);\n        }\n        return count;\n    }\n}\n"}},"variants":[{"name":"Range sum queries","when":"The array is fixed and you're asked for the sum of many ranges.","idea":"Build pre once with a leading 0. The sum of nums[left..right] is pre[right + 1] − pre[left], in O(1) per query.","code":{"python":"def build_prefix(nums: list[int]) -> list[int]:\n    # pre[i] = sum of the first i numbers; pre[0] = 0\n    pre = [0] * (len(nums) + 1)\n    for i, x in enumerate(nums):\n        pre[i + 1] = pre[i] + x\n    return pre\n\ndef range_sum(pre: list[int], left: int, right: int) -> int:\n    # sum of nums[left..right], both ends included\n    return pre[right + 1] - pre[left]\n","cpp":"vector<int> buildPrefix(const vector<int>& nums) {\n    // pre[i] = sum of the first i numbers; pre[0] = 0\n    vector<int> pre(nums.size() + 1, 0);\n    for (size_t i = 0; i < nums.size(); ++i) {\n        pre[i + 1] = pre[i] + nums[i];\n    }\n    return pre;\n}\n\nint rangeSum(const vector<int>& pre, int left, int right) {\n    // sum of nums[left..right], both ends included\n    return pre[right + 1] - pre[left];\n}\n","java":"class Solution {\n    public int[] buildPrefix(int[] nums) {\n        // pre[i] = sum of the first i numbers; pre[0] = 0\n        int[] pre = new int[nums.length + 1];\n        for (int i = 0; i < nums.length; i++) {\n            pre[i + 1] = pre[i] + nums[i];\n        }\n        return pre;\n    }\n\n    public int rangeSum(int[] pre, int left, int right) {\n        // sum of nums[left..right], both ends included\n        return pre[right + 1] - pre[left];\n    }\n}\n"},"problemSlug":"range-sum-query-immutable"},{"name":"Longest: store the first index","when":"You want the longest subarray with some sum, not how many.","idea":"Map each prefix to the first index where it appeared, and never overwrite it. When the same prefix comes back at i, the stretch between has sum 0 and length i − first. Recode 0 as −1 to turn \"equal 0s and 1s\" into \"sum 0\".","code":{"python":"def find_max_length(nums: list[int]) -> int:\n    # count 0 as -1, so a balanced stretch sums to 0.\n    # prefix -> the FIRST index where it appeared\n    first = {0: -1}\n    prefix = best = 0\n    for i, x in enumerate(nums):\n        prefix += 1 if x == 1 else -1\n        if prefix in first:\n            # same prefix twice: the part between sums to 0\n            best = max(best, i - first[prefix])\n        else:\n            # keep the first index: it gives the longest\n            first[prefix] = i\n    return best\n","cpp":"int findMaxLength(const vector<int>& nums) {\n    // count 0 as -1, so a balanced stretch sums to 0.\n    // prefix -> the FIRST index where it appeared\n    unordered_map<int, int> first{{0, -1}};\n    int prefix = 0, best = 0;\n    for (int i = 0; i < (int)nums.size(); ++i) {\n        prefix += nums[i] == 1 ? 1 : -1;\n        auto it = first.find(prefix);\n        if (it != first.end()) {\n            // same prefix twice: the part between sums to 0\n            best = max(best, i - it->second);\n        } else {\n            // keep the first index: it gives the longest\n            first[prefix] = i;\n        }\n    }\n    return best;\n}\n","java":"class Solution {\n    public int findMaxLength(int[] nums) {\n        // count 0 as -1, so a balanced stretch sums to 0.\n        // prefix -> the FIRST index where it appeared\n        Map<Integer, Integer> first = new HashMap<>();\n        first.put(0, -1);\n        int prefix = 0, best = 0;\n        for (int i = 0; i < nums.length; i++) {\n            prefix += nums[i] == 1 ? 1 : -1;\n            if (first.containsKey(prefix)) {\n                // same prefix twice: the part between sums to 0\n                best = Math.max(best, i - first.get(prefix));\n            } else {\n                // keep the first index: it gives the longest\n                first.put(prefix, i);\n            }\n        }\n        return best;\n    }\n}\n"},"problemSlug":"contiguous-array"},{"name":"Remainders instead of sums","when":"The subarray sum has to be divisible by k.","idea":"Key the map on prefix mod k. Equal remainders mean the part between is a multiple of k. In C++ and Java, % can be negative, so normalise with ((x % k) + k) % k.","code":{"python":"def subarrays_div_by_k(nums: list[int], k: int) -> int:\n    # prefix remainder -> how many times it has appeared.\n    # equal remainders differ by a multiple of k\n    seen = {0: 1}\n    rem = count = 0\n    for x in nums:\n        # keep the remainder in 0..k-1, even for negatives\n        rem = ((rem + x) % k + k) % k\n        count += seen.get(rem, 0)\n        seen[rem] = seen.get(rem, 0) + 1\n    return count\n","cpp":"int subarraysDivByK(const vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n    // prefix remainder -> how many times it has appeared.\n    // equal remainders differ by a multiple of k\n    unordered_map<int, int> seen{{0, 1}};\n    int rem = 0, count = 0;\n    for (int x : nums) {\n        // keep the remainder in 0..k-1, even for negatives\n        rem = ((rem + x) % k + k) % k;\n        count += seen[rem];\n        ++seen[rem];\n    }\n    return count;\n}\n","java":"class Solution {\n    public int subarraysDivByK(int[] nums, int k) {\n        // prefix remainder -> how many times it has appeared.\n        // equal remainders differ by a multiple of k\n        Map<Integer, Integer> seen = new HashMap<>();\n        seen.put(0, 1);\n        int rem = 0, count = 0;\n        for (int x : nums) {\n            // keep the remainder in 0..k-1, even for negatives\n            rem = ((rem + x) % k + k) % k;\n            count += seen.getOrDefault(rem, 0);\n            seen.merge(rem, 1, Integer::sum);\n        }\n        return count;\n    }\n}\n"},"problemSlug":"subarray-sums-divisible-by-k"}],"complexity":{"time":"O(n) to build or scan; O(1) per range query","space":"O(n) for the prefix array or the map","why":"Each element is added to the running total once, and the map does one lookup and one insert per element at O(1) on average. A query is two array reads and a subtraction."},"notThis":["Every number is positive and you want the shortest or longest window with sum at least k: a sliding window does it in O(1) space.","The array changes between queries: a Fenwick tree or segment tree, since one update would rebuild the whole prefix array.","You need the range maximum or minimum: a monotonic deque for windows, or a sparse table, because max can't be subtracted out."],"ladder":[{"slug":"running-sum-of-1d-array","tier":"warm-up","note":"Build the prefix array and nothing else."},{"slug":"range-sum-query-immutable","tier":"warm-up","note":"pre[r + 1] − pre[l]: the leading zero earns its place."},{"slug":"find-pivot-index","tier":"warm-up","note":"Left sum and right sum from one total, with no array at all."},{"slug":"subarray-sum-equals-k","tier":"core","note":"The template: count earlier prefixes equal to prefix − k."},{"slug":"contiguous-array","tier":"core","note":"Longest instead of count: store the first index, and treat 0 as −1."},{"slug":"subarray-sums-divisible-by-k","tier":"core","note":"Key on the remainder, and handle negative %."},{"slug":"continuous-subarray-sum","tier":"core","note":"Remainders plus first index: the length must be at least 2."},{"slug":"product-of-array-except-self","tier":"core","note":"Prefix and suffix products, multiplied together, with no division."},{"slug":"range-sum-query-2d-immutable","tier":"core","note":"Two dimensions: add two rectangles, subtract the overlap."},{"slug":"car-pooling","tier":"core","note":"A difference array: +p at pickup, −p at drop-off, then one prefix pass."},{"slug":"number-of-submatrices-that-sum-to-target","tier":"stretch","note":"Fix two rows, squash the columns, and run Subarray Sum Equals K."},{"slug":"maximum-sum-of-3-non-overlapping-subarrays","tier":"stretch","note":"Window sums from prefixes, plus the best window to the left and right of each middle one."}]}$json$::jsonb),
('shortest-path',
  'Shortest Path',
  'Shortest Path',
  array['The question asks for the minimum total cost, time or distance from one place to another, and edges cost different amounts.', 'Plain BFS would work if every edge cost the same, but here they don''t.', 'A grid where each move has a price: a height difference, an obstacle to remove, a wait.', 'The cost of a path isn''t a sum but still only gets worse as the path grows: the largest step, or a product of probabilities.', 'There is an extra limit on the path, such as at most k stops, which turns into rounds of relaxation.']::text[],
  'Keep dist[v], the cheapest cost found so far to reach v, with dist[source] = 0 and everything else infinite. Put (0, source) in a min-heap. Pop the smallest entry (d, u). Then relax every edge u → v with weight w: if d + w beats dist[v], lower dist[v] and push (dist[v], v).

The invariant: when a node is popped for the first time, its distance is final. Every entry still in the heap is at least d, and edges never cost less than 0, so no path through another node can come back cheaper. That''s why Dijkstra needs non-negative weights.

A node can sit in the heap several times, once for each time its distance dropped. When an old entry pops with d > dist[u], skip it. Without that check, nodes get processed again and the time blows up. With it, each edge is relaxed once from its final distance, and there are at most E pushes. That gives O((V + E) log V) time.

When weights are only 0 or 1, use a deque instead of a heap: push the 0-edge neighbour to the front and the 1-edge neighbour to the back. The deque stays sorted by distance, so it''s Dijkstra in O(V + E). This is 0-1 BFS.

Dijkstra breaks on negative edges. A node can be popped and finished, and then a negative edge makes it cheaper later. Then use Bellman-Ford: relax every edge V − 1 times, in O(V · E). It also helps when the path may use at most k edges, since each round adds one more edge. A further round that still improves something means there''s a negative cycle.',
  'def network_delay_time(
    times: list[list[int]], n: int, k: int
) -> int:
    graph: list[list[tuple[int, int]]] = [
        [] for _ in range(n + 1)
    ]
    for u, v, w in times:
        graph[u].append((v, w))
    inf = float("inf")
    dist = [inf] * (n + 1)
    dist[k] = 0
    # (distance so far, node); the smallest pops first
    heap = [(0, k)]
    while heap:
        d, u = heapq.heappop(heap)
        # a stale entry: u was already reached more cheaply
        if d > dist[u]:
            continue
        # d is final for u; try every edge out of it
        for v, w in graph[u]:
            if d + w < dist[v]:
                dist[v] = d + w
                heapq.heappush(heap, (dist[v], v))
    best = 0
    for i in range(1, n + 1):
        # a node never reached: the signal can''t get there
        if dist[i] == inf:
            return -1
        best = max(best, dist[i])
    return best
',
  'Time O((V + E) log V) for Dijkstra; O(V + E) for 0-1 BFS; O(k · E) for k rounds of Bellman-Ford · Space O(V + E)',
  array['Using Dijkstra with negative edge weights. A node popped as final can get cheaper later; use Bellman-Ford.', 'Leaving out the stale check (d > dist[u]). Answers stay correct, but old entries make you relax the same edges again and again.', 'Marking a node visited when you push it instead of when you pop it. A cheaper path found later is then ignored.', 'Starting with an infinity that overflows: INT_MAX + w wraps negative. Guard it, or use a large value that leaves room.', 'Forgetting the copy in k-limited Bellman-Ford. Reading this round''s updates lets one round use several edges.', 'Treating a directed graph as undirected, or the reverse. Read whether u → v also means v → u.', 'Returning dist[target] without checking it was ever reached. Unreached nodes keep infinity; the problem usually wants −1.']::text[],
  array['shortest-path-in-binary-matrix', 'network-delay-time', 'path-with-maximum-probability']::text[],
  19,
  $json${"version":1,"summary":"For the cheapest route through a graph whose edges have different costs. Dijkstra settles nodes in order of distance with a min-heap; 0-1 BFS and Bellman-Ford cover the cases it can't.","prerequisites":["graphs-bfs","heap-top-k"],"walkthrough":{"problemSlug":"network-delay-time","input":"times = [[1,2,4],[1,3,1],[3,2,2],[2,4,1],[3,4,5]], n = 4, k = 1","intro":"one step per pop from the heap, which starts as {(0, 1)}.","columns":["Heap after the step","What happened","dist for nodes 1, 2, 3, 4"],"steps":[{"window":"{(1,3), (4,2)}","action":"pop (0,1): node 1 is final. Relax: 2 gets 0+4 = 4, 3 gets 0+1 = 1","best":"0, 4, 1, ∞"},{"window":"{(3,2), (4,2), (6,4)}","action":"pop (1,3): node 3 is final. 2 drops to 1+2 = 3; 4 gets 1+5 = 6","best":"0, 3, 1, 6"},{"window":"{(4,2), (4,4), (6,4)}","action":"pop (3,2): node 2 is final. 4 drops to 3+1 = 4","best":"0, 3, 1, 4"},{"window":"{(4,4), (6,4)}","action":"pop (4,2): stale, because dist[2] is already 3. Skip","best":"0, 3, 1, 4"},{"window":"{(6,4)}","action":"pop (4,4): node 4 is final. It has no edges out","best":"0, 3, 1, 4"},{"window":"{}","action":"pop (6,4): stale, because dist[4] is already 4. Skip","best":"0, 3, 1, 4"}],"result":"4, the largest final distance. The direct edge 1 → 2 costs 4, but the path 1 → 3 → 2 costs 3, and Dijkstra found it because node 3 popped first."},"template":{"note":"Pop the smallest, skip it if stale, relax its edges, push what improved. Change what a path costs (a sum, a max, a product) and how you store the graph, and the loop stays the same.","code":{"python":"def network_delay_time(\n    times: list[list[int]], n: int, k: int\n) -> int:\n    graph: list[list[tuple[int, int]]] = [\n        [] for _ in range(n + 1)\n    ]\n    for u, v, w in times:\n        graph[u].append((v, w))\n    inf = float(\"inf\")\n    dist = [inf] * (n + 1)\n    dist[k] = 0\n    # (distance so far, node); the smallest pops first\n    heap = [(0, k)]\n    while heap:\n        d, u = heapq.heappop(heap)\n        # a stale entry: u was already reached more cheaply\n        if d > dist[u]:\n            continue\n        # d is final for u; try every edge out of it\n        for v, w in graph[u]:\n            if d + w < dist[v]:\n                dist[v] = d + w\n                heapq.heappush(heap, (dist[v], v))\n    best = 0\n    for i in range(1, n + 1):\n        # a node never reached: the signal can't get there\n        if dist[i] == inf:\n            return -1\n        best = max(best, dist[i])\n    return best\n","cpp":"int networkDelayTime(const vector<vector<int>>& times,\n                     int n, int k) {\n    vector<vector<pair<int, int>>> graph(n + 1);\n    for (const auto& t : times) {\n        graph[t[0]].push_back({t[1], t[2]});\n    }\n    vector<int> dist(n + 1, INT_MAX);\n    dist[k] = 0;\n    // (distance so far, node); the smallest pops first\n    using P = pair<int, int>;\n    priority_queue<P, vector<P>, greater<P>> heap;\n    heap.push({0, k});\n    while (!heap.empty()) {\n        auto [d, u] = heap.top();\n        heap.pop();\n        // a stale entry: u was already reached more cheaply\n        if (d > dist[u]) continue;\n        // d is final for u; try every edge out of it\n        for (auto [v, w] : graph[u]) {\n            if (d + w < dist[v]) {\n                dist[v] = d + w;\n                heap.push({dist[v], v});\n            }\n        }\n    }\n    int best = 0;\n    for (int i = 1; i <= n; i++) {\n        // a node never reached: the signal can't get there\n        if (dist[i] == INT_MAX) return -1;\n        best = max(best, dist[i]);\n    }\n    return best;\n}\n","java":"class Solution {\n    public int networkDelayTime(int[][] times, int n, int k) {\n        List<List<int[]>> graph = new ArrayList<>();\n        for (int i = 0; i <= n; i++) {\n            graph.add(new ArrayList<>());\n        }\n        for (int[] t : times) {\n            graph.get(t[0]).add(new int[]{t[1], t[2]});\n        }\n        int[] dist = new int[n + 1];\n        Arrays.fill(dist, Integer.MAX_VALUE);\n        dist[k] = 0;\n        // (distance so far, node); the smallest pops first\n        PriorityQueue<int[]> heap = new PriorityQueue<>(\n            (a, b) -> a[0] != b[0]\n                ? Integer.compare(a[0], b[0])\n                : Integer.compare(a[1], b[1]));\n        heap.offer(new int[]{0, k});\n        while (!heap.isEmpty()) {\n            int[] top = heap.poll();\n            int d = top[0], u = top[1];\n            // a stale entry: u was already reached more cheaply\n            if (d > dist[u]) continue;\n            // d is final for u; try every edge out of it\n            for (int[] e : graph.get(u)) {\n                int v = e[0], w = e[1];\n                if (d + w < dist[v]) {\n                    dist[v] = d + w;\n                    heap.offer(new int[]{dist[v], v});\n                }\n            }\n        }\n        int best = 0;\n        for (int i = 1; i <= n; i++) {\n            // a node never reached: the signal can't get there\n            if (dist[i] == Integer.MAX_VALUE) return -1;\n            best = Math.max(best, dist[i]);\n        }\n        return best;\n    }\n}\n"}},"variants":[{"name":"0-1 BFS","when":"Every move costs 0 or 1: remove an obstacle or not, follow a sign or change it.","idea":"Swap the heap for a deque. A 0-cost neighbour goes to the front and a 1-cost neighbour to the back, so the front always holds the smallest distance. Same relax rule, O(V + E).","code":{"python":"def minimum_obstacles(grid: list[list[int]]) -> int:\n    m, n = len(grid), len(grid[0])\n    inf = float(\"inf\")\n    dist = [[inf] * n for _ in range(m)]\n    dist[0][0] = 0\n    dq = deque([(0, 0)])\n    while dq:\n        r, c = dq.popleft()\n        for dr, dc in ((1, 0), (-1, 0), (0, 1), (0, -1)):\n            nr, nc = r + dr, c + dc\n            if not (0 <= nr < m and 0 <= nc < n):\n                continue\n            # entering an obstacle costs 1, a free cell 0\n            nd = dist[r][c] + grid[nr][nc]\n            if nd < dist[nr][nc]:\n                dist[nr][nc] = nd\n                # cost 0: front of the deque; cost 1: back\n                if grid[nr][nc] == 0:\n                    dq.appendleft((nr, nc))\n                else:\n                    dq.append((nr, nc))\n    return dist[m - 1][n - 1]\n","cpp":"int minimumObstacles(const vector<vector<int>>& grid) {\n    int m = grid.size(), n = grid[0].size();\n    vector<vector<int>> dist(m, vector<int>(n, INT_MAX));\n    dist[0][0] = 0;\n    deque<pair<int, int>> dq;\n    dq.push_back({0, 0});\n    int dr[4] = {1, -1, 0, 0}, dc[4] = {0, 0, 1, -1};\n    while (!dq.empty()) {\n        auto [r, c] = dq.front();\n        dq.pop_front();\n        for (int d = 0; d < 4; d++) {\n            int nr = r + dr[d], nc = c + dc[d];\n            if (nr < 0 || nr >= m || nc < 0 || nc >= n) {\n                continue;\n            }\n            // entering an obstacle costs 1, a free cell 0\n            int nd = dist[r][c] + grid[nr][nc];\n            if (nd < dist[nr][nc]) {\n                dist[nr][nc] = nd;\n                // cost 0: front of the deque; cost 1: back\n                if (grid[nr][nc] == 0) {\n                    dq.push_front({nr, nc});\n                } else {\n                    dq.push_back({nr, nc});\n                }\n            }\n        }\n    }\n    return dist[m - 1][n - 1];\n}\n","java":"class Solution {\n    public int minimumObstacles(int[][] grid) {\n        int m = grid.length, n = grid[0].length;\n        int[][] dist = new int[m][n];\n        for (int[] row : dist) {\n            Arrays.fill(row, Integer.MAX_VALUE);\n        }\n        dist[0][0] = 0;\n        Deque<int[]> dq = new ArrayDeque<>();\n        dq.addLast(new int[]{0, 0});\n        int[] dr = {1, -1, 0, 0}, dc = {0, 0, 1, -1};\n        while (!dq.isEmpty()) {\n            int[] cell = dq.pollFirst();\n            int r = cell[0], c = cell[1];\n            for (int d = 0; d < 4; d++) {\n                int nr = r + dr[d], nc = c + dc[d];\n                if (nr < 0 || nr >= m || nc < 0 || nc >= n) {\n                    continue;\n                }\n                // entering an obstacle costs 1, a free cell 0\n                int nd = dist[r][c] + grid[nr][nc];\n                if (nd < dist[nr][nc]) {\n                    dist[nr][nc] = nd;\n                    // cost 0: front of the deque; cost 1: back\n                    if (grid[nr][nc] == 0) {\n                        dq.addFirst(new int[]{nr, nc});\n                    } else {\n                        dq.addLast(new int[]{nr, nc});\n                    }\n                }\n            }\n        }\n        return dist[m - 1][n - 1];\n    }\n}\n"},"problemSlug":"minimum-obstacle-removal-to-reach-corner"},{"name":"Minimax path","when":"A path is as bad as its worst step: the biggest height jump, the highest water level.","idea":"Use max(d, step) instead of d + step. It still never gets smaller as the path grows, so the first pop of the target is final and you can return there.","code":{"python":"def minimum_effort_path(heights: list[list[int]]) -> int:\n    m, n = len(heights), len(heights[0])\n    inf = float(\"inf\")\n    effort = [[inf] * n for _ in range(m)]\n    effort[0][0] = 0\n    # (worst step on the path so far, row, col)\n    heap = [(0, 0, 0)]\n    while heap:\n        e, r, c = heapq.heappop(heap)\n        if e > effort[r][c]:\n            continue\n        # the first time the corner pops, it is final\n        if r == m - 1 and c == n - 1:\n            return e\n        for dr, dc in ((1, 0), (-1, 0), (0, 1), (0, -1)):\n            nr, nc = r + dr, c + dc\n            if not (0 <= nr < m and 0 <= nc < n):\n                continue\n            step = abs(heights[nr][nc] - heights[r][c])\n            # a path costs its worst step, not the sum\n            ne = max(e, step)\n            if ne < effort[nr][nc]:\n                effort[nr][nc] = ne\n                heapq.heappush(heap, (ne, nr, nc))\n    return 0\n","cpp":"int minimumEffortPath(const vector<vector<int>>& heights) {\n    int m = heights.size(), n = heights[0].size();\n    vector<vector<int>> effort(m, vector<int>(n, INT_MAX));\n    effort[0][0] = 0;\n    // (worst step on the path so far, row, col)\n    using T = tuple<int, int, int>;\n    priority_queue<T, vector<T>, greater<T>> heap;\n    heap.push({0, 0, 0});\n    int dr[4] = {1, -1, 0, 0}, dc[4] = {0, 0, 1, -1};\n    while (!heap.empty()) {\n        auto [e, r, c] = heap.top();\n        heap.pop();\n        if (e > effort[r][c]) continue;\n        // the first time the corner pops, it is final\n        if (r == m - 1 && c == n - 1) return e;\n        for (int d = 0; d < 4; d++) {\n            int nr = r + dr[d], nc = c + dc[d];\n            if (nr < 0 || nr >= m || nc < 0 || nc >= n) {\n                continue;\n            }\n            int step = abs(heights[nr][nc] - heights[r][c]);\n            // a path costs its worst step, not the sum\n            int ne = max(e, step);\n            if (ne < effort[nr][nc]) {\n                effort[nr][nc] = ne;\n                heap.push({ne, nr, nc});\n            }\n        }\n    }\n    return 0;\n}\n","java":"class Solution {\n    public int minimumEffortPath(int[][] heights) {\n        int m = heights.length, n = heights[0].length;\n        int[][] effort = new int[m][n];\n        for (int[] row : effort) {\n            Arrays.fill(row, Integer.MAX_VALUE);\n        }\n        effort[0][0] = 0;\n        // (worst step on the path so far, row, col)\n        PriorityQueue<int[]> heap = new PriorityQueue<>(\n            (a, b) -> Integer.compare(a[0], b[0]));\n        heap.offer(new int[]{0, 0, 0});\n        int[] dr = {1, -1, 0, 0}, dc = {0, 0, 1, -1};\n        while (!heap.isEmpty()) {\n            int[] top = heap.poll();\n            int e = top[0], r = top[1], c = top[2];\n            if (e > effort[r][c]) continue;\n            // the first time the corner pops, it is final\n            if (r == m - 1 && c == n - 1) return e;\n            for (int d = 0; d < 4; d++) {\n                int nr = r + dr[d], nc = c + dc[d];\n                if (nr < 0 || nr >= m || nc < 0 || nc >= n) {\n                    continue;\n                }\n                int step = Math.abs(\n                    heights[nr][nc] - heights[r][c]);\n                // a path costs its worst step, not the sum\n                int ne = Math.max(e, step);\n                if (ne < effort[nr][nc]) {\n                    effort[nr][nc] = ne;\n                    heap.offer(new int[]{ne, nr, nc});\n                }\n            }\n        }\n        return 0;\n    }\n}\n"},"problemSlug":"path-with-minimum-effort"},{"name":"At most k edges: Bellman-Ford rounds","when":"The path may use at most k + 1 edges, or weights can be negative.","idea":"Run k + 1 rounds. In each round, copy last round's costs and relax every edge from the copy. After r rounds, cost[v] is the cheapest path with at most r edges. Without the copy, one round can chain several edges.","code":{"python":"def find_cheapest_price(\n    n: int, flights: list[list[int]], src: int, dst: int, k: int\n) -> int:\n    inf = float(\"inf\")\n    cost = [inf] * n\n    cost[src] = 0\n    # after r rounds: the cheapest using at most r flights\n    for _ in range(k + 1):\n        # copy first, so a round adds at most one flight\n        prev = cost[:]\n        for u, v, w in flights:\n            if prev[u] != inf and prev[u] + w < cost[v]:\n                cost[v] = prev[u] + w\n    return -1 if cost[dst] == inf else cost[dst]\n","cpp":"int findCheapestPrice(int n, const vector<vector<int>>& flights,\n                      int src, int dst, int k) {\n    vector<int> cost(n, INT_MAX);\n    cost[src] = 0;\n    // after r rounds: the cheapest using at most r flights\n    for (int round = 0; round <= k; round++) {\n        // copy first, so a round adds at most one flight\n        vector<int> prev = cost;\n        for (const auto& f : flights) {\n            int u = f[0], v = f[1], w = f[2];\n            if (prev[u] != INT_MAX && prev[u] + w < cost[v]) {\n                cost[v] = prev[u] + w;\n            }\n        }\n    }\n    return cost[dst] == INT_MAX ? -1 : cost[dst];\n}\n","java":"class Solution {\n    public int findCheapestPrice(int n, int[][] flights,\n                                 int src, int dst, int k) {\n        int[] cost = new int[n];\n        Arrays.fill(cost, Integer.MAX_VALUE);\n        cost[src] = 0;\n        // after r rounds: the cheapest using at most r flights\n        for (int round = 0; round <= k; round++) {\n            // copy first, so a round adds at most one flight\n            int[] prev = cost.clone();\n            for (int[] f : flights) {\n                int u = f[0], v = f[1], w = f[2];\n                if (prev[u] != Integer.MAX_VALUE\n                        && prev[u] + w < cost[v]) {\n                    cost[v] = prev[u] + w;\n                }\n            }\n        }\n        return cost[dst] == Integer.MAX_VALUE ? -1 : cost[dst];\n    }\n}\n"},"problemSlug":"cheapest-flights-within-k-stops"}],"complexity":{"time":"O((V + E) log V) for Dijkstra; O(V + E) for 0-1 BFS; O(k · E) for k rounds of Bellman-Ford","space":"O(V + E)","why":"Each successful relax pushes one entry, so the heap gets at most E pushes and each costs O(log E) = O(log V). Stale entries are popped once and skipped. 0-1 BFS does O(1) work per deque operation. Bellman-Ford looks at every edge once per round. All three store the adjacency list and one distance per node."},"notThis":["Every edge costs the same: plain BFS gives shortest steps in O(V + E). See Graph BFS / DFS.","You need distances between every pair of nodes and V is small (a few hundred): Floyd-Warshall, three nested loops in O(V³).","The graph is a DAG: relax edges in topological order in O(V + E), even with negative weights. See Topological Sort.","You only need to know whether two nodes connect, not the cost: Union-Find or a single BFS."],"ladder":[{"slug":"shortest-path-in-binary-matrix","tier":"warm-up","note":"All moves cost 1, so BFS is already a shortest path. The baseline Dijkstra generalises."},{"slug":"network-delay-time","tier":"warm-up","note":"The template: Dijkstra from one source, then the largest distance."},{"slug":"path-with-maximum-probability","tier":"core","note":"Maximise a product of values in [0, 1]: a max-heap, and the path only gets worse as it grows."},{"slug":"path-with-minimum-effort","tier":"core","note":"Dijkstra on a grid where a path costs its worst step."},{"slug":"cheapest-flights-within-k-stops","tier":"core","note":"A limit on edges: k + 1 rounds of Bellman-Ford, reading from a copy."},{"slug":"find-the-city-with-the-smallest-number-of-neighbors-at-a-threshold-distance","tier":"core","note":"All pairs on a small graph: Dijkstra from every node, or Floyd-Warshall."},{"slug":"number-of-ways-to-arrive-at-destination","tier":"core","note":"Count shortest paths: add ways on a tie, reset them on an improvement."},{"slug":"minimum-obstacle-removal-to-reach-corner","tier":"core","note":"Weights of 0 and 1: 0-1 BFS with a deque."},{"slug":"swim-in-rising-water","tier":"stretch","note":"Minimax again: the time is the highest cell on the path."},{"slug":"minimum-cost-to-make-at-least-one-valid-path-in-a-grid","tier":"stretch","note":"Following the arrow costs 0 and changing it costs 1: 0-1 BFS with the costs to work out yourself."},{"slug":"minimum-time-to-visit-a-cell-in-a-grid","tier":"stretch","note":"Waiting is allowed, so the arrival time at a cell depends on parity. Dijkstra with a smarter relax."}]}$json$::jsonb),
('sliding-window',
  'Sliding Window',
  'Sliding Window',
  array['The answer is a contiguous subarray or substring, and the question says longest, shortest, maximum, minimum or how many.', 'There''s a rule the stretch has to satisfy: at most k distinct, no repeats, sum at least target, contains every character of t.', 'A fixed length k is given and you need something about every window of that length.', 'The brute force is two nested loops over start and end. That''s the O(n²) the window removes.']::text[],
  'Keep a window [left, right] and a small summary of what''s inside it: a count map, a running sum, a set. Move right one step at a time to take a new element in. Whenever the window breaks the rule, move left forward until it''s valid again, taking elements out of the summary as they leave.

Neither index ever moves backwards, so each element enters the window once and leaves at most once. That''s why the whole scan is O(n), even though there''s a loop inside a loop.

The shrinking step only works if the rule is monotonic: if a window is invalid, every larger window that contains it is invalid too. "No repeated characters" is like that: adding characters never removes a repeat. When a rule isn''t monotonic (a sum with negative numbers in it), shrinking from the left can skip the answer, and you need a different tool.

Fixed-size windows are the simple case: the window is always k long, so each step adds one element and removes one.',
  'def length_of_longest_substring(s: str) -> int:
    count = {}  # what''s inside the window
    left = best = 0
    for right, ch in enumerate(s):
        # 1. grow: take s[right] in
        count[ch] = count.get(ch, 0) + 1
        # 2. shrink while the window is invalid
        while count[ch] > 1:
            count[s[left]] -= 1
            left += 1
        # 3. valid again: record it
        best = max(best, right - left + 1)
    return best
',
  'Time O(n) · Space O(k), or O(size of the alphabet) for character counts',
  array['Shrinking with if instead of while. One removal isn''t always enough to make the window valid again ("abba" is a quick test).', 'Forgetting to take the left element out of the summary when it leaves: decrement its count, subtract it from the sum.', 'Recording the answer before the window is valid again. Record after the shrink loop.', 'Off-by-one in the length: a window from left to right holds right − left + 1 elements.', 'Leaving zero-count keys in the map when you use the map''s size as "distinct count". Delete them.', 'Reaching for a sum window when the array has negative numbers.']::text[],
  array['maximum-average-subarray-i', 'contains-duplicate-ii', 'maximum-number-of-vowels-in-a-substring-of-given-length']::text[],
  4,
  $json${"version":1,"summary":"For questions about a contiguous stretch of an array or string: the best, longest, shortest or count of subarrays that meet a rule. Two indices walk forward together, so each element is looked at a constant number of times.","prerequisites":["two-pointers"],"walkthrough":{"problemSlug":"longest-substring-without-repeating-characters","input":"s = \"pwwkew\"","intro":"one step per character.","columns":["Window","What happened","Best"],"steps":[{"window":"[p]","action":"take p","best":"1"},{"window":"[p w]","action":"take w","best":"2"},{"window":"p w [w]","action":"take w → w twice; drop p, drop w","best":"2"},{"window":"p w [w k]","action":"take k","best":"2"},{"window":"p w [w k e]","action":"take e","best":"3"},{"window":"p w w [k e w]","action":"take w → w twice; drop w","best":"3"}],"result":"3, for \"wke\" (or \"kew\")"},"template":{"note":"Grow, shrink while invalid, record. Almost every variable-size window problem is these three lines with a different summary and a different rule.","code":{"python":"def length_of_longest_substring(s: str) -> int:\n    count = {}  # what's inside the window\n    left = best = 0\n    for right, ch in enumerate(s):\n        # 1. grow: take s[right] in\n        count[ch] = count.get(ch, 0) + 1\n        # 2. shrink while the window is invalid\n        while count[ch] > 1:\n            count[s[left]] -= 1\n            left += 1\n        # 3. valid again: record it\n        best = max(best, right - left + 1)\n    return best\n","cpp":"int lengthOfLongestSubstring(const string& s) {\n    unordered_map<char, int> count;  // what's inside the window\n    int left = 0, best = 0;\n    for (int right = 0; right < (int)s.size(); ++right) {\n        char c = s[right];\n        // 1. grow: take s[right] in\n        ++count[c];\n        // 2. shrink while the window is invalid\n        while (count[c] > 1) {\n            --count[s[left]];\n            ++left;\n        }\n        // 3. valid again: record it\n        best = max(best, right - left + 1);\n    }\n    return best;\n}\n","java":"class Solution {\n    public int lengthOfLongestSubstring(String s) {\n        // what's inside the window\n        Map<Character, Integer> count = new HashMap<>();\n        int left = 0, best = 0;\n        for (int right = 0; right < s.length(); right++) {\n            char c = s.charAt(right);\n            // 1. grow: take s[right] in\n            count.merge(c, 1, Integer::sum);\n            // 2. shrink while the window is invalid\n            while (count.get(c) > 1) {\n                count.merge(s.charAt(left), -1, Integer::sum);\n                left++;\n            }\n            // 3. valid again: record it\n            best = Math.max(best, right - left + 1);\n        }\n        return best;\n    }\n}\n"}},"variants":[{"name":"Fixed size k","when":"The window length is given.","idea":"Build the first window once, then slide: add nums[right], remove nums[right − k]. No while loop.","code":{"python":"def find_max_average(nums: list[int], k: int) -> float:\n    # the first window, built once\n    window = sum(nums[:k])\n    best = window\n    for right in range(k, len(nums)):\n        # one in, one out\n        window += nums[right] - nums[right - k]\n        best = max(best, window)\n    return best / k\n","cpp":"double findMaxAverage(const vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n    // the first window, built once\n    long long window = 0;\n    for (int i = 0; i < k; ++i) window += nums[i];\n    long long best = window;\n    for (int right = k; right < (int)nums.size(); ++right) {\n        // one in, one out\n        window += nums[right] - nums[right - k];\n        best = max(best, window);\n    }\n    return (double)best / k;\n}\n","java":"class Solution {\n    public double findMaxAverage(int[] nums, int k) {\n        // the first window, built once\n        long window = 0;\n        for (int i = 0; i < k; i++) window += nums[i];\n        long best = window;\n        for (int right = k; right < nums.length; right++) {\n            // one in, one out\n            window += nums[right] - nums[right - k];\n            best = Math.max(best, window);\n        }\n        return (double) best / k;\n    }\n}\n"},"problemSlug":"maximum-average-subarray-i"},{"name":"Count windows: exactly K = at most K − at most K−1","when":"The question asks how many subarrays have exactly k of something.","idea":"Counting \"exactly\" directly doesn't shrink cleanly, but \"at most\" does: for each right, every window from left to right is valid, so add right − left + 1. Then subtract the at-most-(k−1) count.","code":{"python":"def subarrays_with_k_distinct(nums: list[int], k: int) -> int:\n    def at_most(k: int) -> int:\n        count, left, total = {}, 0, 0\n        for right, x in enumerate(nums):\n            count[x] = count.get(x, 0) + 1\n            # too many distinct: shrink\n            while len(count) > k:\n                count[nums[left]] -= 1\n                if count[nums[left]] == 0:\n                    del count[nums[left]]\n                left += 1\n            # every window that ends at right\n            total += right - left + 1\n        return total\n\n    # exactly k = at most k − at most k−1\n    return at_most(k) - at_most(k - 1)\n","cpp":"long long atMost(const vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n    unordered_map<int, int> count;\n    long long total = 0;\n    int left = 0;\n    for (int right = 0; right < (int)nums.size(); ++right) {\n        ++count[nums[right]];\n        // too many distinct: shrink\n        while ((int)count.size() > k) {\n            if (--count[nums[left]] == 0) {\n                count.erase(nums[left]);\n            }\n            ++left;\n        }\n        // every window that ends at right\n        total += right - left + 1;\n    }\n    return total;\n}\n\nint subarraysWithKDistinct(const vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n    // exactly k = at most k − at most k−1\n    return atMost(nums, k) - atMost(nums, k - 1);\n}\n","java":"class Solution {\n    public int subarraysWithKDistinct(int[] nums, int k) {\n        // exactly k = at most k − at most k−1\n        return atMost(nums, k) - atMost(nums, k - 1);\n    }\n\n    private int atMost(int[] nums, int k) {\n        Map<Integer, Integer> count = new HashMap<>();\n        int left = 0, total = 0;\n        for (int right = 0; right < nums.length; right++) {\n            count.merge(nums[right], 1, Integer::sum);\n            // too many distinct: shrink\n            while (count.size() > k) {\n                int x = nums[left];\n                if (count.merge(x, -1, Integer::sum) == 0) {\n                    count.remove(x);\n                }\n                left++;\n            }\n            // every window that ends at right\n            total += right - left + 1;\n        }\n        return total;\n    }\n}\n"},"problemSlug":"subarrays-with-k-different-integers"},{"name":"Max or min of every window","when":"You need the largest (or smallest) value in each window of size k.","idea":"Keep a deque of indices whose values decrease from front to back. A new value pops every smaller one off the back, since they can't be a maximum again while it's in the window. The front is the answer; drop it once it slides out.","code":{"python":"from collections import deque\n\ndef max_sliding_window(nums: list[int], k: int) -> list[int]:\n    # indices; values decrease from front to back\n    dq = deque()\n    out = []\n    for i, x in enumerate(nums):\n        # smaller values can never be a max again\n        while dq and nums[dq[-1]] <= x:\n            dq.pop()\n        dq.append(i)\n        # the front slid out of the window\n        if dq[0] <= i - k:\n            dq.popleft()\n        # the front is this window's max\n        if i >= k - 1:\n            out.append(nums[dq[0]])\n    return out\n","cpp":"vector<int> maxSlidingWindow(const vector<int>& nums, int k) {\n    // indices; values decrease from front to back\n    deque<int> dq;\n    vector<int> out;\n    for (int i = 0; i < (int)nums.size(); ++i) {\n        // smaller values can never be a max again\n        while (!dq.empty() && nums[dq.back()] <= nums[i]) {\n            dq.pop_back();\n        }\n        dq.push_back(i);\n        // the front slid out of the window\n        if (dq.front() <= i - k) dq.pop_front();\n        // the front is this window's max\n        if (i >= k - 1) out.push_back(nums[dq.front()]);\n    }\n    return out;\n}\n","java":"class Solution {\n    public int[] maxSlidingWindow(int[] nums, int k) {\n        // indices; values decrease from front to back\n        Deque<Integer> dq = new ArrayDeque<>();\n        int[] out = new int[nums.length - k + 1];\n        for (int i = 0; i < nums.length; i++) {\n            int x = nums[i];\n            // smaller values can never be a max again\n            while (!dq.isEmpty() && nums[dq.peekLast()] <= x) {\n                dq.pollLast();\n            }\n            dq.offerLast(i);\n            // the front slid out of the window\n            if (dq.peekFirst() <= i - k) dq.pollFirst();\n            // the front is this window's max\n            if (i >= k - 1) {\n                out[i - k + 1] = nums[dq.peekFirst()];\n            }\n        }\n        return out;\n    }\n}\n"},"problemSlug":"sliding-window-maximum"}],"complexity":{"time":"O(n)","space":"O(k), or O(size of the alphabet) for character counts","why":"left and right each move from 0 to n at most once, so all the shrinking across the whole scan adds up to n steps, not n per step. The deque variant is O(n) for the same reason: each index is pushed once and popped at most once."},"notThis":["Subarray sum equals k with negative numbers allowed: shrinking from the left can skip the answer. Use prefix sums with a hash map.","The elements don't have to be contiguous (subsequences): that's usually DP or greedy.","The pair you're looking for can be anywhere in a sorted array: that's two pointers from both ends, not a window."],"ladder":[{"slug":"maximum-average-subarray-i","tier":"warm-up","note":"Fixed window, a running sum. The smallest possible version."},{"slug":"contains-duplicate-ii","tier":"warm-up","note":"Fixed window kept as a set: is there a repeat within k?"},{"slug":"maximum-number-of-vowels-in-a-substring-of-given-length","tier":"warm-up","note":"Fixed window with a count instead of a sum."},{"slug":"longest-substring-without-repeating-characters","tier":"core","note":"The template itself: grow, shrink while there's a repeat, record."},{"slug":"minimum-size-subarray-sum","tier":"core","note":"Shortest instead of longest: record inside the shrink loop."},{"slug":"max-consecutive-ones-iii","tier":"core","note":"The rule is a budget: at most k zeros in the window."},{"slug":"longest-repeating-character-replacement","tier":"core","note":"Window length minus its most common letter must stay ≤ k."},{"slug":"permutation-in-string","tier":"core","note":"Fixed window compared against a target count."},{"slug":"subarray-product-less-than-k","tier":"core","note":"Counting windows: add right − left + 1 each step."},{"slug":"count-number-of-nice-subarrays","tier":"core","note":"Exactly k odds = at most k − at most k−1."},{"slug":"minimum-window-substring","tier":"stretch","note":"Shortest window that covers every needed character, with a counter of what's still missing."},{"slug":"sliding-window-maximum","tier":"stretch","note":"The monotonic deque."},{"slug":"subarrays-with-k-different-integers","tier":"stretch","note":"Exactly K distinct, the at-most trick at full strength."}]}$json$::jsonb),
('stack',
  'Stack',
  'Stack',
  array['Things open and close in nested order: brackets, tags, function calls, "3[a2[c]]".', 'A new item cancels or merges with the item right before it: backspaces, adjacent duplicates, colliding asteroids.', 'You evaluate an expression and need to hold operands until their operator shows up (Reverse Polish notation, a calculator).', 'You must undo or go back to the most recent state, like ".." in a file path.']::text[],
  'A stack holds what hasn''t been dealt with yet, newest on top. Read the input left to right. If an item opens something, push it. If an item closes something, it can only close the newest open item, which is the top: check it, then pop it.

That last-in, first-out rule is the whole reason to use a stack. In "([]{})" the ] has to close the [ that was opened most recently, not the ( from earlier. The top is always the innermost open item, so one comparison is enough.

The invariant: after reading a prefix of the input, the stack holds exactly the items that are open but not closed, in the order they were opened. So the input is valid only if no close ever meets an empty stack or the wrong top, and the stack is empty at the end.

Each item is pushed at most once and popped at most once, so the pass is O(n) even when one step pops several items. Push, pop and top are all O(1).

A plain stack isn''t enough when you need the oldest pending item (that''s a queue), or when you need the min or max of what''s waiting without popping. Min Stack stores the minimum next to each value. For "next greater element" kinds of questions, keep the stack sorted: that''s the Monotonic Stack primer.',
  'def is_valid(s: str) -> bool:
    # each closer and the opener it needs
    pairs = {")": "(", "]": "[", "}": "{"}
    stack: list[str] = []
    for ch in s:
        if ch not in pairs:
            # an opener: wait for its closer
            stack.append(ch)
        elif not stack or stack[-1] != pairs[ch]:
            # nothing open, or the wrong kind is open
            return False
        else:
            # closes the most recent open bracket
            stack.pop()
    # anything still open was never closed
    return not stack
',
  'Time O(n) · Space O(n) for the stack in the worst case',
  array['Reading or popping the top without checking that the stack isn''t empty ("]" on its own).', 'Returning true at the end without checking the stack is empty, so "((" passes.', 'Popping operands in the wrong order. For a − b and a / b, the first value popped is b.', 'Rounding division the wrong way. RPN wants toward zero; Python''s // rounds down, so −7 // 2 is −4, not −3.', 'Parsing "-11" as a minus operator. Compare the whole token, not its first character.', 'Using java.util.Stack, or comparing boxed Characters with ==. Use ArrayDeque, and equals or unboxed chars.', 'Forgetting multi-digit numbers in Decode String: build the count as num × 10 + digit.']::text[],
  array['baseball-game', 'remove-all-adjacent-duplicates-in-string', 'backspace-string-compare']::text[],
  6,
  $json${"version":1,"summary":"Push things that are still waiting to be resolved, and pop them when something arrives that resolves the most recent one. Anything nested, or anything where the newest open item has to close first, fits this shape.","prerequisites":["arrays-hashing"],"walkthrough":{"problemSlug":"valid-parentheses","input":"s = \"([]{})\"","intro":"one step per character.","columns":["Stack (bottom to top)","What happened","Still open"],"steps":[{"window":"(","action":"( opens: push","best":"1"},{"window":"( [","action":"[ opens: push","best":"2"},{"window":"(","action":"] needs [, top is [: pop","best":"1"},{"window":"( {","action":"{ opens: push","best":"2"},{"window":"(","action":"} needs {, top is {: pop","best":"1"},{"window":"empty","action":") needs (, top is (: pop","best":"0"}],"result":"true: no close ever met the wrong top, and nothing is left open."},"template":{"note":"Push what opens, check and pop the top on what closes, and look at what's left at the end. The stack problems below change what gets pushed and what a pop means.","code":{"python":"def is_valid(s: str) -> bool:\n    # each closer and the opener it needs\n    pairs = {\")\": \"(\", \"]\": \"[\", \"}\": \"{\"}\n    stack: list[str] = []\n    for ch in s:\n        if ch not in pairs:\n            # an opener: wait for its closer\n            stack.append(ch)\n        elif not stack or stack[-1] != pairs[ch]:\n            # nothing open, or the wrong kind is open\n            return False\n        else:\n            # closes the most recent open bracket\n            stack.pop()\n    # anything still open was never closed\n    return not stack\n","cpp":"bool isValid(const string& s) {\n    // each closer and the opener it needs\n    unordered_map<char, char> pairs = {\n        {')', '('}, {']', '['}, {'}', '{'}};\n    vector<char> stack;\n    for (char ch : s) {\n        if (!pairs.count(ch)) {\n            // an opener: wait for its closer\n            stack.push_back(ch);\n        } else if (stack.empty() || stack.back() != pairs[ch]) {\n            // nothing open, or the wrong kind is open\n            return false;\n        } else {\n            // closes the most recent open bracket\n            stack.pop_back();\n        }\n    }\n    // anything still open was never closed\n    return stack.empty();\n}\n","java":"class Solution {\n    public boolean isValid(String s) {\n        // each closer and the opener it needs\n        Map<Character, Character> pairs =\n            Map.of(')', '(', ']', '[', '}', '{');\n        Deque<Character> stack = new ArrayDeque<>();\n        for (char ch : s.toCharArray()) {\n            if (!pairs.containsKey(ch)) {\n                // an opener: wait for its closer\n                stack.push(ch);\n            } else if (stack.isEmpty()\n                    || !stack.peek().equals(pairs.get(ch))) {\n                // nothing open, or the wrong kind is open\n                return false;\n            } else {\n                // closes the most recent open bracket\n                stack.pop();\n            }\n        }\n        // anything still open was never closed\n        return stack.isEmpty();\n    }\n}\n"}},"variants":[{"name":"Operands wait for their operator","when":"You evaluate postfix (Reverse Polish) expressions, or any expression where an operator comes after its inputs.","idea":"Push numbers. On an operator, pop twice: the first pop is the right operand, the second the left. Push the result back. One value is left at the end.","code":{"python":"def eval_rpn(tokens: list[str]) -> int:\n    stack: list[int] = []\n    for tok in tokens:\n        if tok not in (\"+\", \"-\", \"*\", \"/\"):\n            stack.append(int(tok))\n            continue\n        # the top is the RIGHT operand: pop it first\n        b = stack.pop()\n        a = stack.pop()\n        if tok == \"+\":\n            stack.append(a + b)\n        elif tok == \"-\":\n            stack.append(a - b)\n        elif tok == \"*\":\n            stack.append(a * b)\n        else:\n            # divide, rounding toward zero\n            stack.append(int(a / b))\n    return stack[-1]\n","cpp":"int evalRPN(const vector<string>& tokens) {\n    vector<int> stack;\n    for (const string& tok : tokens) {\n        bool isOp = tok == \"+\" || tok == \"-\"\n                    || tok == \"*\" || tok == \"/\";\n        if (!isOp) {\n            stack.push_back(stoi(tok));\n            continue;\n        }\n        // the top is the RIGHT operand: pop it first\n        int b = stack.back();\n        stack.pop_back();\n        int a = stack.back();\n        stack.pop_back();\n        if (tok == \"+\") {\n            stack.push_back(a + b);\n        } else if (tok == \"-\") {\n            stack.push_back(a - b);\n        } else if (tok == \"*\") {\n            stack.push_back(a * b);\n        } else {\n            // divide, rounding toward zero\n            stack.push_back(a / b);\n        }\n    }\n    return stack.back();\n}\n","java":"class Solution {\n    public int evalRPN(String[] tokens) {\n        Deque<Integer> stack = new ArrayDeque<>();\n        for (String tok : tokens) {\n            if (!List.of(\"+\", \"-\", \"*\", \"/\").contains(tok)) {\n                stack.push(Integer.parseInt(tok));\n                continue;\n            }\n            // the top is the RIGHT operand: pop it first\n            int b = stack.pop();\n            int a = stack.pop();\n            if (tok.equals(\"+\")) {\n                stack.push(a + b);\n            } else if (tok.equals(\"-\")) {\n                stack.push(a - b);\n            } else if (tok.equals(\"*\")) {\n                stack.push(a * b);\n            } else {\n                // divide, rounding toward zero\n                stack.push(a / b);\n            }\n        }\n        return stack.peek();\n    }\n}\n"},"problemSlug":"evaluate-reverse-polish-notation"},{"name":"Save the outer state on open","when":"Nested structure where the inside has to be finished before the outside can continue, like \"3[a2[c]]\".","idea":"On [, push the text built so far and the repeat count, then start fresh. On ], pop them and set the text to the saved text plus the inside repeated. The stack remembers where each level left off.","code":{"python":"def decode_string(s: str) -> str:\n    # one entry each per open \"[\": the text before it,\n    # and how many times its inside repeats\n    strs: list[str] = []\n    counts: list[int] = []\n    cur, num = \"\", 0\n    for ch in s:\n        if ch.isdigit():\n            num = num * 10 + int(ch)\n        elif ch == \"[\":\n            # save where we are, start a fresh string\n            strs.append(cur)\n            counts.append(num)\n            cur, num = \"\", 0\n        elif ch == \"]\":\n            # saved text, then the inside repeated\n            cur = strs.pop() + cur * counts.pop()\n        else:\n            cur += ch\n    return cur\n","cpp":"string decodeString(const string& s) {\n    // one entry each per open \"[\": the text before it,\n    // and how many times its inside repeats\n    vector<string> strs;\n    vector<int> counts;\n    string cur;\n    int num = 0;\n    for (char ch : s) {\n        if (isdigit(ch)) {\n            num = num * 10 + (ch - '0');\n        } else if (ch == '[') {\n            // save where we are, start a fresh string\n            strs.push_back(cur);\n            counts.push_back(num);\n            cur = \"\";\n            num = 0;\n        } else if (ch == ']') {\n            // saved text, then the inside repeated\n            string out = strs.back();\n            strs.pop_back();\n            for (int i = 0; i < counts.back(); ++i) out += cur;\n            counts.pop_back();\n            cur = out;\n        } else {\n            cur += ch;\n        }\n    }\n    return cur;\n}\n","java":"class Solution {\n    public String decodeString(String s) {\n        // one entry each per open \"[\": the text before it,\n        // and how many times its inside repeats\n        Deque<String> strs = new ArrayDeque<>();\n        Deque<Integer> counts = new ArrayDeque<>();\n        String cur = \"\";\n        int num = 0;\n        for (char ch : s.toCharArray()) {\n            if (Character.isDigit(ch)) {\n                num = num * 10 + (ch - '0');\n            } else if (ch == '[') {\n                // save where we are, start a fresh string\n                strs.push(cur);\n                counts.push(num);\n                cur = \"\";\n                num = 0;\n            } else if (ch == ']') {\n                // saved text, then the inside repeated\n                cur = strs.pop() + cur.repeat(counts.pop());\n            } else {\n                cur += ch;\n            }\n        }\n        return cur;\n    }\n}\n"},"problemSlug":"decode-string"},{"name":"New item fights the top","when":"A new item can cancel the most recent survivor, and maybe the one before that too.","idea":"A left-moving asteroid hits right-moving ones on top. While it's bigger, pop and keep going. If equal, pop and stop. If smaller, it dies. Push it only if it survived.","code":{"python":"def asteroid_collision(asteroids: list[int]) -> list[int]:\n    # survivors so far, left to right\n    stack: list[int] = []\n    for a in asteroids:\n        alive = True\n        # a moves left into one moving right: they collide\n        while alive and a < 0 and stack and stack[-1] > 0:\n            if stack[-1] < -a:\n                # smaller top explodes; a keeps going\n                stack.pop()\n                continue\n            if stack[-1] == -a:\n                # same size: both explode\n                stack.pop()\n            alive = False\n        if alive:\n            stack.append(a)\n    return stack\n","cpp":"vector<int> asteroidCollision(const vector<int>& asteroids) {\n    // survivors so far, left to right\n    vector<int> stack;\n    for (int a : asteroids) {\n        bool alive = true;\n        // a moves left into one moving right: they collide\n        while (alive && a < 0 && !stack.empty()\n               && stack.back() > 0) {\n            if (stack.back() < -a) {\n                // smaller top explodes; a keeps going\n                stack.pop_back();\n                continue;\n            }\n            if (stack.back() == -a) {\n                // same size: both explode\n                stack.pop_back();\n            }\n            alive = false;\n        }\n        if (alive) stack.push_back(a);\n    }\n    return stack;\n}\n","java":"class Solution {\n    public int[] asteroidCollision(int[] asteroids) {\n        // survivors so far, left to right\n        Deque<Integer> stack = new ArrayDeque<>();\n        for (int a : asteroids) {\n            boolean alive = true;\n            // a moves left into one moving right: they collide\n            while (alive && a < 0 && !stack.isEmpty()\n                   && stack.peekLast() > 0) {\n                if (stack.peekLast() < -a) {\n                    // smaller top explodes; a keeps going\n                    stack.pollLast();\n                    continue;\n                }\n                if (stack.peekLast() == -a) {\n                    // same size: both explode\n                    stack.pollLast();\n                }\n                alive = false;\n            }\n            if (alive) stack.offerLast(a);\n        }\n        return stack.stream().mapToInt(x -> x).toArray();\n    }\n}\n"},"problemSlug":"asteroid-collision"}],"complexity":{"time":"O(n)","space":"O(n) for the stack in the worst case","why":"Each item is pushed at most once and popped at most once, so the total work across all the while loops is at most 2n stack operations. The worst case for space is input that only opens, like \"((((\"."},"notThis":["You need the next greater or smaller element for every index: keep the stack in sorted order, see Monotonic Stack.","Items must come out in the order they arrived (first in, first out): use a queue, or BFS.","You only need to know whether brackets balance with a single kind of bracket: a counter is enough, no stack needed."],"ladder":[{"slug":"baseball-game","tier":"warm-up","note":"Push scores; \"C\" pops, \"D\" and \"+\" read the top one or two."},{"slug":"remove-all-adjacent-duplicates-in-string","tier":"warm-up","note":"A new character cancels the top if it matches."},{"slug":"backspace-string-compare","tier":"warm-up","note":"# pops the last character: build both strings with a stack."},{"slug":"valid-parentheses","tier":"core","note":"The template: push opens, match and pop on closes."},{"slug":"min-stack","tier":"core","note":"Store the minimum so far next to every value."},{"slug":"evaluate-reverse-polish-notation","tier":"core","note":"Operands wait on the stack; pop order matters for − and /."},{"slug":"simplify-path","tier":"core","note":"Split on /; \"..\" pops, \".\" and empty parts do nothing."},{"slug":"asteroid-collision","tier":"core","note":"One new item can pop several old ones, or die itself."},{"slug":"decode-string","tier":"core","note":"Push the outer state on [, rebuild it on ]."},{"slug":"minimum-remove-to-make-valid-parentheses","tier":"core","note":"Push indices of ( so you know which ones to delete at the end."},{"slug":"basic-calculator","tier":"stretch","note":"Push the running result and sign on (, combine them on )."},{"slug":"longest-valid-parentheses","tier":"stretch","note":"Keep the index before the current valid run at the bottom of the stack."}]}$json$::jsonb),
('topological-sort',
  'Topological Sort',
  'Topological Sort',
  array['The input is a list of pairs meaning "a needs b first": prerequisites, dependencies, ingredients.', 'The question asks for a valid order, or whether all tasks can be finished at all.', 'The edges are directed and a cycle would make the answer impossible.', 'Each task takes time, things can run in parallel, and you want the earliest finish: the longest path through the dependencies.']::text[],
  'Draw each task as a node and each rule "b before a" as an edge b → a. A node''s in-degree is the number of edges coming into it: how many tasks it is still waiting for. A task with in-degree 0 is free to do right now.

Kahn''s algorithm keeps a queue of free tasks. Take one out, add it to the order, and remove its outgoing edges by lowering each neighbour''s in-degree by one. Any neighbour that drops to 0 has nothing left to wait for, so it joins the queue. Repeat until the queue is empty.

The invariant is that a task joins the queue only after every task it depends on is already in the order. So the order is always valid. It is fast because each node is queued once and each edge is removed once: O(V + E).

The same loop detects cycles for free. Tasks on a cycle wait on each other, so their in-degree never reaches 0 and they never enter the queue. If the order ends with fewer than n tasks, there is a cycle and no valid order exists.

DFS can do the same job: mark each node as "on the current path" while you explore it and "done" after. Reaching a node that is still on the path means a cycle. A plain visited flag is not enough for directed graphs, because two paths that meet again (a diamond) are not a cycle.',
  'def find_order(num_courses: int,
               prerequisites: list[list[int]]) -> list[int]:
    graph: list[list[int]] = [[] for _ in range(num_courses)]
    indegree = [0] * num_courses
    # [a, b] means take b before a: an edge b -> a
    for a, b in prerequisites:
        graph[b].append(a)
        indegree[a] += 1
    # start with every course that needs nothing
    queue = deque(i for i in range(num_courses)
                  if indegree[i] == 0)
    order: list[int] = []
    while queue:
        u = queue.popleft()
        order.append(u)
        for v in graph[u]:
            # u is done, so v waits on one course fewer
            indegree[v] -= 1
            if indegree[v] == 0:
                queue.append(v)
    # a cycle keeps its courses above 0 forever
    if len(order) < num_courses:
        return []
    return order
',
  'Time O(V + E) · Space O(V + E) for the adjacency list, plus O(V) for counts and the queue',
  array['Getting the edge direction backwards. For [a, b] meaning b before a, the edge is b → a and a''s in-degree goes up.', 'Forgetting the cycle check: return the order only if it holds all n nodes.', 'Starting from one node only. Every node with in-degree 0 goes in the queue at the start, including ones with no edges at all.', 'Using a single visited flag for DFS cycle detection on a directed graph, which calls a diamond a cycle. Use three states.', 'Lowering in-degrees but queueing a node before it reaches exactly 0.', 'Missing self-loops like [0, 0]. They count as a cycle, and Kahn''s handles them only if the edge is added like any other.']::text[],
  array['find-the-town-judge', 'course-schedule', 'course-schedule-ii']::text[],
  17,
  $json${"version":1,"summary":"For tasks with \"do this before that\" rules: courses and prerequisites, build steps, recipes and ingredients. Put the tasks in an order where every task comes after everything it depends on, or find out that a cycle makes this impossible.","prerequisites":["graphs-bfs"],"walkthrough":{"problemSlug":"course-schedule-ii","input":"numCourses = 6, prerequisites = [[1,0], [2,0], [3,1], [3,2], [4,3], [4,5]]","intro":"one step per course taken from the queue, after counting in-degrees. [a, b] is the edge b → a.","columns":["Queue","What happened","Order so far"],"steps":[{"window":"[0, 5]","action":"in-degrees are 0:0, 1:1, 2:1, 3:2, 4:2, 5:0; queue the two courses that need nothing","best":"[]"},{"window":"[5, 1, 2]","action":"take 0; 1 and 2 drop to 0, so both join the queue","best":"[0]"},{"window":"[1, 2]","action":"take 5; 4 drops from 2 to 1 and keeps waiting","best":"[0, 5]"},{"window":"[2]","action":"take 1; 3 drops from 2 to 1","best":"[0, 5, 1]"},{"window":"[3]","action":"take 2; 3 drops to 0 and joins","best":"[0, 5, 1, 2]"},{"window":"[4]","action":"take 3; 4 drops to 0 and joins","best":"[0, 5, 1, 2, 3]"},{"window":"[]","action":"take 4; queue empty, 6 of 6 courses placed, so there is no cycle","best":"[0, 5, 1, 2, 3, 4]"}],"result":"[0, 5, 1, 2, 3, 4]. Course 3 waited until both 1 and 2 were done; other valid orders exist and are also accepted."},"template":{"note":"Build the edges and in-degrees, queue every in-degree 0 node, then take, append and lower the neighbours' counts, queueing any that reach 0. If the order is shorter than n, there was a cycle.","code":{"python":"def find_order(num_courses: int,\n               prerequisites: list[list[int]]) -> list[int]:\n    graph: list[list[int]] = [[] for _ in range(num_courses)]\n    indegree = [0] * num_courses\n    # [a, b] means take b before a: an edge b -> a\n    for a, b in prerequisites:\n        graph[b].append(a)\n        indegree[a] += 1\n    # start with every course that needs nothing\n    queue = deque(i for i in range(num_courses)\n                  if indegree[i] == 0)\n    order: list[int] = []\n    while queue:\n        u = queue.popleft()\n        order.append(u)\n        for v in graph[u]:\n            # u is done, so v waits on one course fewer\n            indegree[v] -= 1\n            if indegree[v] == 0:\n                queue.append(v)\n    # a cycle keeps its courses above 0 forever\n    if len(order) < num_courses:\n        return []\n    return order\n","cpp":"vector<int> findOrder(int numCourses,\n                      vector<vector<int>>& prerequisites) {\n    vector<vector<int>> graph(numCourses);\n    vector<int> indegree(numCourses, 0);\n    // [a, b] means take b before a: an edge b -> a\n    for (auto& p : prerequisites) {\n        graph[p[1]].push_back(p[0]);\n        indegree[p[0]]++;\n    }\n    // start with every course that needs nothing\n    queue<int> q;\n    for (int i = 0; i < numCourses; i++) {\n        if (indegree[i] == 0) q.push(i);\n    }\n    vector<int> order;\n    while (!q.empty()) {\n        int u = q.front();\n        q.pop();\n        order.push_back(u);\n        for (int v : graph[u]) {\n            // u is done, so v waits on one course fewer\n            indegree[v]--;\n            if (indegree[v] == 0) q.push(v);\n        }\n    }\n    // a cycle keeps its courses above 0 forever\n    if ((int)order.size() < numCourses) return {};\n    return order;\n}\n","java":"class Solution {\n    public int[] findOrder(int numCourses,\n                           int[][] prerequisites) {\n        List<List<Integer>> graph = new ArrayList<>();\n        for (int i = 0; i < numCourses; i++) {\n            graph.add(new ArrayList<>());\n        }\n        int[] indegree = new int[numCourses];\n        // [a, b] means take b before a: an edge b -> a\n        for (int[] p : prerequisites) {\n            graph.get(p[1]).add(p[0]);\n            indegree[p[0]]++;\n        }\n        // start with every course that needs nothing\n        Deque<Integer> queue = new ArrayDeque<>();\n        for (int i = 0; i < numCourses; i++) {\n            if (indegree[i] == 0) queue.add(i);\n        }\n        int[] order = new int[numCourses];\n        int taken = 0;\n        while (!queue.isEmpty()) {\n            int u = queue.poll();\n            order[taken++] = u;\n            for (int v : graph.get(u)) {\n                // u is done, so v waits on one course fewer\n                indegree[v]--;\n                if (indegree[v] == 0) queue.add(v);\n            }\n        }\n        // a cycle keeps its courses above 0 forever\n        if (taken < numCourses) return new int[0];\n        return order;\n    }\n}\n"}},"variants":[{"name":"Kahn's on the reversed graph","when":"A node is good only if everything it points to is good, starting from nodes with no way out.","idea":"Flip every edge and use out-degree in place of in-degree. Terminal nodes start in the queue. When a node's last outgoing edge leads to a safe node, its count hits 0 and it is safe too. Nodes on or leading into a cycle never get there.","code":{"python":"def eventual_safe_nodes(graph: list[list[int]]) -> list[int]:\n    n = len(graph)\n    # flip every edge: parents[v] lists who points at v\n    parents: list[list[int]] = [[] for _ in range(n)]\n    outdegree = [0] * n\n    for u in range(n):\n        outdegree[u] = len(graph[u])\n        for v in graph[u]:\n            parents[v].append(u)\n    # nodes with no way out are safe; peel back from them\n    queue = deque(u for u in range(n) if outdegree[u] == 0)\n    safe = [False] * n\n    while queue:\n        v = queue.popleft()\n        safe[v] = True\n        for u in parents[v]:\n            outdegree[u] -= 1\n            # every edge out of u now leads to a safe node\n            if outdegree[u] == 0:\n                queue.append(u)\n    return [u for u in range(n) if safe[u]]\n","cpp":"vector<int> eventualSafeNodes(vector<vector<int>>& graph) {\n    int n = graph.size();\n    // flip every edge: parents[v] lists who points at v\n    vector<vector<int>> parents(n);\n    vector<int> outdegree(n, 0);\n    for (int u = 0; u < n; u++) {\n        outdegree[u] = graph[u].size();\n        for (int v : graph[u]) parents[v].push_back(u);\n    }\n    // nodes with no way out are safe; peel back from them\n    queue<int> q;\n    for (int u = 0; u < n; u++) {\n        if (outdegree[u] == 0) q.push(u);\n    }\n    vector<bool> safe(n, false);\n    while (!q.empty()) {\n        int v = q.front();\n        q.pop();\n        safe[v] = true;\n        for (int u : parents[v]) {\n            outdegree[u]--;\n            // every edge out of u now leads to a safe node\n            if (outdegree[u] == 0) q.push(u);\n        }\n    }\n    vector<int> result;\n    for (int u = 0; u < n; u++) {\n        if (safe[u]) result.push_back(u);\n    }\n    return result;\n}\n","java":"class Solution {\n    public List<Integer> eventualSafeNodes(int[][] graph) {\n        int n = graph.length;\n        // flip every edge: parents[v] lists who points at v\n        List<List<Integer>> parents = new ArrayList<>();\n        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {\n            parents.add(new ArrayList<>());\n        }\n        int[] outdegree = new int[n];\n        for (int u = 0; u < n; u++) {\n            outdegree[u] = graph[u].length;\n            for (int v : graph[u]) parents.get(v).add(u);\n        }\n        // nodes with no way out are safe; peel back from them\n        Deque<Integer> queue = new ArrayDeque<>();\n        for (int u = 0; u < n; u++) {\n            if (outdegree[u] == 0) queue.add(u);\n        }\n        boolean[] safe = new boolean[n];\n        while (!queue.isEmpty()) {\n            int v = queue.poll();\n            safe[v] = true;\n            for (int u : parents.get(v)) {\n                outdegree[u]--;\n                // every edge out of u now leads to a safe node\n                if (outdegree[u] == 0) queue.add(u);\n            }\n        }\n        List<Integer> result = new ArrayList<>();\n        for (int u = 0; u < n; u++) {\n            if (safe[u]) result.add(u);\n        }\n        return result;\n    }\n}\n"},"problemSlug":"find-eventual-safe-states"},{"name":"DFS with three states","when":"You only need to know whether a cycle exists, and DFS feels more natural.","idea":"Give each node a state: 0 not visited, 1 on the current path, 2 done. Meeting a 1 means you came back around: a cycle. Meeting a 2 is safe; that node was fully checked. Set 1 on entry and 2 on exit.","code":{"python":"def can_finish(num_courses: int,\n               prerequisites: list[list[int]]) -> bool:\n    graph: list[list[int]] = [[] for _ in range(num_courses)]\n    for a, b in prerequisites:\n        graph[b].append(a)\n    # 0 = not visited, 1 = on the current path, 2 = done\n    state = [0] * num_courses\n\n    # True if a cycle can be reached from u\n    def has_cycle(u: int) -> bool:\n        if state[u] == 1:\n            # back on the current path: a cycle\n            return True\n        if state[u] == 2:\n            # fully checked before: no cycle through here\n            return False\n        state[u] = 1\n        for v in graph[u]:\n            if has_cycle(v):\n                return True\n        state[u] = 2\n        return False\n\n    for u in range(num_courses):\n        if has_cycle(u):\n            return False\n    return True\n","cpp":"// true if a cycle can be reached from u\nbool hasCycle(int u, vector<vector<int>>& graph,\n              vector<int>& state) {\n    if (state[u] == 1) {\n        // back on the current path: a cycle\n        return true;\n    }\n    if (state[u] == 2) {\n        // fully checked before: no cycle through here\n        return false;\n    }\n    state[u] = 1;\n    for (int v : graph[u]) {\n        if (hasCycle(v, graph, state)) return true;\n    }\n    state[u] = 2;\n    return false;\n}\n\nbool canFinish(int numCourses,\n               vector<vector<int>>& prerequisites) {\n    vector<vector<int>> graph(numCourses);\n    for (auto& p : prerequisites) graph[p[1]].push_back(p[0]);\n    // 0 = not visited, 1 = on the current path, 2 = done\n    vector<int> state(numCourses, 0);\n    for (int u = 0; u < numCourses; u++) {\n        if (hasCycle(u, graph, state)) return false;\n    }\n    return true;\n}\n","java":"class Solution {\n    private List<List<Integer>> graph;\n    // 0 = not visited, 1 = on the current path, 2 = done\n    private int[] state;\n\n    public boolean canFinish(int numCourses,\n                             int[][] prerequisites) {\n        graph = new ArrayList<>();\n        for (int i = 0; i < numCourses; i++) {\n            graph.add(new ArrayList<>());\n        }\n        for (int[] p : prerequisites) graph.get(p[1]).add(p[0]);\n        state = new int[numCourses];\n        for (int u = 0; u < numCourses; u++) {\n            if (hasCycle(u)) return false;\n        }\n        return true;\n    }\n\n    // true if a cycle can be reached from u\n    private boolean hasCycle(int u) {\n        if (state[u] == 1) {\n            // back on the current path: a cycle\n            return true;\n        }\n        if (state[u] == 2) {\n            // fully checked before: no cycle through here\n            return false;\n        }\n        state[u] = 1;\n        for (int v : graph.get(u)) {\n            if (hasCycle(v)) return true;\n        }\n        state[u] = 2;\n        return false;\n    }\n}\n"},"problemSlug":"course-schedule"},{"name":"Longest path in the order","when":"Tasks take time, run in parallel, and each must wait for all its prerequisites.","idea":"Run Kahn's and carry start[v], the earliest time v can begin. When u finishes at start[u] + time[u], push that into each neighbour: start[v] = max(start[v], finish). The answer is the largest finish time.","code":{"python":"def minimum_time(n: int, relations: list[list[int]],\n                 time: list[int]) -> int:\n    graph: list[list[int]] = [[] for _ in range(n)]\n    indegree = [0] * n\n    # courses are 1..n; [a, b] means a before b\n    for a, b in relations:\n        graph[a - 1].append(b - 1)\n        indegree[b - 1] += 1\n    # start[v]: the earliest month v can begin\n    start = [0] * n\n    queue = deque(i for i in range(n) if indegree[i] == 0)\n    best = 0\n    while queue:\n        u = queue.popleft()\n        finish = start[u] + time[u]\n        best = max(best, finish)\n        for v in graph[u]:\n            # v waits for its slowest prerequisite\n            start[v] = max(start[v], finish)\n            indegree[v] -= 1\n            if indegree[v] == 0:\n                queue.append(v)\n    return best\n","cpp":"int minimumTime(int n, vector<vector<int>>& relations,\n                vector<int>& time) {\n    vector<vector<int>> graph(n);\n    vector<int> indegree(n, 0);\n    // courses are 1..n; [a, b] means a before b\n    for (auto& r : relations) {\n        graph[r[0] - 1].push_back(r[1] - 1);\n        indegree[r[1] - 1]++;\n    }\n    // start[v]: the earliest month v can begin\n    vector<int> start(n, 0);\n    queue<int> q;\n    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {\n        if (indegree[i] == 0) q.push(i);\n    }\n    int best = 0;\n    while (!q.empty()) {\n        int u = q.front();\n        q.pop();\n        int finish = start[u] + time[u];\n        best = max(best, finish);\n        for (int v : graph[u]) {\n            // v waits for its slowest prerequisite\n            start[v] = max(start[v], finish);\n            indegree[v]--;\n            if (indegree[v] == 0) q.push(v);\n        }\n    }\n    return best;\n}\n","java":"class Solution {\n    public int minimumTime(int n, int[][] relations,\n                           int[] time) {\n        List<List<Integer>> graph = new ArrayList<>();\n        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {\n            graph.add(new ArrayList<>());\n        }\n        int[] indegree = new int[n];\n        // courses are 1..n; [a, b] means a before b\n        for (int[] r : relations) {\n            graph.get(r[0] - 1).add(r[1] - 1);\n            indegree[r[1] - 1]++;\n        }\n        // start[v]: the earliest month v can begin\n        int[] start = new int[n];\n        Deque<Integer> queue = new ArrayDeque<>();\n        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {\n            if (indegree[i] == 0) queue.add(i);\n        }\n        int best = 0;\n        while (!queue.isEmpty()) {\n            int u = queue.poll();\n            int finish = start[u] + time[u];\n            best = Math.max(best, finish);\n            for (int v : graph.get(u)) {\n                // v waits for its slowest prerequisite\n                start[v] = Math.max(start[v], finish);\n                indegree[v]--;\n                if (indegree[v] == 0) queue.add(v);\n            }\n        }\n        return best;\n    }\n}\n"},"problemSlug":"parallel-courses-iii"}],"complexity":{"time":"O(V + E)","space":"O(V + E) for the adjacency list, plus O(V) for counts and the queue","why":"Building the graph reads every edge once. In the loop, each node enters the queue at most once, and each edge lowers one in-degree exactly once when its source is taken out. DFS with three states also visits each node and edge once."},"notThis":["The edges are undirected: there is no \"before\". To find groups use Graph BFS / DFS or Union-Find.","You need the shortest path in a weighted graph with cycles: use Dijkstra. See Shortest Path.","The question is only whether two items end up connected, not in what order: use Union-Find."],"ladder":[{"slug":"find-the-town-judge","tier":"warm-up","note":"Count in-degree and out-degree: the judge has n − 1 in and 0 out."},{"slug":"course-schedule","tier":"warm-up","note":"Kahn's as a yes/no: did all n courses get taken?"},{"slug":"course-schedule-ii","tier":"core","note":"The template: return the order itself, or [] for a cycle."},{"slug":"find-eventual-safe-states","tier":"core","note":"Reverse the edges and peel back from the terminal nodes."},{"slug":"find-all-possible-recipes-from-given-supplies","tier":"core","note":"Nodes are strings: supplies start in the queue, recipes wait on their ingredients."},{"slug":"course-schedule-iv","tier":"core","note":"Pass each node's set of prerequisites forward in topological order, then answer queries."},{"slug":"minimum-height-trees","tier":"core","note":"Kahn's on an undirected tree: trim leaves layer by layer until one or two nodes remain."},{"slug":"parallel-courses-iii","tier":"stretch","note":"Carry the earliest start time through the order: the longest path in a DAG."},{"slug":"largest-color-value-in-a-directed-graph","tier":"stretch","note":"A DP table of 26 counts per node, pushed along the order, plus the cycle check."},{"slug":"sort-items-by-groups-respecting-dependencies","tier":"stretch","note":"Two levels of topological sort: groups first, then items inside each group."}]}$json$::jsonb),
('tree-bfs',
  'Breadth-First Search',
  'Tree BFS',
  array['The answer is grouped by depth: a list per level, an average or max per level, the rightmost node of each level.', 'You want the nearest of something: the minimum depth, the first leaf, the closest node with a property.', 'The output order is top to bottom, left to right, or alternates direction per level.', 'Nodes on the same level have to be compared with each other (cousins, width, even-odd rules).']::text[],
  'Put the root in a queue. Repeatedly take a node from the front and add its children to the back. Because children always join behind everything already waiting, the tree comes out one level at a time: all of depth 0, then all of depth 1, and so on.

To know where one level ends, read the queue''s length before you start popping. At that moment the queue holds exactly one full level and nothing else. Pop that many nodes; their children, added during the loop, form the next level. This size snapshot is the whole trick, and nearly every level problem is this loop with something different done to each level.

It''s correct because a node at depth d is added while its parent at depth d − 1 is being processed, so every depth d − 1 node leaves the queue before any depth d node does. That ordering is also why BFS finds the shallowest leaf first and can stop right there, while DFS might explore a deep branch before it finds a shallow one.

Each node is added once and removed once, so the time is O(n). The queue holds at most one level plus part of the next, so the memory is O(w), where w is the widest level. That can be about n/2 for a full tree, which is more than DFS''s O(h) on a balanced tree. When levels don''t matter and you just need to visit every node, DFS is simpler.',
  'def level_order(root: Optional[TreeNode]) -> list[list[int]]:
    if not root:
        return []
    out = []
    queue = deque([root])
    while queue:
        level = []
        # everything in the queue right now is one level
        for _ in range(len(queue)):
            node = queue.popleft()
            level.append(node.val)
            # children wait behind the rest of this level
            if node.left:
                queue.append(node.left)
            if node.right:
                queue.append(node.right)
        out.append(level)
    return out
',
  'Time O(n) · Space O(w), where w is the widest level (up to about n/2)',
  array['Re-reading the queue''s size while the loop pushes children, as in for (i = 0; i < q.size(); i++). Save the size first. (Python''s range(len(queue)) is fine: it''s read once.)', 'Pushing null children and then reading .val from them. Check before you push.', 'Using a Python list with pop(0) as the queue. Each pop is O(n); use collections.deque.', 'For zigzag, reversing the order children are pushed. That scrambles the next level; reverse the finished level instead.', 'Treating a node with one child as a leaf in minimum depth. Only a node with no children ends a path.', 'Forgetting the empty tree. Return early when root is null.']::text[],
  array['average-of-levels-in-binary-tree', 'minimum-depth-of-binary-tree', 'cousins-in-binary-tree']::text[],
  13,
  $json${"version":1,"summary":"For binary tree questions about levels: the values at each depth, the first or last node of every level, the shallowest leaf. A queue visits the tree one level at a time, closest to the root first.","prerequisites":["tree-dfs"],"walkthrough":{"problemSlug":"binary-tree-level-order-traversal","input":"root = [3,9,20,null,null,15,7]","intro":"one step per node popped.","columns":["Queue after","What happened","Levels so far"],"steps":[{"window":"9, 20","action":"level of 1: pop 3, push 9 and 20; level done","best":"[[3]]"},{"window":"20","action":"level of 2: pop 9, no children","best":"[[3]], building [9]"},{"window":"15, 7","action":"pop 20, push 15 and 7; level done","best":"[[3], [9, 20]]"},{"window":"7","action":"level of 2: pop 15, no children","best":"[[3], [9, 20]], building [15]"},{"window":"(empty)","action":"pop 7; level done; queue empty, stop","best":"[[3], [9, 20], [15, 7]]"}],"result":"[[3],[9,20],[15,7]]"},"template":{"note":"Snapshot the queue's size, pop exactly that many, push their children. The size snapshot is what separates the levels; most level problems only change what you do with each popped node.","code":{"python":"def level_order(root: Optional[TreeNode]) -> list[list[int]]:\n    if not root:\n        return []\n    out = []\n    queue = deque([root])\n    while queue:\n        level = []\n        # everything in the queue right now is one level\n        for _ in range(len(queue)):\n            node = queue.popleft()\n            level.append(node.val)\n            # children wait behind the rest of this level\n            if node.left:\n                queue.append(node.left)\n            if node.right:\n                queue.append(node.right)\n        out.append(level)\n    return out\n","cpp":"vector<vector<int>> levelOrder(TreeNode* root) {\n    vector<vector<int>> out;\n    if (!root) return out;\n    queue<TreeNode*> q;\n    q.push(root);\n    while (!q.empty()) {\n        vector<int> level;\n        // everything in the queue right now is one level\n        int size = q.size();\n        for (int i = 0; i < size; ++i) {\n            TreeNode* node = q.front();\n            q.pop();\n            level.push_back(node->val);\n            // children wait behind the rest of this level\n            if (node->left) q.push(node->left);\n            if (node->right) q.push(node->right);\n        }\n        out.push_back(level);\n    }\n    return out;\n}\n","java":"class Solution {\n    public List<List<Integer>> levelOrder(TreeNode root) {\n        List<List<Integer>> out = new ArrayList<>();\n        if (root == null) return out;\n        Deque<TreeNode> q = new ArrayDeque<>();\n        q.add(root);\n        while (!q.isEmpty()) {\n            List<Integer> level = new ArrayList<>();\n            // everything in the queue right now is one level\n            int size = q.size();\n            for (int i = 0; i < size; i++) {\n                TreeNode node = q.poll();\n                level.add(node.val);\n                // children wait behind the rest of this level\n                if (node.left != null) q.add(node.left);\n                if (node.right != null) q.add(node.right);\n            }\n            out.add(level);\n        }\n        return out;\n    }\n}\n"}},"variants":[{"name":"One node per level","when":"You need the first or last node of each level, like the view from the right side.","idea":"Keep the template's loop and its index i. The last node popped in a level (i == size − 1) is the rightmost one; i == 0 gives the leftmost.","code":{"python":"def right_side_view(root: Optional[TreeNode]) -> list[int]:\n    if not root:\n        return []\n    out = []\n    queue = deque([root])\n    while queue:\n        size = len(queue)\n        for i in range(size):\n            node = queue.popleft()\n            # the last node of the level is the one you see\n            if i == size - 1:\n                out.append(node.val)\n            if node.left:\n                queue.append(node.left)\n            if node.right:\n                queue.append(node.right)\n    return out\n","cpp":"vector<int> rightSideView(TreeNode* root) {\n    vector<int> out;\n    if (!root) return out;\n    queue<TreeNode*> q;\n    q.push(root);\n    while (!q.empty()) {\n        int size = q.size();\n        for (int i = 0; i < size; ++i) {\n            TreeNode* node = q.front();\n            q.pop();\n            // the last node of the level is the one you see\n            if (i == size - 1) out.push_back(node->val);\n            if (node->left) q.push(node->left);\n            if (node->right) q.push(node->right);\n        }\n    }\n    return out;\n}\n","java":"class Solution {\n    public List<Integer> rightSideView(TreeNode root) {\n        List<Integer> out = new ArrayList<>();\n        if (root == null) return out;\n        Deque<TreeNode> q = new ArrayDeque<>();\n        q.add(root);\n        while (!q.isEmpty()) {\n            int size = q.size();\n            for (int i = 0; i < size; i++) {\n                TreeNode node = q.poll();\n                // the last node of the level is the one you see\n                if (i == size - 1) out.add(node.val);\n                if (node.left != null) q.add(node.left);\n                if (node.right != null) q.add(node.right);\n            }\n        }\n        return out;\n    }\n}\n"},"problemSlug":"binary-tree-right-side-view"},{"name":"Alternate the direction","when":"Odd levels read right to left, even levels left to right.","idea":"Don't change the order you push children: that would break the next level. Build each level normally and reverse it before saving when the flag says so, then flip the flag.","code":{"python":"def zigzag_level_order(\n    root: Optional[TreeNode],\n) -> list[list[int]]:\n    if not root:\n        return []\n    out = []\n    queue = deque([root])\n    left_to_right = True\n    while queue:\n        level = []\n        for _ in range(len(queue)):\n            node = queue.popleft()\n            level.append(node.val)\n            # queue order never changes, only the output\n            if node.left:\n                queue.append(node.left)\n            if node.right:\n                queue.append(node.right)\n        if not left_to_right:\n            level.reverse()\n        out.append(level)\n        left_to_right = not left_to_right\n    return out\n","cpp":"vector<vector<int>> zigzagLevelOrder(TreeNode* root) {\n    vector<vector<int>> out;\n    if (!root) return out;\n    queue<TreeNode*> q;\n    q.push(root);\n    bool leftToRight = true;\n    while (!q.empty()) {\n        vector<int> level;\n        int size = q.size();\n        for (int i = 0; i < size; ++i) {\n            TreeNode* node = q.front();\n            q.pop();\n            level.push_back(node->val);\n            // queue order never changes, only the output\n            if (node->left) q.push(node->left);\n            if (node->right) q.push(node->right);\n        }\n        if (!leftToRight) reverse(level.begin(), level.end());\n        out.push_back(level);\n        leftToRight = !leftToRight;\n    }\n    return out;\n}\n","java":"class Solution {\n    public List<List<Integer>> zigzagLevelOrder(TreeNode root) {\n        List<List<Integer>> out = new ArrayList<>();\n        if (root == null) return out;\n        Deque<TreeNode> q = new ArrayDeque<>();\n        q.add(root);\n        boolean leftToRight = true;\n        while (!q.isEmpty()) {\n            List<Integer> level = new ArrayList<>();\n            int size = q.size();\n            for (int i = 0; i < size; i++) {\n                TreeNode node = q.poll();\n                level.add(node.val);\n                // queue order never changes, only the output\n                if (node.left != null) q.add(node.left);\n                if (node.right != null) q.add(node.right);\n            }\n            if (!leftToRight) Collections.reverse(level);\n            out.add(level);\n            leftToRight = !leftToRight;\n        }\n        return out;\n    }\n}\n"},"problemSlug":"binary-tree-zigzag-level-order-traversal"},{"name":"Stop at the first hit","when":"You want the shallowest node with some property, like the nearest leaf.","idea":"Levels come out in order of depth, so the first leaf you pop is a shallowest one. Return the depth right there instead of finishing the tree.","code":{"python":"def min_depth(root: Optional[TreeNode]) -> int:\n    if not root:\n        return 0\n    queue = deque([root])\n    depth = 1\n    while queue:\n        for _ in range(len(queue)):\n            node = queue.popleft()\n            # levels come in order: first leaf is closest\n            if not node.left and not node.right:\n                return depth\n            if node.left:\n                queue.append(node.left)\n            if node.right:\n                queue.append(node.right)\n        depth += 1\n    return depth\n","cpp":"int minDepth(TreeNode* root) {\n    if (!root) return 0;\n    queue<TreeNode*> q;\n    q.push(root);\n    int depth = 1;\n    while (!q.empty()) {\n        int size = q.size();\n        for (int i = 0; i < size; ++i) {\n            TreeNode* node = q.front();\n            q.pop();\n            // levels come in order: first leaf is closest\n            if (!node->left && !node->right) return depth;\n            if (node->left) q.push(node->left);\n            if (node->right) q.push(node->right);\n        }\n        ++depth;\n    }\n    return depth;\n}\n","java":"class Solution {\n    public int minDepth(TreeNode root) {\n        if (root == null) return 0;\n        Deque<TreeNode> q = new ArrayDeque<>();\n        q.add(root);\n        int depth = 1;\n        while (!q.isEmpty()) {\n            int size = q.size();\n            for (int i = 0; i < size; i++) {\n                TreeNode node = q.poll();\n                // levels come in order: first leaf is closest\n                if (node.left == null && node.right == null) {\n                    return depth;\n                }\n                if (node.left != null) q.add(node.left);\n                if (node.right != null) q.add(node.right);\n            }\n            depth++;\n        }\n        return depth;\n    }\n}\n"},"problemSlug":"minimum-depth-of-binary-tree"}],"complexity":{"time":"O(n)","space":"O(w), where w is the widest level (up to about n/2)","why":"Every node is pushed once and popped once, and the work per node is constant. The queue holds at most one full level plus the children added so far, so its size is bounded by the widest part of the tree. The output list itself takes O(n)."},"notThis":["The answer combines whole subtrees (height, diameter, balanced, path sums): recursion is shorter. See Tree DFS.","Levels don't matter and you only need to visit every node once: DFS uses O(h) memory instead of O(w).","Shortest path in a graph, not a tree: the same queue, plus a visited set because nodes can be reached twice. See Graph BFS / DFS."],"ladder":[{"slug":"average-of-levels-in-binary-tree","tier":"warm-up","note":"The template with a running sum per level instead of a list."},{"slug":"minimum-depth-of-binary-tree","tier":"warm-up","note":"Stop at the first leaf: a case where BFS beats DFS."},{"slug":"cousins-in-binary-tree","tier":"warm-up","note":"Same level, different parents: note each node's parent while you scan a level."},{"slug":"binary-tree-level-order-traversal","tier":"core","note":"The template: snapshot the size, pop that many."},{"slug":"binary-tree-right-side-view","tier":"core","note":"Keep only the last node of each level."},{"slug":"binary-tree-zigzag-level-order-traversal","tier":"core","note":"Reverse every other finished level."},{"slug":"find-largest-value-in-each-tree-row","tier":"core","note":"One number per level: its max."},{"slug":"maximum-level-sum-of-a-binary-tree","tier":"core","note":"Compare whole levels and keep the first best one."},{"slug":"even-odd-tree","tier":"core","note":"Check a rule between neighbours inside a level."},{"slug":"maximum-width-of-binary-tree","tier":"core","note":"Carry a position index with each node; width is last minus first plus one."},{"slug":"all-nodes-distance-k-in-binary-tree","tier":"core","note":"Add parent links, then BFS outward k steps from the target, like a graph."},{"slug":"amount-of-time-for-binary-tree-to-be-infected","tier":"stretch","note":"The same spread from a start node, run until every node is reached."},{"slug":"vertical-order-traversal-of-a-binary-tree","tier":"stretch","note":"Carry (row, column) with each node, then sort the ties."},{"slug":"serialize-and-deserialize-binary-tree","tier":"stretch","note":"Write the tree out level by level with null markers, then rebuild it with the same queue."}]}$json$::jsonb),
('tree-dfs',
  'Depth-First Search',
  'Tree DFS',
  array['The input is a TreeNode root and the answer is a number or a yes/no about the whole tree: depth, diameter, balanced, same tree.', 'A node''s answer can be built from its left and right children''s answers.', 'The question is about root-to-leaf paths, or about what lies above a node (the largest value so far, the sum so far).', 'You need to find a node or a meeting point of two nodes (lowest common ancestor).']::text[],
  'Treat every node as the root of its own small tree. Write a function that solves the problem for one subtree, assuming the calls on its left and right children already return the right answers. Then add the base case: an empty subtree (null) returns something neutral, like 0 for a height or true for "is balanced".

Information moves in two directions. Bottom-up, a node gets results from its children and returns one result to its parent, like a height. Top-down, a node passes something into its children''s calls, like the largest value seen on the path so far. Many problems need one of each.

Often the parent needs one thing and the question asks for another. In the diameter problem the parent needs the height of a subtree, but the answer is the longest path, and that path can bend at any node. So the function returns the height and, at each node, updates a separate best value with left height + right height. Mixing the two in one return value is the classic bug.

It''s correct by induction: if the children''s calls are right, the node''s combination is right, and null is right by definition. It''s fast because each node is entered once and does constant work beyond its two calls, so O(n) time. The recursion stack holds one frame per level, O(h) space, which is O(log n) for a balanced tree and O(n) for a tree shaped like a line. Very deep trees can overflow the stack; then use an explicit stack.',
  'def diameter_of_binary_tree(root: Optional[TreeNode]) -> int:
    best = 0

    # returns the height: nodes on the longest path down
    def height(node: Optional[TreeNode]) -> int:
        nonlocal best
        if not node:
            return 0
        left = height(node.left)
        right = height(node.right)
        # the longest path that bends at this node, in edges
        best = max(best, left + right)
        # the parent only needs the taller side
        return 1 + max(left, right)

    height(root)
    return best
',
  'Time O(n) · Space O(h) for the recursion, where h is the height',
  array['Returning the answer when the parent needs something else, like returning the diameter instead of the height. Keep a separate best value.', 'Forgetting the null base case, or putting it after code that reads node.val.', 'Counting nodes when the question counts edges (or the reverse). Diameter is in edges; a height of 1 means one node.', 'Treating a node with one child as a leaf. A leaf has no children at all.', 'Sharing a path list without removing the node on the way back up, or saving the list itself instead of a copy.', 'Using a global that isn''t reset between test cases. In Java or C++, reset it at the start of the public method.', 'Calling a height function inside another recursive function at every node, which turns O(n) into O(n²). Compute both in one pass.']::text[],
  array['maximum-depth-of-binary-tree', 'invert-binary-tree', 'same-tree']::text[],
  12,
  $json${"version":1,"summary":"For binary tree questions where each node's answer depends on its subtrees (heights, sums, paths) or on the path from the root. A recursive function returns what the parent needs, and every node is visited once.","prerequisites":["linked-list"],"walkthrough":{"problemSlug":"diameter-of-binary-tree","input":"root = [1,2,3,4,5]","intro":"one step per node, in the order the calls return (children before parent).","columns":["Node","What happened","Best path (edges)"],"steps":[{"window":"4 (leaf)","action":"left 0, right 0: path 0; return height 1","best":"0"},{"window":"5 (leaf)","action":"left 0, right 0: path 0; return height 1","best":"0"},{"window":"2","action":"left 1, right 1: path 1 + 1 = 2; return height 2","best":"2"},{"window":"3 (leaf)","action":"left 0, right 0: path 0; return height 1","best":"2"},{"window":"1 (root)","action":"left 2, right 1: path 2 + 1 = 3; return height 3","best":"3"}],"result":"3, the path 4 → 2 → 1 → 3 (or 5 → 2 → 1 → 3)"},"template":{"note":"Return what the parent needs (the height); record what the question asks (the best path) on the side. Base case first, then the two calls, then combine.","code":{"python":"def diameter_of_binary_tree(root: Optional[TreeNode]) -> int:\n    best = 0\n\n    # returns the height: nodes on the longest path down\n    def height(node: Optional[TreeNode]) -> int:\n        nonlocal best\n        if not node:\n            return 0\n        left = height(node.left)\n        right = height(node.right)\n        # the longest path that bends at this node, in edges\n        best = max(best, left + right)\n        # the parent only needs the taller side\n        return 1 + max(left, right)\n\n    height(root)\n    return best\n","cpp":"// returns the height: nodes on the longest path down\nint height(TreeNode* node, int& best) {\n    if (!node) return 0;\n    int left = height(node->left, best);\n    int right = height(node->right, best);\n    // the longest path that bends at this node, in edges\n    best = max(best, left + right);\n    // the parent only needs the taller side\n    return 1 + max(left, right);\n}\n\nint diameterOfBinaryTree(TreeNode* root) {\n    int best = 0;\n    height(root, best);\n    return best;\n}\n","java":"class Solution {\n    private int best;\n\n    public int diameterOfBinaryTree(TreeNode root) {\n        best = 0;\n        height(root);\n        return best;\n    }\n\n    // returns the height: nodes on the longest path down\n    private int height(TreeNode node) {\n        if (node == null) return 0;\n        int left = height(node.left);\n        int right = height(node.right);\n        // the longest path that bends at this node, in edges\n        best = Math.max(best, left + right);\n        // the parent only needs the taller side\n        return 1 + Math.max(left, right);\n    }\n}\n"}},"variants":[{"name":"Top-down: pass state into the children","when":"Whether a node counts depends on the path above it: the max so far, the sum so far, the depth.","idea":"Add a parameter that carries the path's state. Update it at the node, then hand the new value to both children. Each child gets its own copy, so nothing leaks from the left branch into the right.","code":{"python":"def good_nodes(root: TreeNode) -> int:\n    # top_max: the largest value on the path from the root\n    def dfs(node: Optional[TreeNode], top_max: int) -> int:\n        if not node:\n            return 0\n        good = 1 if node.val >= top_max else 0\n        # hand each child the max of the path so far\n        top_max = max(top_max, node.val)\n        left = dfs(node.left, top_max)\n        right = dfs(node.right, top_max)\n        return good + left + right\n\n    return dfs(root, root.val)\n","cpp":"// topMax: the largest value on the path from the root\nint dfs(TreeNode* node, int topMax) {\n    if (!node) return 0;\n    int good = node->val >= topMax ? 1 : 0;\n    // hand each child the max of the path so far\n    topMax = max(topMax, node->val);\n    int left = dfs(node->left, topMax);\n    int right = dfs(node->right, topMax);\n    return good + left + right;\n}\n\nint goodNodes(TreeNode* root) {\n    return dfs(root, root->val);\n}\n","java":"class Solution {\n    public int goodNodes(TreeNode root) {\n        return dfs(root, root.val);\n    }\n\n    // topMax: the largest value on the path from the root\n    private int dfs(TreeNode node, int topMax) {\n        if (node == null) return 0;\n        int good = node.val >= topMax ? 1 : 0;\n        // hand each child the max of the path so far\n        topMax = Math.max(topMax, node.val);\n        int left = dfs(node.left, topMax);\n        int right = dfs(node.right, topMax);\n        return good + left + right;\n    }\n}\n"},"problemSlug":"count-good-nodes-in-binary-tree"},{"name":"Return what you found","when":"You're searching for nodes and need where their paths meet.","idea":"Return the target if this node is one, else whatever the children found. If both sides return something, this node is the lowest common ancestor. If one side does, pass it up.","code":{"python":"def lowest_common_ancestor(\n    root: Optional[TreeNode], p: TreeNode, q: TreeNode\n) -> Optional[TreeNode]:\n    # returns p, q, their LCA, or None if neither is below\n    if not root or root is p or root is q:\n        return root\n    left = lowest_common_ancestor(root.left, p, q)\n    right = lowest_common_ancestor(root.right, p, q)\n    # one found on each side: they meet here\n    if left and right:\n        return root\n    # otherwise pass up whatever was found\n    return left if left else right\n","cpp":"TreeNode* lowestCommonAncestor(TreeNode* root, TreeNode* p,\n                               TreeNode* q) {\n    // returns p, q, their LCA, or null if neither is below\n    if (!root || root == p || root == q) return root;\n    TreeNode* left = lowestCommonAncestor(root->left, p, q);\n    TreeNode* right = lowestCommonAncestor(root->right, p, q);\n    // one found on each side: they meet here\n    if (left && right) return root;\n    // otherwise pass up whatever was found\n    return left ? left : right;\n}\n","java":"class Solution {\n    public TreeNode lowestCommonAncestor(\n            TreeNode root, TreeNode p, TreeNode q) {\n        // returns p, q, their LCA, or null if neither is below\n        if (root == null || root == p || root == q) return root;\n        TreeNode left = lowestCommonAncestor(root.left, p, q);\n        TreeNode right = lowestCommonAncestor(root.right, p, q);\n        // one found on each side: they meet here\n        if (left != null && right != null) return root;\n        // otherwise pass up whatever was found\n        return left != null ? left : right;\n    }\n}\n"},"problemSlug":"lowest-common-ancestor-of-a-binary-tree"},{"name":"Carry a path, undo on the way up","when":"The answer lists whole root-to-leaf paths, not just a count.","idea":"Keep one shared list. Append the node before recursing and remove it after both children return, so the list always holds exactly the current path. Copy it when you record an answer. This is backtracking on a tree.","code":{"python":"def path_sum(\n    root: Optional[TreeNode], target: int\n) -> list[list[int]]:\n    out = []\n    path = []\n\n    def dfs(node: Optional[TreeNode], remaining: int) -> None:\n        if not node:\n            return\n        # on the way down: this node joins the path\n        path.append(node.val)\n        remaining -= node.val\n        # a leaf that uses up the target exactly\n        if not node.left and not node.right and remaining == 0:\n            out.append(path.copy())\n        dfs(node.left, remaining)\n        dfs(node.right, remaining)\n        # on the way up: undo, so the path is clean again\n        path.pop()\n\n    dfs(root, target)\n    return out\n","cpp":"void dfs(TreeNode* node, int remaining, vector<int>& path,\n         vector<vector<int>>& out) {\n    if (!node) return;\n    // on the way down: this node joins the path\n    path.push_back(node->val);\n    remaining -= node->val;\n    // a leaf that uses up the target exactly\n    if (!node->left && !node->right && remaining == 0) {\n        out.push_back(path);\n    }\n    dfs(node->left, remaining, path, out);\n    dfs(node->right, remaining, path, out);\n    // on the way up: undo, so the path is clean again\n    path.pop_back();\n}\n\nvector<vector<int>> pathSum(TreeNode* root, int target) {\n    vector<vector<int>> out;\n    vector<int> path;\n    dfs(root, target, path, out);\n    return out;\n}\n","java":"class Solution {\n    public List<List<Integer>> pathSum(TreeNode root,\n                                       int target) {\n        List<List<Integer>> out = new ArrayList<>();\n        dfs(root, target, new ArrayList<>(), out);\n        return out;\n    }\n\n    private void dfs(TreeNode node, int remaining,\n                     List<Integer> path,\n                     List<List<Integer>> out) {\n        if (node == null) return;\n        // on the way down: this node joins the path\n        path.add(node.val);\n        remaining -= node.val;\n        // a leaf that uses up the target exactly\n        if (node.left == null && node.right == null\n                && remaining == 0) {\n            out.add(new ArrayList<>(path));\n        }\n        dfs(node.left, remaining, path, out);\n        dfs(node.right, remaining, path, out);\n        // on the way up: undo, so the path is clean again\n        path.remove(path.size() - 1);\n    }\n}\n"},"problemSlug":"path-sum-ii"}],"complexity":{"time":"O(n)","space":"O(h) for the recursion, where h is the height","why":"Each node is entered once and does constant work besides its two recursive calls. The call stack only holds the nodes on the current root-to-node path, so its depth is the tree's height: about log n when balanced, up to n for a chain. Path Sum II also pays to copy each answer path."},"notThis":["The question is about levels: the values at each depth, the rightmost node per level, the shallowest leaf. Use BFS. See Tree BFS.","The tree is a binary search tree and the question uses its order (find, insert, kth smallest): go left or right by comparing values. See Binary Search Tree.","A general graph with cycles: DFS still works, but you need a visited set. See Graph BFS / DFS."],"ladder":[{"slug":"maximum-depth-of-binary-tree","tier":"warm-up","note":"The smallest bottom-up recursion: 1 + the taller child."},{"slug":"invert-binary-tree","tier":"warm-up","note":"Do the same thing at every node; swap the children."},{"slug":"same-tree","tier":"warm-up","note":"Recurse on two trees at once."},{"slug":"path-sum","tier":"warm-up","note":"Pass the remaining sum down and check it at the leaves."},{"slug":"diameter-of-binary-tree","tier":"core","note":"The template: return the height, record the best path on the side."},{"slug":"balanced-binary-tree","tier":"core","note":"The same height function, with −1 meaning already unbalanced so it stops early."},{"slug":"subtree-of-another-tree","tier":"core","note":"Run same-tree from every node: recursion inside recursion."},{"slug":"count-good-nodes-in-binary-tree","tier":"core","note":"Top-down: carry the path maximum into each call."},{"slug":"path-sum-ii","tier":"core","note":"Carry the whole path: add on the way down, remove on the way up."},{"slug":"lowest-common-ancestor-of-a-binary-tree","tier":"core","note":"Return what you found and let the parent decide."},{"slug":"construct-binary-tree-from-preorder-and-inorder-traversal","tier":"core","note":"Build the tree from index ranges, with a hash map for the root's position."},{"slug":"house-robber-iii","tier":"core","note":"Return two values per node: the best with it and the best without it."},{"slug":"binary-tree-maximum-path-sum","tier":"stretch","note":"Diameter with values: a negative branch counts as 0."},{"slug":"binary-tree-cameras","tier":"stretch","note":"Return a state per node (covered, has a camera, needs one) and decide from the leaves up."}]}$json$::jsonb),
('trie',
  'Trie',
  'Trie',
  array['Many words, and many questions about their prefixes: starts with, autocomplete, shortest root.', 'You check a string letter by letter against a whole dictionary, and want to stop the moment no word can match.', 'A search over a grid or a stream must match any of a list of words at once.', 'A design problem asks for insert, search and startsWith (or a wildcard like ".").', 'Maximum XOR of two numbers: a trie over bits, highest bit first.']::text[],
  'Each node stands for one prefix. It has up to 26 children, one per next letter, and a flag that says whether a whole word ends here. The root is the empty prefix. To insert a word, walk from the root one letter at a time, creating any child that is missing, and set the flag on the last node.

To look something up, walk the same way. If a child is missing, no stored word has that prefix, and you stop. If the walk finishes, the prefix exists; it is a whole word only if the last node''s flag is set. That flag is the difference between search("app") and startsWith("app") after inserting "apple".

It''s fast because the cost depends on the length of the string, not on how many words are stored. A hash set can also check a whole word in O(L), but it can''t tell you that no word starts with "xq" without scanning everything. The trie can, and that is what lets a search give up early.

The price is memory: one node per distinct prefix, each with 26 slots. When the alphabet is large or unknown, use a hash map for the children instead of an array. For a single lookup, or when no prefix question is asked, a hash set is simpler.',
  'class TrieNode:
    def __init__(self) -> None:
        # one slot per letter a..z
        self.children: list[Optional[TrieNode]] = [None] * 26
        self.is_end = False


class Trie:
    def __init__(self) -> None:
        self.root = TrieNode()

    def insert(self, word: str) -> None:
        node = self.root
        for c in word:
            i = ord(c) - ord("a")
            # make the path if it isn''t there yet
            if node.children[i] is None:
                node.children[i] = TrieNode()
            node = node.children[i]
        # mark that a whole word ends here
        node.is_end = True

    # the node at the end of the path, or None
    def walk(self, s: str) -> Optional[TrieNode]:
        node = self.root
        for c in s:
            node = node.children[ord(c) - ord("a")]
            if node is None:
                return None
        return node

    def search(self, word: str) -> bool:
        node = self.walk(word)
        return node is not None and node.is_end

    def startsWith(self, prefix: str) -> bool:
        return self.walk(prefix) is not None
',
  'Time O(L) per insert or lookup, where L is the length of the string · Space O(total letters inserted × 26) in the worst case',
  array['Forgetting the end-of-word flag, so search("app") returns true after inserting only "apple".', 'Writing search and startsWith as two separate walks. They are the same walk; only the last check differs.', 'Creating nodes during a search. Only insert should add nodes, or lookups slowly fill the trie.', 'Indexing with c - ''a'' when the input can hold uppercase letters or other symbols. Use a hash map for the children then.', 'In Word Search II, not marking the cell as used while it is on the path, so a letter gets used twice in one word.', 'Reporting the same word twice when several grid paths spell it. Clear the stored word once it''s found.', 'Using a trie for one lookup in a list of words. A hash set does that with less code.']::text[],
  array['implement-trie-prefix-tree', 'longest-common-prefix', 'longest-word-in-dictionary']::text[],
  15,
  $json${"version":1,"summary":"A tree of letters where each root-to-node path spells a prefix, so words that start the same way share nodes. It answers \"is this a word?\" and \"does any word start with this?\" in time proportional to the word's length, however many words are stored.","prerequisites":["tree-dfs"],"walkthrough":{"problemSlug":"implement-trie-prefix-tree","input":"insert(\"cat\"), insert(\"car\"), search(\"ca\"), startsWith(\"ca\"), insert(\"ca\"), search(\"ca\"), search(\"cab\")","intro":"one step per call. A * marks a node whose end-of-word flag is set.","columns":["Trie paths","Call","Returns"],"steps":[{"window":"c → a → t*","action":"insert(\"cat\"): no children yet, so create c, a and t; flag t","best":"null"},{"window":"c → a → {t*, r*}","action":"insert(\"car\"): c and a exist; create r under a and flag it","best":"null"},{"window":"c → a → {t*, r*}","action":"search(\"ca\"): the walk reaches a, but a has no flag","best":"false"},{"window":"c → a → {t*, r*}","action":"startsWith(\"ca\"): the walk reaches a, so the prefix exists","best":"true"},{"window":"c → a* → {t*, r*}","action":"insert(\"ca\"): both nodes exist; nothing is created, a gets its flag","best":"null"},{"window":"c → a* → {t*, r*}","action":"search(\"ca\"): the walk reaches a, and now it is flagged","best":"true"},{"window":"c → a* → {t*, r*}","action":"search(\"cab\"): a has no b child, so the walk stops","best":"false"}],"result":"false, true, true, false for the four lookups. Three words share the nodes c and a; only the flag tells \"ca\" the word from \"ca\" the prefix."},"template":{"note":"insert creates missing children and flags the last node. search and startsWith share one walk that returns the last node or null; search also checks the flag.","code":{"python":"class TrieNode:\n    def __init__(self) -> None:\n        # one slot per letter a..z\n        self.children: list[Optional[TrieNode]] = [None] * 26\n        self.is_end = False\n\n\nclass Trie:\n    def __init__(self) -> None:\n        self.root = TrieNode()\n\n    def insert(self, word: str) -> None:\n        node = self.root\n        for c in word:\n            i = ord(c) - ord(\"a\")\n            # make the path if it isn't there yet\n            if node.children[i] is None:\n                node.children[i] = TrieNode()\n            node = node.children[i]\n        # mark that a whole word ends here\n        node.is_end = True\n\n    # the node at the end of the path, or None\n    def walk(self, s: str) -> Optional[TrieNode]:\n        node = self.root\n        for c in s:\n            node = node.children[ord(c) - ord(\"a\")]\n            if node is None:\n                return None\n        return node\n\n    def search(self, word: str) -> bool:\n        node = self.walk(word)\n        return node is not None and node.is_end\n\n    def startsWith(self, prefix: str) -> bool:\n        return self.walk(prefix) is not None\n","cpp":"struct TrieNode {\n    // one slot per letter a..z\n    TrieNode* children[26] = {};\n    bool isEnd = false;\n};\n\nclass Trie {\npublic:\n    TrieNode* root = new TrieNode();\n\n    void insert(const string& word) {\n        TrieNode* node = root;\n        for (char c : word) {\n            int i = c - 'a';\n            // make the path if it isn't there yet\n            if (!node->children[i]) {\n                node->children[i] = new TrieNode();\n            }\n            node = node->children[i];\n        }\n        // mark that a whole word ends here\n        node->isEnd = true;\n    }\n\n    // the node at the end of the path, or nullptr\n    TrieNode* walk(const string& s) {\n        TrieNode* node = root;\n        for (char c : s) {\n            node = node->children[c - 'a'];\n            if (!node) return nullptr;\n        }\n        return node;\n    }\n\n    bool search(const string& word) {\n        TrieNode* node = walk(word);\n        return node && node->isEnd;\n    }\n\n    bool startsWith(const string& prefix) {\n        return walk(prefix) != nullptr;\n    }\n};\n","java":"class TrieNode {\n    // one slot per letter a..z\n    TrieNode[] children = new TrieNode[26];\n    boolean isEnd = false;\n}\n\nclass Trie {\n    private final TrieNode root = new TrieNode();\n\n    public void insert(String word) {\n        TrieNode node = root;\n        for (char c : word.toCharArray()) {\n            int i = c - 'a';\n            // make the path if it isn't there yet\n            if (node.children[i] == null) {\n                node.children[i] = new TrieNode();\n            }\n            node = node.children[i];\n        }\n        // mark that a whole word ends here\n        node.isEnd = true;\n    }\n\n    // the node at the end of the path, or null\n    private TrieNode walk(String s) {\n        TrieNode node = root;\n        for (char c : s.toCharArray()) {\n            node = node.children[c - 'a'];\n            if (node == null) return null;\n        }\n        return node;\n    }\n\n    public boolean search(String word) {\n        TrieNode node = walk(word);\n        return node != null && node.isEnd;\n    }\n\n    public boolean startsWith(String prefix) {\n        return walk(prefix) != null;\n    }\n}\n"}},"variants":[{"name":"Wildcards: branch on \".\"","when":"A query letter can match anything, like \".\" in \"b..\".","idea":"Make the walk a DFS over (node, index). A normal letter follows its one child. A \".\" tries every child that exists and succeeds if any branch does. At the end of the query, the answer is the node's flag.","code":{"python":"class WordDictionary:\n    def __init__(self) -> None:\n        self.root = TrieNode()\n\n    def addWord(self, word: str) -> None:\n        node = self.root\n        for c in word:\n            i = ord(c) - ord(\"a\")\n            if node.children[i] is None:\n                node.children[i] = TrieNode()\n            node = node.children[i]\n        node.is_end = True\n\n    def search(self, word: str) -> bool:\n        # can word[i:] be matched starting from node?\n        def dfs(node: TrieNode, i: int) -> bool:\n            if i == len(word):\n                return node.is_end\n            if word[i] == \".\":\n                # \".\" matches any letter: try every child\n                for child in node.children:\n                    if child and dfs(child, i + 1):\n                        return True\n                return False\n            child = node.children[ord(word[i]) - ord(\"a\")]\n            return child is not None and dfs(child, i + 1)\n\n        return dfs(self.root, 0)\n","cpp":"class WordDictionary {\npublic:\n    TrieNode* root = new TrieNode();\n\n    void addWord(const string& word) {\n        TrieNode* node = root;\n        for (char c : word) {\n            int i = c - 'a';\n            if (!node->children[i]) {\n                node->children[i] = new TrieNode();\n            }\n            node = node->children[i];\n        }\n        node->isEnd = true;\n    }\n\n    bool search(const string& word) {\n        return dfs(word, root, 0);\n    }\n\n    // can word[i:] be matched starting from node?\n    bool dfs(const string& word, TrieNode* node, size_t i) {\n        if (i == word.size()) return node->isEnd;\n        if (word[i] == '.') {\n            // \".\" matches any letter: try every child\n            for (TrieNode* child : node->children) {\n                if (child && dfs(word, child, i + 1)) {\n                    return true;\n                }\n            }\n            return false;\n        }\n        TrieNode* child = node->children[word[i] - 'a'];\n        return child && dfs(word, child, i + 1);\n    }\n};\n","java":"class WordDictionary {\n    private final TrieNode root = new TrieNode();\n\n    public void addWord(String word) {\n        TrieNode node = root;\n        for (char c : word.toCharArray()) {\n            int i = c - 'a';\n            if (node.children[i] == null) {\n                node.children[i] = new TrieNode();\n            }\n            node = node.children[i];\n        }\n        node.isEnd = true;\n    }\n\n    public boolean search(String word) {\n        return dfs(word, root, 0);\n    }\n\n    // can word[i:] be matched starting from node?\n    private boolean dfs(String word, TrieNode node, int i) {\n        if (i == word.length()) return node.isEnd;\n        if (word.charAt(i) == '.') {\n            // \".\" matches any letter: try every child\n            for (TrieNode child : node.children) {\n                if (child != null && dfs(word, child, i + 1)) {\n                    return true;\n                }\n            }\n            return false;\n        }\n        TrieNode child = node.children[word.charAt(i) - 'a'];\n        return child != null && dfs(word, child, i + 1);\n    }\n}\n"},"problemSlug":"design-add-and-search-words-data-structure"},{"name":"Stop at the first flag","when":"You want the shortest stored word that is a prefix of the input.","idea":"Build the trie from the dictionary, then walk each word of the sentence. The first flagged node on the walk is the shortest root, so cut the word there. A missing child means no root fits, and the word stays as it is.","code":{"python":"def replace_words(dictionary: list[str], sentence: str) -> str:\n    root = TrieNode()\n    for word in dictionary:\n        node = root\n        for c in word:\n            i = ord(c) - ord(\"a\")\n            if node.children[i] is None:\n                node.children[i] = TrieNode()\n            node = node.children[i]\n        node.is_end = True\n\n    out: list[str] = []\n    for word in sentence.split(\" \"):\n        node = root\n        replaced = word\n        for j, c in enumerate(word):\n            node = node.children[ord(c) - ord(\"a\")]\n            # no root is a prefix of this word\n            if node is None:\n                break\n            # the first end mark is the shortest root\n            if node.is_end:\n                replaced = word[: j + 1]\n                break\n        out.append(replaced)\n    return \" \".join(out)\n","cpp":"string replaceWords(vector<string>& dictionary,\n                    const string& sentence) {\n    TrieNode* root = new TrieNode();\n    for (const string& word : dictionary) {\n        TrieNode* node = root;\n        for (char c : word) {\n            int i = c - 'a';\n            if (!node->children[i]) {\n                node->children[i] = new TrieNode();\n            }\n            node = node->children[i];\n        }\n        node->isEnd = true;\n    }\n\n    string out;\n    istringstream words(sentence);\n    string word;\n    while (words >> word) {\n        TrieNode* node = root;\n        string replaced = word;\n        for (size_t j = 0; j < word.size(); j++) {\n            node = node->children[word[j] - 'a'];\n            // no root is a prefix of this word\n            if (!node) break;\n            // the first end mark is the shortest root\n            if (node->isEnd) {\n                replaced = word.substr(0, j + 1);\n                break;\n            }\n        }\n        if (!out.empty()) out += \" \";\n        out += replaced;\n    }\n    return out;\n}\n","java":"class Solution {\n    public String replaceWords(List<String> dictionary,\n                               String sentence) {\n        TrieNode root = new TrieNode();\n        for (String word : dictionary) {\n            TrieNode node = root;\n            for (char c : word.toCharArray()) {\n                int i = c - 'a';\n                if (node.children[i] == null) {\n                    node.children[i] = new TrieNode();\n                }\n                node = node.children[i];\n            }\n            node.isEnd = true;\n        }\n\n        List<String> out = new ArrayList<>();\n        for (String word : sentence.split(\" \")) {\n            TrieNode node = root;\n            String replaced = word;\n            for (int j = 0; j < word.length(); j++) {\n                node = node.children[word.charAt(j) - 'a'];\n                // no root is a prefix of this word\n                if (node == null) break;\n                // the first end mark is the shortest root\n                if (node.isEnd) {\n                    replaced = word.substring(0, j + 1);\n                    break;\n                }\n            }\n            out.add(replaced);\n        }\n        return String.join(\" \", out);\n    }\n}\n"},"problemSlug":"replace-words"},{"name":"Walk the trie and the grid together","when":"Find which of many words can be traced on a grid.","idea":"Run one backtracking DFS from each cell, moving down the trie as you move across the grid. When the grid letter has no child in the trie, no word continues that way, so stop. Store the word at its end node and clear it once found, so it is reported once.","code":{"python":"class TrieNode:\n    def __init__(self) -> None:\n        self.children: list[Optional[TrieNode]] = [None] * 26\n        # the whole word if one ends here, else \"\"\n        self.word = \"\"\n\n\ndef find_words(board: list[list[str]],\n               words: list[str]) -> list[str]:\n    root = TrieNode()\n    for w in words:\n        node = root\n        for c in w:\n            i = ord(c) - ord(\"a\")\n            if node.children[i] is None:\n                node.children[i] = TrieNode()\n            node = node.children[i]\n        node.word = w\n\n    rows, cols = len(board), len(board[0])\n    found: list[str] = []\n\n    def dfs(r: int, c: int, node: TrieNode) -> None:\n        ch = board[r][c]\n        # \"#\" marks a cell already on this path\n        if ch == \"#\":\n            return\n        child = node.children[ord(ch) - ord(\"a\")]\n        # no word goes this way: stop early\n        if child is None:\n            return\n        if child.word:\n            found.append(child.word)\n            # clear it so each word is reported once\n            child.word = \"\"\n        board[r][c] = \"#\"\n        for dr, dc in ((-1, 0), (1, 0), (0, -1), (0, 1)):\n            nr, nc = r + dr, c + dc\n            if 0 <= nr < rows and 0 <= nc < cols:\n                dfs(nr, nc, child)\n        # undo the mark so other paths can use this cell\n        board[r][c] = ch\n\n    for r in range(rows):\n        for c in range(cols):\n            dfs(r, c, root)\n    return found\n","cpp":"struct TrieNode {\n    TrieNode* children[26] = {};\n    // the whole word if one ends here, else \"\"\n    string word;\n};\n\nvoid dfs(vector<vector<char>>& board, int r, int c,\n         TrieNode* node, vector<string>& found) {\n    char ch = board[r][c];\n    // \"#\" marks a cell already on this path\n    if (ch == '#') return;\n    TrieNode* child = node->children[ch - 'a'];\n    // no word goes this way: stop early\n    if (!child) return;\n    if (!child->word.empty()) {\n        found.push_back(child->word);\n        // clear it so each word is reported once\n        child->word = \"\";\n    }\n    board[r][c] = '#';\n    int rows = board.size(), cols = board[0].size();\n    int dr[] = {-1, 1, 0, 0}, dc[] = {0, 0, -1, 1};\n    for (int d = 0; d < 4; d++) {\n        int nr = r + dr[d], nc = c + dc[d];\n        if (nr >= 0 && nr < rows && nc >= 0 && nc < cols) {\n            dfs(board, nr, nc, child, found);\n        }\n    }\n    // undo the mark so other paths can use this cell\n    board[r][c] = ch;\n}\n\nvector<string> findWords(vector<vector<char>>& board,\n                         vector<string>& words) {\n    TrieNode* root = new TrieNode();\n    for (const string& w : words) {\n        TrieNode* node = root;\n        for (char c : w) {\n            int i = c - 'a';\n            if (!node->children[i]) {\n                node->children[i] = new TrieNode();\n            }\n            node = node->children[i];\n        }\n        node->word = w;\n    }\n\n    int rows = board.size(), cols = board[0].size();\n    vector<string> found;\n    for (int r = 0; r < rows; r++) {\n        for (int c = 0; c < cols; c++) {\n            dfs(board, r, c, root, found);\n        }\n    }\n    return found;\n}\n","java":"class TrieNode {\n    TrieNode[] children = new TrieNode[26];\n    // the whole word if one ends here, else \"\"\n    String word = \"\";\n}\n\nclass Solution {\n    private char[][] board;\n    private List<String> found;\n\n    public List<String> findWords(char[][] board,\n                                  String[] words) {\n        TrieNode root = new TrieNode();\n        for (String w : words) {\n            TrieNode node = root;\n            for (char c : w.toCharArray()) {\n                int i = c - 'a';\n                if (node.children[i] == null) {\n                    node.children[i] = new TrieNode();\n                }\n                node = node.children[i];\n            }\n            node.word = w;\n        }\n\n        this.board = board;\n        found = new ArrayList<>();\n        for (int r = 0; r < board.length; r++) {\n            for (int c = 0; c < board[0].length; c++) {\n                dfs(r, c, root);\n            }\n        }\n        return found;\n    }\n\n    private void dfs(int r, int c, TrieNode node) {\n        char ch = board[r][c];\n        // \"#\" marks a cell already on this path\n        if (ch == '#') return;\n        TrieNode child = node.children[ch - 'a'];\n        // no word goes this way: stop early\n        if (child == null) return;\n        if (!child.word.isEmpty()) {\n            found.add(child.word);\n            // clear it so each word is reported once\n            child.word = \"\";\n        }\n        board[r][c] = '#';\n        int[][] dirs = {{-1, 0}, {1, 0}, {0, -1}, {0, 1}};\n        for (int[] d : dirs) {\n            int nr = r + d[0], nc = c + d[1];\n            if (nr >= 0 && nr < board.length\n                    && nc >= 0 && nc < board[0].length) {\n                dfs(nr, nc, child);\n            }\n        }\n        // undo the mark so other paths can use this cell\n        board[r][c] = ch;\n    }\n}\n"},"problemSlug":"word-search-ii"}],"complexity":{"time":"O(L) per insert or lookup, where L is the length of the string","space":"O(total letters inserted × 26) in the worst case","why":"Each letter moves one level down the trie with one array lookup, so the work depends only on the string being processed. Every new letter can create a node with 26 slots, which is where the memory goes. A wildcard query can visit many branches, up to every node in the trie."},"notThis":["You only check whole words, never prefixes: use a hash set. See Arrays & Hashing.","Searching for one pattern inside one long text: use string matching (KMP, or a rolling hash), not a trie of one word.","Words in sorted order with a prefix question asked once: sort and binary search for the first word >= the prefix. See Binary Search."],"ladder":[{"slug":"implement-trie-prefix-tree","tier":"warm-up","note":"The template: insert, search and startsWith on one walk."},{"slug":"longest-common-prefix","tier":"warm-up","note":"Insert every word, then walk down while there is exactly one child and no word has ended."},{"slug":"longest-word-in-dictionary","tier":"warm-up","note":"Every prefix must also be a word: only step through flagged nodes."},{"slug":"replace-words","tier":"core","note":"Stop at the first flag on the walk: the shortest root."},{"slug":"design-add-and-search-words-data-structure","tier":"core","note":"A \".\" tries every child: the walk becomes a DFS."},{"slug":"map-sum-pairs","tier":"core","note":"Keep a sum at every node on the path and fix it up when a key's value changes."},{"slug":"search-suggestions-system","tier":"core","note":"One step down per typed letter, then list the first three words below that node."},{"slug":"maximum-xor-of-two-numbers-in-an-array","tier":"core","note":"A trie over bits, top bit first: take the opposite bit whenever it exists."},{"slug":"word-search-ii","tier":"stretch","note":"Backtrack on the grid and the trie at once, and prune where no word continues."},{"slug":"sum-of-prefix-scores-of-strings","tier":"stretch","note":"Count the words that pass through each node; a word's score is the sum along its path."},{"slug":"stream-of-characters","tier":"stretch","note":"Insert the words reversed and walk back from the newest letter."}]}$json$::jsonb),
('two-pointers',
  'Two Pointers',
  'Two Pointers',
  array['The array is sorted (or you''re allowed to sort it) and you''re looking for a pair or triplet with a target sum, difference or product.', 'You compare or swap things from both ends: palindromes, reversing, squares of a sorted array.', 'You have to filter or compact an array in place with O(1) extra space: remove duplicates, move zeroes, keep only valid items.', 'The brute force tries every pair (i, j), and there''s an order that tells you which side to give up on.']::text[],
  'Put left at the start and right at the end of a sorted array, and look at the pair they point to. If the sum is too small, numbers[left] is too small even with the largest partner still available, so no answer uses it: move left forward. If the sum is too big, numbers[right] is too big even with the smallest partner: move right back. If it matches, you''re done.

The invariant is that any answer still lies between left and right. Each move removes one element together with every pair it could have formed, and sorted order is what proves none of those pairs worked. Without sorting, the same move would throw away pairs you never checked.

Every step moves one pointer one place, and they start n − 1 apart, so the loop runs at most n − 1 times. That''s O(n) after an O(n log n) sort, against O(n²) for checking every pair.

The second shape is two pointers going the same way. read scans every element, and write marks where the next kept element goes. Everything before write is the finished part of the answer. It''s how you filter in place in one pass. Fast and slow pointers on a linked list are the same idea, and the Linked List primer covers them.

It breaks when the order gives you no rule for which pointer to move. Unsorted input where you need the original indices is the usual case: use a hash map then.',
  'def two_sum(numbers: list[int], target: int) -> list[int]:
    left, right = 0, len(numbers) - 1
    while left < right:
        s = numbers[left] + numbers[right]
        if s == target:
            # the problem wants 1-based indices
            return [left + 1, right + 1]
        if s < target:
            # too small even with the largest partner,
            # so numbers[left] fits no answer: drop it
            left += 1
        else:
            # too big even with the smallest partner,
            # so numbers[right] fits no answer: drop it
            right -= 1
    return []
',
  'Time O(n) per scan; O(n log n) if you sort first; O(n²) for 3Sum · Space O(1) extra, apart from the sort and the output',
  array['Using left <= right for pairs, which lets an element pair with itself.', 'Running opposite-end pointers on an unsorted array. The move rule depends on sorted order.', 'Sorting when the problem wants original indices. Sorting loses them, so use a hash map or sort (value, index) pairs.', 'In 3Sum, not skipping duplicates, which reports the same triplet more than once. Skip at the first value and after each match.', 'Moving the taller wall in Container With Most Water. It can never help, and you can skip the real answer.', 'Returning 0-based indices when the problem asks for 1-based (Two Sum II).']::text[],
  array['reverse-string', 'valid-palindrome', 'merge-sorted-array']::text[],
  3,
  $json${"version":1,"summary":"Two indices move through the array in a planned way, either inward from both ends of sorted data or forward together as a reader and a writer. Each step rules out a whole row of candidates, so a search over pairs takes O(n) instead of O(n²).","prerequisites":["arrays-hashing"],"walkthrough":{"problemSlug":"two-sum-ii-input-array-is-sorted","input":"numbers = [1, 3, 4, 6, 8, 11], target = 10","intro":"one step per comparison.","columns":["Pointers","Check","Answer"],"steps":[{"window":"[1] 3 4 6 8 [11]","action":"1 + 11 = 12, too big: right − 1","best":"none"},{"window":"[1] 3 4 6 [8] 11","action":"1 + 8 = 9, too small: left + 1","best":"none"},{"window":"1 [3] 4 6 [8] 11","action":"3 + 8 = 11, too big: right − 1","best":"none"},{"window":"1 [3] 4 [6] 8 11","action":"3 + 6 = 9, too small: left + 1","best":"none"},{"window":"1 3 [4] [6] 8 11","action":"4 + 6 = 10, found","best":"[3, 4]"}],"result":"[3, 4] in 1-based indices. Five checks instead of the 15 pairs a nested loop would try."},"template":{"note":"Compare, then move the pointer whose element can't be part of any answer. The ladder changes what is compared and what \"too small\" means, not the shape.","code":{"python":"def two_sum(numbers: list[int], target: int) -> list[int]:\n    left, right = 0, len(numbers) - 1\n    while left < right:\n        s = numbers[left] + numbers[right]\n        if s == target:\n            # the problem wants 1-based indices\n            return [left + 1, right + 1]\n        if s < target:\n            # too small even with the largest partner,\n            # so numbers[left] fits no answer: drop it\n            left += 1\n        else:\n            # too big even with the smallest partner,\n            # so numbers[right] fits no answer: drop it\n            right -= 1\n    return []\n","cpp":"vector<int> twoSum(const vector<int>& numbers, int target) {\n    int left = 0, right = (int)numbers.size() - 1;\n    while (left < right) {\n        int s = numbers[left] + numbers[right];\n        if (s == target) {\n            // the problem wants 1-based indices\n            return {left + 1, right + 1};\n        }\n        if (s < target) {\n            // too small even with the largest partner,\n            // so numbers[left] fits no answer: drop it\n            ++left;\n        } else {\n            // too big even with the smallest partner,\n            // so numbers[right] fits no answer: drop it\n            --right;\n        }\n    }\n    return {};\n}\n","java":"class Solution {\n    public int[] twoSum(int[] numbers, int target) {\n        int left = 0, right = numbers.length - 1;\n        while (left < right) {\n            int s = numbers[left] + numbers[right];\n            if (s == target) {\n                // the problem wants 1-based indices\n                return new int[]{left + 1, right + 1};\n            }\n            if (s < target) {\n                // too small even with the largest partner,\n                // so numbers[left] fits no answer: drop it\n                left++;\n            } else {\n                // too big even with the smallest partner,\n                // so numbers[right] fits no answer: drop it\n                right--;\n            }\n        }\n        return new int[0];\n    }\n}\n"}},"variants":[{"name":"Read and write, same direction","when":"You must filter or compact an array in place.","idea":"read visits every element; write only moves when you keep one. Everything before write is the answer so far, and the function returns write as the new length.","code":{"python":"def remove_duplicates(nums: list[int]) -> int:\n    # nums[0..write-1] holds the values kept so far\n    write = 0\n    for read in range(len(nums)):\n        # sorted, so a new value differs from the last kept\n        if write == 0 or nums[read] != nums[write - 1]:\n            nums[write] = nums[read]\n            write += 1\n    return write\n","cpp":"int removeDuplicates(vector<int>& nums) {\n    // nums[0..write-1] holds the values kept so far\n    int write = 0;\n    for (int read = 0; read < (int)nums.size(); ++read) {\n        // sorted, so a new value differs from the last kept\n        if (write == 0 || nums[read] != nums[write - 1]) {\n            nums[write] = nums[read];\n            ++write;\n        }\n    }\n    return write;\n}\n","java":"class Solution {\n    public int removeDuplicates(int[] nums) {\n        // nums[0..write-1] holds the values kept so far\n        int write = 0;\n        for (int read = 0; read < nums.length; read++) {\n            // sorted, so a new value differs from the last kept\n            if (write == 0 || nums[read] != nums[write - 1]) {\n                nums[write] = nums[read];\n                write++;\n            }\n        }\n        return write;\n    }\n}\n"},"problemSlug":"remove-duplicates-from-sorted-array"},{"name":"Fix one, two-pointer the rest","when":"You need triplets (or quadruplets) with a target sum.","idea":"Sort. For each i, run the template on i + 1..n − 1 looking for −nums[i]. Skip a first value equal to the one before it, and after a match skip equal values on the left, so no triplet repeats.","code":{"python":"def three_sum(nums: list[int]) -> list[list[int]]:\n    nums.sort()\n    out: list[list[int]] = []\n    n = len(nums)\n    for i in range(n - 2):\n        # same first value again would repeat triplets\n        if i > 0 and nums[i] == nums[i - 1]:\n            continue\n        # two-sum on the rest, looking for -a\n        a = nums[i]\n        lo, hi = i + 1, n - 1\n        while lo < hi:\n            s = a + nums[lo] + nums[hi]\n            if s < 0:\n                lo += 1\n                continue\n            if s > 0:\n                hi -= 1\n                continue\n            out.append([a, nums[lo], nums[hi]])\n            lo += 1\n            hi -= 1\n            # skip copies of the value just used\n            while lo < hi and nums[lo] == nums[lo - 1]:\n                lo += 1\n    return out\n","cpp":"vector<vector<int>> threeSum(vector<int> nums) {\n    sort(nums.begin(), nums.end());\n    vector<vector<int>> out;\n    int n = nums.size();\n    for (int i = 0; i + 2 < n; ++i) {\n        // same first value again would repeat triplets\n        if (i > 0 && nums[i] == nums[i - 1]) continue;\n        // two-sum on the rest, looking for -a\n        int a = nums[i];\n        int lo = i + 1, hi = n - 1;\n        while (lo < hi) {\n            int s = a + nums[lo] + nums[hi];\n            if (s < 0) { ++lo; continue; }\n            if (s > 0) { --hi; continue; }\n            out.push_back({a, nums[lo], nums[hi]});\n            ++lo;\n            --hi;\n            // skip copies of the value just used\n            while (lo < hi && nums[lo] == nums[lo - 1]) {\n                ++lo;\n            }\n        }\n    }\n    return out;\n}\n","java":"class Solution {\n    public List<List<Integer>> threeSum(int[] nums) {\n        Arrays.sort(nums);\n        List<List<Integer>> out = new ArrayList<>();\n        int n = nums.length;\n        for (int i = 0; i + 2 < n; i++) {\n            // same first value again would repeat triplets\n            if (i > 0 && nums[i] == nums[i - 1]) continue;\n            // two-sum on the rest, looking for -a\n            int a = nums[i];\n            int lo = i + 1, hi = n - 1;\n            while (lo < hi) {\n                int s = a + nums[lo] + nums[hi];\n                if (s < 0) { lo++; continue; }\n                if (s > 0) { hi--; continue; }\n                out.add(List.of(a, nums[lo], nums[hi]));\n                lo++;\n                hi--;\n                // skip copies of the value just used\n                while (lo < hi && nums[lo] == nums[lo - 1]) {\n                    lo++;\n                }\n            }\n        }\n        return out;\n    }\n}\n"},"problemSlug":"3sum"},{"name":"Move the side that limits you","when":"You maximise something that depends on the shorter of two ends and the distance between them.","idea":"The area is min(height) × width. Pairing the shorter wall with anything closer can only lose width without gaining height, so it's finished: move it inward.","code":{"python":"def max_area(height: list[int]) -> int:\n    left, right = 0, len(height) - 1\n    best = 0\n    while left < right:\n        h = min(height[left], height[right])\n        best = max(best, h * (right - left))\n        # the shorter wall can't do better with any closer\n        # partner, so it's done: move it inward\n        if height[left] < height[right]:\n            left += 1\n        else:\n            right -= 1\n    return best\n","cpp":"int maxArea(const vector<int>& height) {\n    int left = 0, right = (int)height.size() - 1;\n    int best = 0;\n    while (left < right) {\n        int h = min(height[left], height[right]);\n        best = max(best, h * (right - left));\n        // the shorter wall can't do better with any closer\n        // partner, so it's done: move it inward\n        if (height[left] < height[right]) {\n            ++left;\n        } else {\n            --right;\n        }\n    }\n    return best;\n}\n","java":"class Solution {\n    public int maxArea(int[] height) {\n        int left = 0, right = height.length - 1;\n        int best = 0;\n        while (left < right) {\n            int h = Math.min(height[left], height[right]);\n            best = Math.max(best, h * (right - left));\n            // the shorter wall can't do better with any closer\n            // partner, so it's done: move it inward\n            if (height[left] < height[right]) {\n                left++;\n            } else {\n                right--;\n            }\n        }\n        return best;\n    }\n}\n"},"problemSlug":"container-with-most-water"}],"complexity":{"time":"O(n) per scan; O(n log n) if you sort first; O(n²) for 3Sum","space":"O(1) extra, apart from the sort and the output","why":"The two pointers start n − 1 apart and every step closes the gap by one, so a scan is at most n steps. 3Sum runs one O(n) scan for each of n first values."},"notThis":["Unsorted input and you need the original indices of a pair: a hash map of value → index (Arrays & Hashing).","The answer is a contiguous stretch that grows and shrinks, like the longest substring without repeats: that's a sliding window.","You're searching for one value or boundary in sorted data, not a pair: binary search is O(log n)."],"ladder":[{"slug":"reverse-string","tier":"warm-up","note":"Swap from both ends until the pointers meet."},{"slug":"valid-palindrome","tier":"warm-up","note":"Compare from both ends, skipping characters that don't count."},{"slug":"merge-sorted-array","tier":"warm-up","note":"Three pointers, filling from the back so nothing gets overwritten."},{"slug":"two-sum-ii-input-array-is-sorted","tier":"core","note":"The template: too small, move left; too big, move right."},{"slug":"remove-duplicates-from-sorted-array","tier":"core","note":"Read and write pointers moving the same way."},{"slug":"move-zeroes","tier":"core","note":"Read and write again, keeping the order of what stays."},{"slug":"squares-of-a-sorted-array","tier":"core","note":"The biggest square is at one of the two ends: fill the output from the back."},{"slug":"3sum","tier":"core","note":"Fix one, two-pointer the rest, skip duplicates."},{"slug":"container-with-most-water","tier":"core","note":"Not a sum: move the shorter wall, and say why that's safe."},{"slug":"sort-colors","tier":"core","note":"Three pointers split the array into 0s, 1s and 2s in one pass."},{"slug":"boats-to-save-people","tier":"core","note":"Greedy pairing: the heaviest person takes the lightest one if they fit."},{"slug":"4sum","tier":"stretch","note":"Two fixed indices plus the template, and sums that overflow 32 bits."},{"slug":"trapping-rain-water","tier":"stretch","note":"Move the side with the lower wall, keeping the best height seen on each side."}]}$json$::jsonb),
('union-find',
  'Union-Find',
  'Union-Find',
  array['Edges or "a is linked to b" facts come one by one, and you need to know if two items are already connected.', 'You want the number of connected groups, or the size of each, without running a full search.', 'Adding an edge between two already-connected nodes is the event that matters: it closes a cycle.', 'Equal-to facts first, then not-equal checks: a == b means same group.', 'Kruskal''s minimum spanning tree: take the cheapest edges that join two different groups.']::text[],
  'Store the groups as a forest. parent[x] points to another member of x''s group, and the root, the one node whose parent is itself, names the group. find(x) follows parent pointers up to the root. Two items are in the same group exactly when they have the same root. To merge two groups, find both roots and make one root point at the other.

Done naively, the trees can become long chains and find costs O(n). Two small rules fix this. Union by size hangs the smaller tree under the root of the bigger one, so a node only gets deeper when its group at least doubles, and no tree is taller than log n. Path compression goes further: while find walks up to the root, it points every node it passes straight at the root, so the next find from any of them takes one step.

With both rules, a sequence of m operations costs O(m α(n)). α is the inverse Ackermann function, which is at most 4 for any input that fits in a computer, so treat each operation as constant time.

Have unite return whether it actually merged anything. False means the two items were already connected, which is how you spot a redundant edge, and counting the true results tells you how many groups remain.

Union-Find only merges. It can''t split a group or remove an edge, and it doesn''t give you a path between two nodes. For those, search the graph instead.',
  'class DSU:
    def __init__(self, n: int) -> None:
        # each node starts as the root of its own group
        self.parent = list(range(n))
        self.size = [1] * n

    def find(self, x: int) -> int:
        # path compression: point x straight at its root
        if self.parent[x] != x:
            self.parent[x] = self.find(self.parent[x])
        return self.parent[x]

    # False if a and b were already in one group
    def unite(self, a: int, b: int) -> bool:
        ra, rb = self.find(a), self.find(b)
        if ra == rb:
            return False
        # union by size: the smaller group goes under
        if self.size[ra] < self.size[rb]:
            ra, rb = rb, ra
        self.parent[rb] = ra
        self.size[ra] += self.size[rb]
        return True


def find_redundant_connection(
        edges: list[list[int]]) -> list[int]:
    # nodes are 1..n and there are n edges
    dsu = DSU(len(edges) + 1)
    for a, b in edges:
        # already connected: this edge closes a cycle
        if not dsu.unite(a, b):
            return [a, b]
    return []
',
  'Time O(α(n)) per find or unite, amortized: effectively constant · Space O(n) for parent and size',
  array['Comparing parent[a] == parent[b] instead of find(a) == find(b). Two nodes can share a root without sharing a parent.', 'In unite, setting parent[a] = b instead of parent[root of a] = root of b. That moves one node and leaves the rest of its group behind.', 'Skipping both union by size and path compression. Then a chain forms and every find is O(n).', 'Making the parent array size n when the nodes are numbered 1..n. Allocate n + 1.', 'Counting groups by counting distinct parent values. Count the roots (parent[x] == x), or keep a counter that unite decreases.', 'Checking "!=" facts before all the "==" facts have been united.', 'Reaching for Union-Find when the question needs the path itself or the distance. It only knows the groups.']::text[],
  array['number-of-provinces', 'redundant-connection', 'number-of-operations-to-make-network-connected']::text[],
  18,
  $json${"version":1,"summary":"Keeps items in groups and answers two questions fast: which group is x in, and merge the groups of a and b. Use it when connections arrive one at a time and you keep asking whether two things are already connected.","prerequisites":["graphs-bfs"],"walkthrough":{"problemSlug":"redundant-connection","input":"edges = [[1,2], [3,4], [2,4], [5,6], [4,6], [1,5]]","intro":"one step per edge. Each group is written with its root first.","columns":["Groups","What happened","Answer"],"steps":[{"window":"{1,2} {3} {4} {5} {6}","action":"[1,2]: roots 1 and 2, both size 1; 2 goes under 1","best":"none yet"},{"window":"{1,2} {3,4} {5} {6}","action":"[3,4]: different roots; 4 goes under 3","best":"none yet"},{"window":"{1,2,3,4} {5} {6}","action":"[2,4]: roots 1 and 3, both size 2; root 3 goes under 1","best":"none yet"},{"window":"{1,2,3,4} {5,6}","action":"[5,6]: different roots; 6 goes under 5","best":"none yet"},{"window":"{1,2,3,4,5,6}","action":"[4,6]: find(4) walks 4 → 3 → 1 and repoints 4 straight at 1; root 5 (size 2) goes under 1 (size 4)","best":"none yet"},{"window":"{1,2,3,4,5,6}","action":"[1,5]: find(1) = 1 and find(5) = 1, the same root; unite returns false","best":"[1,5]"}],"result":"[1,5]. Nodes 1 and 5 were already joined through 2, 4 and 6, so this edge closes the cycle 1 → 2 → 4 → 6 → 5 → 1."},"template":{"note":"find with path compression, unite with union by size that returns false when the roots already match. Most Union-Find problems are this class plus a short loop that calls unite and reacts to its answer.","code":{"python":"class DSU:\n    def __init__(self, n: int) -> None:\n        # each node starts as the root of its own group\n        self.parent = list(range(n))\n        self.size = [1] * n\n\n    def find(self, x: int) -> int:\n        # path compression: point x straight at its root\n        if self.parent[x] != x:\n            self.parent[x] = self.find(self.parent[x])\n        return self.parent[x]\n\n    # False if a and b were already in one group\n    def unite(self, a: int, b: int) -> bool:\n        ra, rb = self.find(a), self.find(b)\n        if ra == rb:\n            return False\n        # union by size: the smaller group goes under\n        if self.size[ra] < self.size[rb]:\n            ra, rb = rb, ra\n        self.parent[rb] = ra\n        self.size[ra] += self.size[rb]\n        return True\n\n\ndef find_redundant_connection(\n        edges: list[list[int]]) -> list[int]:\n    # nodes are 1..n and there are n edges\n    dsu = DSU(len(edges) + 1)\n    for a, b in edges:\n        # already connected: this edge closes a cycle\n        if not dsu.unite(a, b):\n            return [a, b]\n    return []\n","cpp":"struct DSU {\n    vector<int> parent, size;\n\n    DSU(int n) : parent(n), size(n, 1) {\n        // each node starts as the root of its own group\n        iota(parent.begin(), parent.end(), 0);\n    }\n\n    int find(int x) {\n        // path compression: point x straight at its root\n        if (parent[x] != x) parent[x] = find(parent[x]);\n        return parent[x];\n    }\n\n    // false if a and b were already in one group\n    bool unite(int a, int b) {\n        int ra = find(a), rb = find(b);\n        if (ra == rb) return false;\n        // union by size: the smaller group goes under\n        if (size[ra] < size[rb]) swap(ra, rb);\n        parent[rb] = ra;\n        size[ra] += size[rb];\n        return true;\n    }\n};\n\nvector<int> findRedundantConnection(\n        vector<vector<int>>& edges) {\n    // nodes are 1..n and there are n edges\n    DSU dsu(edges.size() + 1);\n    for (auto& e : edges) {\n        // already connected: this edge closes a cycle\n        if (!dsu.unite(e[0], e[1])) return {e[0], e[1]};\n    }\n    return {};\n}\n","java":"class DSU {\n    int[] parent, size;\n\n    DSU(int n) {\n        parent = new int[n];\n        size = new int[n];\n        // each node starts as the root of its own group\n        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {\n            parent[i] = i;\n            size[i] = 1;\n        }\n    }\n\n    int find(int x) {\n        // path compression: point x straight at its root\n        if (parent[x] != x) parent[x] = find(parent[x]);\n        return parent[x];\n    }\n\n    // false if a and b were already in one group\n    boolean unite(int a, int b) {\n        int ra = find(a), rb = find(b);\n        if (ra == rb) return false;\n        // union by size: the smaller group goes under\n        if (size[ra] < size[rb]) {\n            int t = ra;\n            ra = rb;\n            rb = t;\n        }\n        parent[rb] = ra;\n        size[ra] += size[rb];\n        return true;\n    }\n}\n\nclass Solution {\n    public int[] findRedundantConnection(int[][] edges) {\n        // nodes are 1..n and there are n edges\n        DSU dsu = new DSU(edges.length + 1);\n        for (int[] e : edges) {\n            // already connected: this edge closes a cycle\n            if (!dsu.unite(e[0], e[1])) return e;\n        }\n        return new int[0];\n    }\n}\n"}},"variants":[{"name":"Count the groups","when":"The answer depends on how many separate groups there are.","idea":"Start with n groups and subtract one each time unite returns true. Joining k groups takes k − 1 edges. Check the cheap impossible case first: fewer than n − 1 edges can never connect n nodes.","code":{"python":"def make_connected(n: int, connections: list[list[int]]) -> int:\n    # joining n computers needs at least n - 1 cables\n    if len(connections) < n - 1:\n        return -1\n    dsu = DSU(n)\n    groups = n\n    for a, b in connections:\n        # each real merge turns two groups into one\n        if dsu.unite(a, b):\n            groups -= 1\n    # one moved cable joins two groups\n    return groups - 1\n","cpp":"int makeConnected(int n, vector<vector<int>>& connections) {\n    // joining n computers needs at least n - 1 cables\n    if ((int)connections.size() < n - 1) return -1;\n    DSU dsu(n);\n    int groups = n;\n    for (auto& c : connections) {\n        // each real merge turns two groups into one\n        if (dsu.unite(c[0], c[1])) groups--;\n    }\n    // one moved cable joins two groups\n    return groups - 1;\n}\n","java":"class Solution {\n    public int makeConnected(int n, int[][] connections) {\n        // joining n computers needs at least n - 1 cables\n        if (connections.length < n - 1) return -1;\n        DSU dsu = new DSU(n);\n        int groups = n;\n        for (int[] c : connections) {\n            // each real merge turns two groups into one\n            if (dsu.unite(c[0], c[1])) groups--;\n        }\n        // one moved cable joins two groups\n        return groups - 1;\n    }\n}\n"},"problemSlug":"number-of-operations-to-make-network-connected"},{"name":"Union first, then check","when":"Some facts join items and others must keep them apart.","idea":"Make two passes. First unite every \"==\" pair, so the groups are final. Then check each \"!=\" pair and fail if both sides have the same root. Checking during the first pass misses an equality that arrives later.","code":{"python":"def equations_possible(equations: list[str]) -> bool:\n    dsu = DSU(26)\n    # pass 1: join every pair that must be equal\n    for e in equations:\n        if e[1] == \"=\":\n            x = ord(e[0]) - ord(\"a\")\n            y = ord(e[3]) - ord(\"a\")\n            dsu.unite(x, y)\n    # pass 2: no \"!=\" may sit inside one group\n    for e in equations:\n        if e[1] == \"!\":\n            x = ord(e[0]) - ord(\"a\")\n            y = ord(e[3]) - ord(\"a\")\n            if dsu.find(x) == dsu.find(y):\n                return False\n    return True\n","cpp":"bool equationsPossible(vector<string>& equations) {\n    DSU dsu(26);\n    // pass 1: join every pair that must be equal\n    for (const string& e : equations) {\n        if (e[1] == '=') {\n            int x = e[0] - 'a';\n            int y = e[3] - 'a';\n            dsu.unite(x, y);\n        }\n    }\n    // pass 2: no \"!=\" may sit inside one group\n    for (const string& e : equations) {\n        if (e[1] == '!') {\n            int x = e[0] - 'a';\n            int y = e[3] - 'a';\n            if (dsu.find(x) == dsu.find(y)) return false;\n        }\n    }\n    return true;\n}\n","java":"class Solution {\n    public boolean equationsPossible(String[] equations) {\n        DSU dsu = new DSU(26);\n        // pass 1: join every pair that must be equal\n        for (String e : equations) {\n            if (e.charAt(1) == '=') {\n                int x = e.charAt(0) - 'a';\n                int y = e.charAt(3) - 'a';\n                dsu.unite(x, y);\n            }\n        }\n        // pass 2: no \"!=\" may sit inside one group\n        for (String e : equations) {\n            if (e.charAt(1) == '!') {\n                int x = e.charAt(0) - 'a';\n                int y = e.charAt(3) - 'a';\n                if (dsu.find(x) == dsu.find(y)) return false;\n            }\n        }\n        return true;\n    }\n}\n"},"problemSlug":"satisfiability-of-equality-equations"},{"name":"Sort the edges, then unite (Kruskal)","when":"Connect everything at the lowest total cost.","idea":"List every edge with its cost and sort from cheapest. Take an edge only if unite returns true, because an edge inside one group would form a cycle. Stop after n − 1 edges.","code":{"python":"def min_cost_connect_points(points: list[list[int]]) -> int:\n    n = len(points)\n    edges: list[tuple[int, int, int]] = []\n    for i in range(n):\n        for j in range(i + 1, n):\n            d = (abs(points[i][0] - points[j][0])\n                 + abs(points[i][1] - points[j][1]))\n            edges.append((d, i, j))\n    # cheapest edges first\n    edges.sort()\n    dsu = DSU(n)\n    total, used = 0, 0\n    for d, i, j in edges:\n        # an edge inside one group would make a cycle\n        if dsu.unite(i, j):\n            total += d\n            used += 1\n            # a tree on n points has n - 1 edges\n            if used == n - 1:\n                break\n    return total\n","cpp":"int minCostConnectPoints(vector<vector<int>>& points) {\n    int n = points.size();\n    vector<array<int, 3>> edges;\n    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {\n        for (int j = i + 1; j < n; j++) {\n            int d = abs(points[i][0] - points[j][0])\n                  + abs(points[i][1] - points[j][1]);\n            edges.push_back({d, i, j});\n        }\n    }\n    // cheapest edges first\n    sort(edges.begin(), edges.end());\n    DSU dsu(n);\n    int total = 0, used = 0;\n    for (auto& [d, i, j] : edges) {\n        // an edge inside one group would make a cycle\n        if (dsu.unite(i, j)) {\n            total += d;\n            used++;\n            // a tree on n points has n - 1 edges\n            if (used == n - 1) break;\n        }\n    }\n    return total;\n}\n","java":"class Solution {\n    public int minCostConnectPoints(int[][] points) {\n        int n = points.length;\n        List<int[]> edges = new ArrayList<>();\n        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {\n            for (int j = i + 1; j < n; j++) {\n                int d = Math.abs(points[i][0] - points[j][0])\n                      + Math.abs(points[i][1] - points[j][1]);\n                edges.add(new int[] {d, i, j});\n            }\n        }\n        // cheapest edges first\n        edges.sort((a, b) -> Integer.compare(a[0], b[0]));\n        DSU dsu = new DSU(n);\n        int total = 0, used = 0;\n        for (int[] e : edges) {\n            // an edge inside one group would make a cycle\n            if (dsu.unite(e[1], e[2])) {\n                total += e[0];\n                used++;\n                // a tree on n points has n - 1 edges\n                if (used == n - 1) break;\n            }\n        }\n        return total;\n    }\n}\n"},"problemSlug":"min-cost-to-connect-all-points"}],"complexity":{"time":"O(α(n)) per find or unite, amortized: effectively constant","space":"O(n) for parent and size","why":"Union by size keeps every tree at most log n tall, and path compression flattens each path it walks, so later finds are short. Together the cost per operation is the inverse Ackermann function, which never goes above 4 in practice. Kruskal adds O(E log E) for the sort."},"notThis":["You need the shortest number of steps between two nodes: use BFS. See Graph BFS / DFS.","The graph is fixed and you only count components once: one DFS or BFS pass is just as fast and shorter to write. See Graph BFS / DFS.","Edges are directed and you need an order or a cycle check on them: Union-Find ignores direction. See Topological Sort.","Edges get removed over time: Union-Find can't split groups. Process the removals backwards as additions, or search again."],"ladder":[{"slug":"number-of-provinces","tier":"warm-up","note":"Unite every connected pair and count what's left."},{"slug":"redundant-connection","tier":"warm-up","note":"The template: the first edge whose unite returns false."},{"slug":"number-of-operations-to-make-network-connected","tier":"core","note":"Count groups; k groups need k − 1 moved cables."},{"slug":"satisfiability-of-equality-equations","tier":"core","note":"Unite all \"==\" first, then check every \"!=\"."},{"slug":"accounts-merge","tier":"core","note":"Map each email to an id, unite emails in the same account, then collect each root's emails."},{"slug":"most-stones-removed-with-same-row-or-column","tier":"core","note":"Unite stones that share a row or column; the answer is stones minus groups."},{"slug":"smallest-string-with-swaps","tier":"core","note":"Swappable positions form groups; sort the letters inside each group."},{"slug":"min-cost-to-connect-all-points","tier":"core","note":"Kruskal: sort the edges, keep each one that joins two groups."},{"slug":"swim-in-rising-water","tier":"stretch","note":"Open cells in order of height and stop when the corners share a root."},{"slug":"remove-max-number-of-edges-to-keep-graph-fully-traversable","tier":"stretch","note":"Two DSUs, one per person; shared edges go into both first."},{"slug":"checking-existence-of-edge-length-limited-paths","tier":"stretch","note":"Sort queries and edges by limit and add edges as the limit grows."}]}$json$::jsonb)
